      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0006" n="art0006">
        <head>TIT-BITS.</head>
        <p>TIT-BITS.</p>
        <p>THE TWO CARTERS.</p>
        <p>T.ev. Samuel Carter was an easy-going, open. I ted, but terribly lazy preacher, who had been many years settled over the old Free-Will i,aptist 6ociety, at Buttermilk Hollow—a certain subdivision of the town of Eaton, N. H. One day, in early spring, our good clergyman was on the road in his old yellow gig, when he, came across a poor carter, whose heavy tart, with a load of oak wood, had got stuck in the 'deep mud. The poor man was punching away at his oxen with his breaded gourd.stick, when the parson came up. Now the carter's oxen were far from being over- fed. In fact, the sharp lines of their ribs would seem to indicate that they lacked food. The parson saw this, and thought to administer a gentle reminder.</p>
        <p>"My good ftiend, you seem to be in trouble," he said, as he reined up alongside the c4rt. "1 should say 1 was. I'm stuck here in the mud, like death to a dead boss and I don't see how I'm gobs' to get out." "My good Mau.,—your oxen are too weak for such a load. Ah ! You should be more careful of them. A humane man is humane to his beast."</p>
        <p>"No ! You don't hit the ease, mister. 'Taint the oxen that's in the fault. The trouble aint wi' them."</p>
        <p>" Ah,—where is the trouble, may I ask?" " Wily," returned the man of the cal t, looking up as though he did not recognise the clergyman, his bleared eyes blinking sharkly the while, "them poor oxen is like the .bee-NVill Baptist Society over in Buttermilk Ildller,—thcy t.,37a a better Carter!" After which the minister rode on, meditatively.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0007" n="art0007">
        <head>YOUTHFUL PRODIGY.</head>
        <p>A YOUTHFUL PRODIGY.</p>
        <p>Adolphus Timpkins is a joker,—a man fond ot good things, who dearly loves to tell a good story, and also to hear one. Furthermore, limpkins is great on conundrums. Hardly a day passes that he does not bewilder his fellow clerks and sales- men with a propoundment of that ilk, either original or stolen; and he is supremely happy when he has become possessed of something new in the conundrum line with which to pose his friends.</p>
        <p>Timpkins s oldest boy is a bright, pert, original genius of eight years, or thereabouts, named Lyeurvs ; and a few evenings since Lyenrgus brought down the house. It happened thus:</p>
        <p>It will be readily understood that the boy, having heard so much of that sort of intellectual puzzling from his father, had come to appreciate a good thing when he heard it, and we shall not wonder that he was ambitious to make a hit en his own account. Well,—on the evening in ques- tion the elder Timpkins had a party at his house— an assembly of choice spirits—and as the evening wore on, and one bright and witty thing after another was thrown off, gushing hilarity ruled the hour. By and by, when there had come a lull in the conversation, up spoke the boy, from his oorner behind the stove:</p>
        <p>. _ . "Papa, I want to ask you a conundrum." "You IP—A conundrum 'exclaimed Pattrfamil- ia4, in astonishment. And others were astonished, beside the father.</p>
        <p>" Yes, sir 1" returned the boy, unblushingly. "And it's one of my own gettin' up too 1" " Well,—go ahead. Let's have it." "Then, sir, why will these men here visiting be like Bunker lull Monument when they've got fairly started on their way homeward !" "Why—will—But, really, 'Curg., are you seri- -0115 ? Is it a real conundrum I" "Of course it is. Yes, sir 1" An then they went to work. The chill March wind howled out of doors, and whistled around the corners, but not a sound within—not a sound, save the occasional dropping of a finger upon the table as the men studied. At length ene of the visitors suggested: "Because we'll be looking up at the sky." "No ! That ain't it. Banker Hill Monument •t look I" • can .</p>
        <p>Adolphus Timpkins looked upon this boy with increasing wonder and a waking admiration; but he could not nolve the problem. It was too deep fur him. Said he:</p>
        <p>"I can't guess it, Lyeurp,us, "1.): yTi give it up ?"</p>
        <p>" Yel"</p>
        <p>_ " D' you all give it up?" Yes,—they all gave it up; and then said the father:</p>
        <p>"'Now tell us my boy,—Why will my guests, .when they have got fairly started on their way homeward, be like Bunker Hill Monument? I .don't believe you can answer it yourself." " Yes, I can," shouted the boy, at the mine time leaving his seat, and reaching for the knob Of the door 6.:hind him ; "They'll be like Bunker Hill Monument—&amp;caule they'll be obt in the cold, cold night ?" As the last word left his Ups the little wretch made for the door, and was out of siEht before the outraged sire could meet him. U.</p>
        <p>A GENTLEMAN recently opened a letter addressed to his son, containing suggestions from a friend to the lattee for a novel which be (the son) was privately writing. The father Was exceedingly -stirprised and frightened upon relding the follow- ing dreadful words :—" Dear Bob,—You really must show more caution in constructing your plots, or the governor will be sure to discriver the .dead body of Geraldine in the cellar, and then :your secret will be out. You consulted me about the strychnine. 1 cei tainly think you are giving it him in rather large doses. Lce Emily put her mother in a madhouse. It will answer your pur- pose well to have the old girl out of the way. I think your forgery is for too small a ma. Make •it three thousand. Leave the rest of your particularly nice family circle to Inc. I will finish them off, and send back the 'fatal dagger' after. wards by book post.—Yours, "Jack."</p>
        <p>Coto:m. Citsrmas SPENCER, American cowl- -senor-at-law.' some years ago had to defend one Marshall, charged with larceny, against whom -there was very strong evidence. Before the trial, spencer went to his client, and told him that his only chance was the plea of insanity, and advised him to play the lunatic, and to answer all questions pit to him with the word "spoons." The day of the trial came and Marshal took his place in the dock, pale, haggard, and "Guilty or not guilty asked the clerk. "Spoons ! drawled the prisoner, ith a blank stare. " Come —plead guilty or not guilty," the clerk responded. "Spoons !" was the reply. "Prisoner, will you answer the question put to you ?" "Spoons !" he ,bewled. At this point the counsel for the prisoner interfered, anti told the court the prisoner was in- sane, and not responsible for his actions, &amp;c. "Do you understand what is said ?" asked the judge of the prisoner. "Spoons !" was the reply. The judge discharged him, as he was evidentlyinsane. spencer eengtatulated him upon his escape, and suggesteil it would be a good idea to pay him. His client stared,' and move% away with the simple remark, "Simons !" _ . .</p>
        <p>Tux advice of azeiebrated florist, when trying Ito keep flowers over night, is to put them in a 'trunk, which the trays and lid make alinoat var- 4.</p>
        <p>rrw days agn Mr. B. was walking anwri Rt. -,l,mes's Stwet, and on ineetin4 the witty Mr. H., ..ers-sset deep regret at the rev••rity of the complaining that it made him hohble. 4•0..! :" says Mr, H., " ii it hilly continues a little r, you will jump from wiuterto summer with- o ing." We ALT. 'viva ova. Trousces.—Sister Mary: Why, Charley, dear boy, what's the matter? You seem quite inisertOle! Chat-Thy: Ah! ain't I jogt T Here's ma says I must wear turn-down e4ll.trs till Christmas, and there's young Sidney Howler, Itho' s not half so tall as I am, has had stiok-une and white chokers for ever so long.</p>
        <p>"Ox, dear!" exclaimed Mrs. F., "where in the world did I put that reel of silk? I was very Care. fl when I put it away to have it fixed in my mind where I placed is." "Had it fixed in your mind, did you?" replied Fogg ; "but neverLhelue you now ,”terri to LC: mixed in soar rind."</p>
