      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0008" n="art0008">
        <head>LADIES' LETTER.</head>
        <p>LADIES' LETTER.</p>
        <p>Mr DEAREST NELLIE,</p>
        <p>Novel capes for the spring are cut just long enough to meet the waist. Some have a round-cut yoke to which a full flounce is gathered; others are composed of three graduated flounces, set full one over the other. The capes for winter wear were mostly made of coloured cloth ; black is being eliefly used now, either in corduroy or diagonal Coth or velvet. Pipings of tartan velvet at the t lges of short capes are used. Such mantles as these are only suitable for young ladies and natrons. Older women should adopt in preference three-quarter capes, which can be composed' of handsome materials, lined with silk and fitted with jet and other trimminos. The combinations of colour that are such a special featly() in the present fashions are, I notice, being carried out in all kinds of bonnet trimmings, such as ribbons, feathers, flowers, and jewelled orna- ments, of which great use is being made. Some of the newest straw hats are flecked with gold, while others are plaited with a very fine gold cord. Others, again, have interwoven coloured metallic threads, and these glint in the sunshine like pre- cious stones., Giant cornflowers, huge buttercups, Japanese iris, and yellow orchids appear the favourite flowers for our sumther millinery, together with posies of periwinkle blossom and reproductions of the brilliantly beautiful Dutch tulips to a pm;=sible size for our bead .'ecoration.</p>
        <p>A very cliarin!og hat, has a Tuscan crown, and the brim lined with black chip hemmed with Tus- can. This is trimmed on the crown with.dainty bows of lace clasped by a jet comb, while a bandeau of pink roses is arranged to rest on theliair, and, two black ostrich feathers trail gracefully over the sack.</p>
        <p>Flowers are much used- this season, besides coloured ribbons in fancy design, for the purpose of trimming medium and large-sized hats, bonnets, and toques, all studded with jewel-studded braids, the crowns in many instances being entirely com- posed of tinsel. They are trimmed with coloured velvet bows, and small tips or osprey. String's to tie under the chin are sometimes added to bon- nets, but they are as often 'worn without them. Shot velvet is more used for millinery than the plain material, .and it certainly is very effective when the light shines upon it. Roses and all tho summer flowers are being worn on hats and bonnets now.</p>
        <p>A very handsome bodice is of (kap bengaline silk, wit Ii a deep flounce of its own fabric, edged with black lace applique, and this can be made in all newest colouring of material h.' a reasonable price. New tweeds for the present season are woven in the lightest of neutral tints, and in very small broken checks. These make up well into plain or tailor costumes, and can be trimmed with either a smooth or a coarse woollen braid. Shot materials, such as alpaca and thin woollen stuffs, sometimes plain, and again with fine stripes and the smallest of spots, form smarter costumes, while the addi• tion of silk Or velvet trimmings at once converts a simple frock-into one which can be worn on dressy occasions.</p>
        <p>I have seen several black dresses worn recently with tight-fitting waistcoats of white satin. This is so becoming and so particularly " dressy "- looking that I feel sure it is an idea which you may like to copy. A well-fitting waistcoat of this kind will make the plainest black dress look smart, and need cost but very little if made at home. These waistcoats are arranged with a tight-fitting back of lining. The entire collar must be of the satin, and a pretty fashion in which to finish the front is with a box-pleat down the centre, fasten- ing with small mother-o'-pearl stad, or an in- visible hook-and-eye fastening, finished with a satin frill.</p>
        <p>One of the most tasty dresses I have seen this season was a lovely gown of shot surah, which was the handiwork of Mine. Berthe, of 14, Kensington High-street. The bands on the skirt and those which cross the bodice diagonally, back and front, were of black guipure. Folds of silk formed collar and waistband, which were in each, case finished with a rosette rt the back. Nothing makes a room look so slsabby as stained ill-kept furni,ure. The expenditure of a little time and a few pence for the proper cleansing materials ure repaid a hundred times over by the bright spick anti span appearance of the tables, sideboards, and so forth. Oiled walnut furnitnre can be made to look as freslx as when new if rubbed over with pure paraffin or olive oil with a soft woollen rag, and then polished with a bit of clean dry flannel. Ikla3logaisy furniture is best washed with waim soap and water, and then wiped over with a soft einth dipped in beeswax an.l sweet oil, and filially polished with a bit of chamois leather. The secret of keeping furniture clean and bright looking ki to remove thew stains as soon as possible and to make a point of giving itu thorough cleansing and polishing once a week. Yours sincerely, NELLIE.</p>
        <p>Epitaph on a Maid of all Work. The following may interest the readers of the " Ladies' Corner" who did not peruse it when printed in the columns some years ago : Here lies a poor woman who always was tired, For she lived in a place where heir) wasn't hired. Her last words on earth were :—Dear friends, I am going Where washing ain't done, nor sweeping, nor sewing, And everything there is exact to my wishes. For vibe' e they don't eat there's no washing of dishes. I'll be where loud anthems will ever be ringing, But having no voice, get clear of the singing. Don't weep for me now, don't weep for nne never, For I'm going to do nothing for ever and ever.</p>
