      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0009" n="art0009">
        <head>EXAMINER. SAT URDA Y„JULY 1,1 1893. DARKWOOD.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER. SAT URDA Y„JULY 1,1 1893. DARKWOOD.</p>
        <p>' Waste</p>
        <p>We waste mar coal and our smoke, our gas and our water, oar laid and our refuse. What we want is more forethought tis times of comparative prosperity. The chemists turn scrap ken into tin, old Wales into lucifer matches, the shavings ol the blacksmith's shop into Prussian blue, fusel oil into oil of apples and pears, the drainings of cow-houses into fashionable perfumery, beggars' rags into new pilot coats, cesspool filth into ammonia, and tar waste into aniline dyes and saccharine. In Paris they first utilise rats to clear the flesh from the bones of carcases, then kill the rats, use up the fur for trimmings, their skins for gloves, their thigh bones for tooth picks, and their tendons and bones for gelatine wrappers.</p>
        <p>"There" was a misunderstanding between us," said the viscount, gravely, "and one which, i could see her, I could soon clear up. She believes me fickle, inconstant, unworthy. If I could only find her., I could explain all." "Ana you cannot find her 1" . . . . .</p>
        <p>"I have searched the kingdom for her, but in vain. I have even called on my most mysterious cousin, Ronald Allerton who has an estate close here, as he told me he had seen someone resembling my description of my lost love. But I could make nothing out of him. • I have visited the Continent, and found no trace of her. She has completely disappeared 'I" "Bit her friends; do you not know her friends?' "She has no friends !"</p>
        <p>Miss Norreys looked amazed. "Are you trying to puzzle me, Lord Chilton?" she asked. "Do you love a girl without home, or friends or position, who has no abiding place, and who, having left her former place of residence, is lost to you" The viscount-bowed a grave assent. "She is all that you have said," he declared, "without home, friends, or, position. Wherever she is in the wide world to-day, she is earning her own living, or-starving in her pride and desolation. But poor as she is, Miss Norreys, she is greater to me than the queen upon her throne; she is to me the one woman in all the world, peerless in her beauty, in her innocence, in her grace and sweet ness. I lo've her with all my heart and soul. If she lives, I will find her. If she Is dead, then I have nothing henceforth to live for. Life without her would be a torture !"</p>
        <p>He spoke with a passionate energy that thrilled Miss Norreys's soul How strange his passion seemed to her ! Had she ever felt like that for any human being? Ah, yes, but so long ago that ages seemed to have passed since then! The fires of pas- sion seemed to have died out centuries since—to count by feeling rather than by years—leaving only dead ashes in their stead. But she kindled at his words and looks, as only a great sympathetic soul can kindle, and she exclammed— " It seems singular, Lord Chilton, that a girl g,6 disappear like this and not be found. You say tha foli have searched AT. hcF, How could she have eluded your search? There seems sonia great mystery about her. Is she an actress?" " She ? No nun in convent cloister was ever more unspotted from the world !" cried the lover. " She_was born and bred-in a wild, secluded region, and spent her girlish years in a strict Paris pen- sionnat. And yet there is, as you surmised, a great Mystery about her. lam -tempted to tell you all - the story, Miss Narreys. My heart aches with this long repression of grief. Possibly- you might help me to find her," he added desperately. "We have all failed—perhaps a woman's wit may let in a ray of light upon this impenetrable gloom." • "Perhaps so," said Miss Norreys. "What is her name, Lord Chilton ?" " Gwendoline Winter."</p>
        <p>" Of the Winters of Staffordshire?"'</p>
        <p>"Of no family whatever !" cried Lord Chilton. "Pardon me, Miss Norreys, but my pure little Gwen is nameless. She, a white lily, stainless and pure as one of heaven's angels, bloomed from a foul soil. They named her Owendoline because, even in her babyhood, she was an aristocrat, and they fancied she should have a fine-sounding. name. They called F.er Winter because, poor child, she came into the world in wintry weather, and found for her that it was to be a wintry world. Her life has been nearly all winter." "She had no name of her own? "None whatever."</p>
        <p>"They never knew her father's name r "They never knew that she had a father. It is a pitiful tale, Miss Norreys. They did not know her mother's name, and that mother died with her secret untold. She lies in a dishonoured grave in a remote country churchyard, and the headstone bears the name of Magdalen.'" Miss Norreys shuddered- The story stirred her strangely. "And you loved the child of that woman she exclaimed. "Lord Chilton, you allow your en- thusiasm and fancy to run away with your judg- ment. White lilies may take root in foul soil, but I would not care to pluck them .to place in my bosom. This girl, of the parentage you describe, is no fit mate for you. Let her go, my friend. Surely among the high and noble families of this realm you can find some girl of pure blood and unstained inheritance whom you might more gladly take to fill the place in your home left vacant by your honoured mother Let not the next Lady Chilton bring a shadow on the name your mother bore l"</p>
        <p>"No Lady Chilton could ever equal the lady of my love !" said the young viscount, with en- thusiasm. "Gwen is pure and noble and grand of soul, Ma Norrcys—a very angel. And she carries herself like a young princess. If you could see her you would think my description Of her tame and insufficient. I lore her; I cannot live without her, if I have- to search the whole world over."</p>
        <p>"This seems like madness. I fear you are very foolish, Lord Chilton. This girl, according to your own description of her, is no fit wife for you. Young men have these infatuations and get over them—so will you. Let the girl go, my friend," urged Miss Norreys, kindly. "I have had' more experience in life than you. Believe me, these un- amid marriages do not always turn out happily. When the glamour of your passion wears away, when this girl's beauty begins to fade, when your noble friends inquire from what family your wife comes, then you will begin to repent your marriage, aud the yoke you have taken upon you will be very grievous to bear." "'You do not know. Gwen," said the young viz. Count,. sumilißg. "If she were to lose her beauty, her soul, herself, would be left. Were she dis- figured, hideous, starred even, she would be to me stili the loveliest woman in the world, for I know her great nature, her brigkt intellect, her sunny disposition. She is in herself noble. She has no. need of family, fortune, or adventitious aids."</p>
        <p>''And this is love !" said MiseNorreys, smiling and sighing, while her eyes gloomed suddenly with a. look of bitterest retrospect. "I will say no more,. Lord Chilton. You must suit yourself. Only be wise. I have a fancy that blood will tell. And , if bad-blood were to show itself in this seemingly innocent young creature--" "Isnpoesible ! Besides, in spite of all the facts, r often refused to believe that she is of bad blood," criet Lord Chilton. "She has all the points that distinguish people of aristocratic lineage. We are taught to believe that generations of culture and refinement produce certain traits of character and persoa that are not to be mistaken. She has all these traits. The small; arched feet, the small, slim heads, the tiny ears, the haughty little head, the graceful carriage, the exquisite gentleness and innate refinement, the generous, brave soul that scorna all falsehood—all these are hors. That she is of good blood it is impossible to doubt. And yet—and yet—" 'en have interested me in her," said Miss Nor- reys.. When and how did you first meet her ?" "It was on a wild Yorkshire moor. I rescued her from insult, and took her home. I visited her ahnost daily thereafter, thinking her mistress of the lonely old house. But I found that she was a dependent there, upon the bounty of old servants. I loved her and asked her to be my wife. She accepted me. I was called away suddenly, and when I went back to her she had vanished. , I have asver seen her since." _</p>
        <p>".And you never even knew who she was 1" "Never, nor did she know herself. Her history remarkable. Have you ever heard of the little town of Penistone, in Yorkshire ?"</p>
        <p>Miss Norreys started, drawing lier breath sharply. She shot. a strange, wild glance at the young viscount, and grew suddenly, deathly white. . He did not mark her singular emotion, but con- tinued— . _ _</p>
        <p>" But of course you have not. It is a little market town on the Manchester an Sheffield line, in the midst of a bleak and dreary district. All around it lie the moors, wild a 341 desolate and strange, dotted here and there with hamlets, yet, for the most part, bare and barrcn." Miss Norrays shivered a little,, and drew the soft folds of her shawl closer. _1</p>
        <p>"One of these moors, called Lonemoor," eon- tinned Lord Chilton, "seems Urger, more barren and bleak, than the rest. It is the property of a gentleman, who has built a, shooting box upon it and leases it during the season. He ha a a large farm upon its edge, and his house is on the moor, and sot in its own large grounds. It is soiled the Lonemoor House, or su-nPy Lonemoor."' Miss Norreys's head was drooping. Could Lord Chilton have seen her faee he would have been frightene4.l. Its psllor was,terrible. But ho, absorbed in thoughts of Gli.es at in the story he was narrating, never thought tn took at her. "It is an old house among trees, with steep gables, and tall, clustering ri*ulleys," he said, with old•fashioned gardens alai 'stableyard and outbuildings—a house that in its day has aeon many a gay assemblage within its old wallsa house, I have beard, that was once noted for its open hospi- tality, its pleasant cheer, its genial warmth of r 'welcome to everyone who chose to seek its shelter. I It belongs to Squire Markham, a 'Atli% old landed</p>
        <p>Yorkshire squire, Who was once a genial gentleman, but who become a misanthrope and a wanderer over the earth."</p>
        <p>Miss Norreys's • slender fingers tightened upon the folds of her shawl as if her nerves were turned to steeL</p>
        <p>"Squire Markham had years ago an only child, a daughter whom he idolized," said Lord Chilton. "She died when very young on the Continent some- where while upon a pleasure-tour. The shock of her death nearly killed him. He returned home from his visit to her grave, dismissed the larger. number of his servants and closed his house to visitors. He seemed suddenly to hate his kind. He became harsh, grim, and cold. He never smiled after her death. A year afterwards he quitted England, and has never returned until a couple of months since. And even now he has not come to stay. He is soured, embittered, and aged by his great loss, and I hear is going back to Egypt soon. lie hopes to die there." . _ _</p>
        <p>Mis; Norreys's mouth, in the shadow, looked strangely drawn and white. There was a tortured expression in her brown eyes—an expression as cf a soul writhing in utter anguish. "My account of lafincinoor, and its strange .master, may seem uncalled for," said the young viscount, half apologetically, "but, in truth, it is necessary'. I want you to understand the gloomy old house, and its gloomy old master, for it was in that house my Gwen was born, that old roof sheltered her innocent childhood, and it was there I visited her. Squire Markham never saw her face, although she was born in his house some months before he quitted it."</p>
        <p>Still Miss Norreys made no comment. "The house is surrounded on two sides by its gardens and lawn, the stableyard is in the rear, abutting upon the moor, and, upon one side of the old house, the barren waste of moorland stretches to the very windows. To this house, one wild and terrible night in November, when snow, and sleet and wind united to create a tempest that is still remembered in those parts, a woman came stag- gering across the moor. It was seventeen years ago—" . .</p>
        <p>Seventeen years ago 1" echoed Miss Norreys in a horrified whisper. "h! wo_man alone, on foot, and but thinly clad. She ioulid the kreat-entrance, and rang thwknocker. The old housekeeper went to the door, and there tottered in a frail girl, with loosened hair, and white, wet face and wild eyes, who fell upon the floor in a swoon. They carried her upstairs. The butler - hurried for a doctor and the housekeeper removed the sodden garments, and ministered to the wanderer. Her clothing was fine, her shawl was costly, her appearance was that of a lady. She was delicious or insane from exposure or previous troubles."</p>
        <p>Miss Norroys gave a quick strange gasp. Her white face was absolutely blazing with horror and excitement. It was well, perhaps, that Lord Chil- ton did not see it, or he might have thought her insane also.</p>
        <p>"That night," continued the young viscount, speaking in a lower tone, "the young stranger gave birth to a child. She showed no affection for it. She never spoke to any one, or told her name, or whence she came nor who were her friends, but seemed stupefied and dazed, staring with wild eyes at every one. The,doctor told me that he was Mare that her mind was diseased. Some (great trouble had destroyed her reason. She had been at Lone- moor a month, when one night occurred another storm, even more terrible than the one in which she had come to Lonemoor. Something in the wild strife of the elements excited her," said Lord Chil- ton, telling the story as the doctor had told it to him, but varying from the truth as Mrs. Quillet and Squire Markham knew it, and as the reader knows it. "She was reminded of the night in which she had came to Lonemoor. Perhaps some memory of home stirred within her. Perhaps her insanity. rose to the height of madness. At any rate, she took advantage of the temporary absence of the nurse, and fled from the house into the awful storm."</p>
        <p>Miss Norrey's look turned to stone. "They searched for her that night they searched next day, and for clays after. The snow fell like a winding sheet upon the moor, and covered furze and shrub out of sight. It fell for days. They could not find her at Penistone, nor at Mulford Bridge. The snow stayed late on the grouml that winter. It was April when the snow melted, and they found one day in a little hollow in the moor all that was left of her. She had stumbled there upon that awful night, and had been, too weak to rise again, and the snow had :covered her over and buried her out of sight." The viscount shuddered. Miss Norreys still sat .motionless,,, her eyes still wild with an unutterable horror..</p>
        <p>"Dead,." she whispered, "Were they sure that, it was- the same." "Yes, the old housekeeper recognised her. The housekeeper caused her body to be buried at Penis- tone,. and it was she who caused the gravestone to be erected to the girl's memory." "Mid, the housekeeper had that name put on the stoner said Miss Norreys, huskily. " KnOwing not her • real name, she caused that name, Magdalen, to be put upon it," said Lord Chilton.. 'But I. shall replace the stone with another, un. which I shall have cut the word Infelicia. " The .childl—the child?" "She lived, and grew up to womanhood. She is my Gwen, whom 1 have described to yon." _</p>
        <p>Miss Norreys lips wore white and tiff. She passed list finger over them unconsciously, but there was no flexibility in them, as she said, in that same husky, unnatural whisper— " They made's: servant of her She grew up in ignorance 7" lie never sea, her, and never did anything for her. The two old servants had their lifetime of savings, and they eared tenderly for the poor little outcast!'</p>
        <p>But they made a servant of her? She grew up in ignorance ?" Lord Chiltombegan to notice how strained and husky was the• lady's whisper, and that her face was drooping and averted. He thought what a aympathiaing nature she possessed, and his heart warmed to her irr a quick gratitude.</p>
        <p>" One:wouldihaveithouiht so," he replied; "but they dithnot make. a servant of her. They believed her mother to have been a lady. Perhaps they thought that they would be rewarded some day for all they Might do for the child. Perhaps they thought that her father might turn up, and pay themull.they 4a expended with interest. At any. rate,• they treated the chio as if she were their superior,. They clothed her daintily. They pro-- oured.a governess to instruct her. This governess. was alady, a gentlewoman of birth and education), and she had charge of the child for seven years,. ;until,Gwen was fourteen. Then the lady married ,a missionary"—it was so the viscount had been informed, by the Quillets, to throw him off the 'scent In, his, search for Gwen—" of the iiame of and went to Africa. Gwen was sent to a Paris pep/Own:at of the best class, and was breught up among noblyborn girls. She was brought up a lady, you see, ;and is refined and accomplished, and I thoroughly educated. She never suspected until ' last autumn, that she was not a relative of Squire Markham,"</p>
        <p>"Strange that the Quillets should have educated her andibronglat her tip like that if they believed • her the child :of a wicked Magdalen," murmured Miss Norreys,, pressing her hand to her forehead. "It cannot be that they thought so. And why should they spend money upon her as a speculation, when every possibility was against the father's being found and the money paid back? I—l don't know what to 'think."</p>
        <p>"Nor do. L Their conduct is cutainly mys- terious. They seem people of hard,. sound jndg- meot, not at all likely to be led away from prac, :tics' views of romantic notions. It must be that Providence guided them in the matter. The chile was remarkably beautiful and daizaty in her ways. They called her Princess on account of her pretty peculiaritiis !" "They were very sure that it, was the body of the mother that they found !" "Very sure," said Lord Chilton, surprised, "Mrs, Quillet recognised it." A hopeless look of bewilderment appeared in the lady's eyes. ‘, Was Mrs. Quillet food of the child?" she asked, after a pause. "Singularly enough, she was not,* declared , Lord Chilton. She trea•ed her kindiy, but as if it were a matter of &amp;sty. She sometimes seemed ' to feet for her a positive hatred. She might have doubted the wisdom of expending so much money on the little waif, and feared that she should never recover it. At any rate, she shrank from Gwen as if the girl's presence were a pain and wrong to her, and the poor child never knew in all her life— except as she knew it from governesses and fellow pupils at school—what it was to be loved. Poor little Gwen! Her whole Me has been hard and cold, and desolate, a very Winter, in truth. And even now she is somewhere out in the great, struggling world, helpless, defenceless. I would give •my data hand to known her whereabouts—</p>
        <p>poor lost Mlle Owen!" His voice broke titeivn suddenly. Was th3t half-choked sob that euivie from the cold and prou East India heiress? "Lord Chilton," she aged, impetuously', her voice gaining strength, 1‘ we must search for thirs girl ! You are right to cling to her, and I you for it. I will help you in your search, .woman's wit can be of any avail. We will put me' upon her track. We will find her l" Her eyes glowed like living coals amid the white; ness of her face. She was fired with a strange lie •</p>
        <p>zeal, with an ever-mastering purpose. ." We will find her !" she repeated, arising het tO feet, "if we have to search the whole world Ore.' Give me time to think. We will talk of this again.</p>
        <p>Some one is coming now I" Sir William Ensor and his sister and Miss Kenright wemapproaching. aia Miss Norreys flitted away like a spirit, 110'. she pause until she reached her own boudoir. TD_S she gave way to the emotions agitating her to"' and wrung her hands, crying out— "I am bewildered 1 lam in a frightful be r derment. Who is this girl? Who was mother? Was that body which was found, body of her mother? I must know the truth. have sent Mr. Barsby away upon another erralsui; but if he were here, I dare not employ him th b matter. Whom can I trust? No ono—no one bt.i my faithful Aga. He is a Hindoo, but learned use English ways. I have no one else to send, ail„(1 Is as keen upon scent as a bloodhound. Hindoo as he is—would succeed where all the. English fail. I will send Aga." She rang her bell, and ordered her Hindoo Or' vant to be sent to her. He came present/Y1 lithe, sinewy, tall East Indian, with small eVi,t; and a face as calm and impenetrable as that of t.O. Sphinx. More than once he had saved his friblo tress's life in India. Like his wife he adored , _ _ .</p>
        <p>gentle, kindly mistress, and would have we'' upon hot ploughshares at her bidding. To this man Miss Norreys gave her commisehl and he departed. An hour or two later, he ht y,-* quitted 13eeohmont, and was on his way to or shire.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0010" n="art0010">
        <head>Tinned Meats.</head>
        <p>Tinned Meats.</p>
        <p>Apropos of tinned meats, it is proposed to abolish tins altogether and to substitute glass. The change might be welcome, for nothing could be wois than to incur the risk of being poisoned at your very table by either sardines or lobster, nobody can tell how long in tins. But there would be a danger even with bottled sardines. The glass would break at times, and how many fingers would be cut in cOnsequence there is no knowing. Whether glass is adopted or not, it is pretty certain that some plan should be devised by which there would be less danger than that ut present existing of being poisoned over a meal..</p>
        <p>Chicken and Ham in Jelly. This is a nice dish for supper or luncheon. Make with a small knuckle of veal some good white stock. When cold, skim, strain, and melt it, and put a quart of iç Into a saucepan with the well beaten whites of three eggs, a dessert spoonful of chili, or a table spoonful of vinegar, and a little salt. Beat the mixture with a fork till it boils, and simmer till reduced to little more than a pint. Strain it, put half of it into a mould, let it nearly set, cut the meat of a roast chicken into small pieces, arrange it in the jelly with neat slices of co d boiled ham. Sprinkle chopped patsley between' the slices. When quite cold, pour in the remainder 9f the jelly ; set the mould in a cool place so that it sets speedily ; dip the mould in boiling water to turn out, and do not let it remain more than a minute. Garnish witit parsley.</p>
        <p>A Cheap Soup. Peasoup.—Take a pint of whole dried peas, or more, according to the number of persons. Soak them in cold water all night ; next morning pour off this water, put the peas into a saucepan with a quart and a half of water, a few carrots and turnips cut up, a small onion, and a little pepper, Let all this stew very gently until tender ; when half done, put in a pickled pigs foot, or a very small piece of pickled pork or bacon (the latter is not so good as pork), and continue to simmer, until the meat is well done. If too thick, the soup can be made thinner with a little boiling water. Turn out into a deep dish. It is a very cheap dish, and, if properly an 3 slowly stewed, is extremely good. In all towns pigs' feet are to be had very cheap. Three .pennyworth of fresh hones from the butchtv would do, broken up and boiled with peas, and the cost of the soup would not ex- ceed 9d. to Is. and would afford a dinner for an ordin- ary family.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>CHARLES JAMES FOX.</head>
        <p>CHARLES JAMES FOX.</p>
        <p>The character of this unique man was not a chap- ter of contradictions;. each part of his nature was in harmony with all the rest. With talents, good nature, and truthfulness, he had no restraining principles, and looked down with contempt on those who had. Priding himself on ignorance of every self-denying virtue, an adept in debauch and vain of his excesses,he feared nothing. Unlucky at the gam- ing table beyond all calculation of chances, draining the cup of pleasure to the dregs, fond of loose women and beloved by them, the delight of profligates, the spoot of : ~isurers, impoverished by his vices, he braved scandal, and gloried in a lordly recklessness of his inability to pay Isis debts, as if superb ostentation in misfortune raised him abcve all his fellow-men. He had a strong will ; but he never used it to bridle his passions, even though their indulgence wronged his own father, or corrupted his young admirers. Born to wealth and rank and easy access to the service of the king, at heart an aristocrat, he could scoff at monarchy and hold the language of a leveller and a demagogue. He loved poetry and elegant letters, the songs of Homer above all ; but science was too dull for him, and even the style and lucidity and novelty of Adam Smith could not charm tl4e licentious, rollicking statesman to the study of political economy. His uncurbed licentiousness seemed rather to excite than to exhaust his lofty powers ; his perceptions were quick and instinctively true ; and in his wildest dissipation ho retained an unextinguishable passion for activity of in- tellect. Living as though men and women were instru- ments of pleasure, he yet felt himself destined for great things, and called forth to tke service of mankind. Ti be talked about he would stake all he had and more on a wager ; but the all-conquering instiltct of his ambition drove him to the House of Commons. There his genius was at home ; and that body cherished him with the indulgent pride which it always manifests to those who keep up its high reputation with the world. A knotty brow, a dark brown complexion, thick, shaggy eye- brows, and a compact frame marked a rugged audacity and a commanding energy, which made him rude and terrible as an adversary • but with all this, he had a loveliness of temper which se endeared him to his friends that the survivors among them never ceased the praise of the sweetness and gentleness of his familiar inter- course. It was natural to him to yenerate greatness like Edmund Burke's ; and a wound in his affections ' easily moved him to tears. His life was dissolute ; his speech was austere. His words were all pure English ; he took no pains to hunt after tieorn ; the aptest came at Iris call, and seemed to belong to hint. Every part of his discourse lived and moved. He never gave up strength of statement for beauty of expression ; and never stopped to fill up gaps with fine phrases. His healthy diction was unaffectedly simple and nervous. always effective, sometimes majestic and resounding, rarely ornate, and then only when he impressed a saying of poet or philosopher to tip his argument with fire. He never dazzled with brilliant colours, but could startle by boldness in the contrast of light and shade. He forced his hearers to be attentive and docile; for he spoke only when he had something to say that needed to be said, and compelled admiration because he made himself understood. What was entangled he could enfold quickly and lucidly ; now speaking with copious fluency, and now discussing point by point ; at one time confining debate within the narrowest limits, and again speaking as if inspired to plead the welfare of all man- kind. He had a wonderful gift at finding and bringing together what he wanted,though lying far off and apart. It was his wont to march straight forward to his end ; but he knew how to turn aside from an attack,to retreat with his eye ever on his enemy, and then, by an unex- pected reversion, to strike him suddenly as with talons. When involved in dispute, he dashed at the central ices, which was of power to decide the question ; grasped it firm and held it fast ; turned it over and over on every side ; presented it in the most various aspects ; came back to dwell upon it With fresh force ; renewed blow after blow until it became annealed like steel. He hit the nail again and again, and always on the head,till he drove it home into the minds of his hearers ; and when he was beaten by the majority, he still bore away the palm as a wrestler. His merits, as summed up by Mackintosh, were "reason, simplicity, and vehemence."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>EXTRAORDINARY FEATS OF MEMORY.</head>
        <p>EXTRAORDINARY FEATS OF MEMORY.</p>
        <p>The following is from the " Westminster Gazette —Sir George Trevelyan ascribes to Lord Macaulay some extraordinary feats of memory. But in Theophile Gautier, it seems, Macaulay might have found his match. His thirst for reading, says Maximo du Camp, was in- satiable, and he consumed grammars, prospectus, s, cookery books, and almanacs as greedily as works of science and imagination. Whatever he read he remem- bered ; and his friends whera in doubt on any point, used to say, " NVe have only got to turn over the leaves of Th9o." On the eveninc of the day on which a n.w volume by victor Hugo had been published, he was dining with some friends. "In his clear voice, with- out inflection, a monochord so to speak, his eves gazing steadfastly as though he was reading from ar-sir a book visible to himself alone,. le recited tha 158 verses of • Les Lions' not repeating himself once, never hesit.ting, and not mistaking a single syllable. We were astonished and said to him, ' You have learnt it by heart. then ? ' To which he replied. N0,,1 read it this morning while I was breakfasting.'" This almost vies with Macaulay's repetition of the" Lay oi the Last Minstrel " after a single reading. Perhaps the most extraordinary feat of memory attributed te!Macaulay was his repetition. forty years afterwardsof a Welsh poem which he had read once through In a provincial paper !</p>
        <p>" CADBURY'S COCOA has, In a remarkable degree, those natural elements of suotenance which glvethe system endur- ance and hardihoott, with ft steady action t,nt renders it a most acceptable and reliable beverage."—Nceith. A farmer named Thomas Booth, aged 89, of Weaver- ham Moor, Nantwich, was, on Saturday, fearfully injured by a cow which he was driving from the field. The cow Euddenlv attacked him, and he was very severely wounded about the head and body. When the old man was rescued he was unconscious, and remained so till his death.</p>
        <p>EPPS'S 00001..--GRATEFITL AND COMFOrreING.—" By a thorough knowledge of the natural laws which govern the operations-of digestion and nutrition, and by a careful ap- plication of the fine properties of well-selected Cocoa. 11r. Epps has provided our breakfa,t tables with R delicately flavoured beverage which may save us many heavy doctors' bills. It Is by the judicious use ot such articles of diet that a constitution may be graduilly built up until strong enough to resist every tenden y to disease. Hundreds of subtle maladies are floating around us ready to attaolc wher- ever there is a weak point. We may escape many a fatal shaft by keeping ourse!ves well fortified with pure blood and a properly nourished frame."—Ciril Sero:ce Gazette.—Made simplywlth boiling water or milk. Sold only in packets, by aroc,ers, labelled Jams 3 Epps, 1 Co„ Romero. ethic Chemists London. Also Halters of Epps's Cocoaine ot. Nib-Eztract,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>TALE OF LONEMOOR.</head>
        <p>A TALE OF LONEMOOR.</p>
        <p>CHAPTER XLIII. LORD CHILTON'S LETTER.</p>
        <p>While this private conference between Miss Nor- reys and her baliff was in progress in the boudoir of theformer, Lord Chilton was busily engaged in the library upon the letter which he had planned to write to Squire Markham. The young viscount began to narrate in what manner he had made the acquaintance of Miss Winter upon Loiremoor—bow lie had believed her to be a relative of Squire Markham, and mistress of the mansion, of Lonetnoor—how he had visited her freqtiently, and his admiration had deepened into love—and how he had finally asked her to marry him, and she had consented to become his wife. He stated that he had been called suddenly from Lonemoor, and that when he had returned she had gone and that though he had searched everywhere for her, and bad advertised and employed assist- ance to trace her, yet he had not been able to find her.</p>
        <p>ne declared that he knew Gwen's history—as much of it as was known to herself—but that he eareernothing for her pedigree or origin—that he loved her just as she was, and that she, poor and usineless, was greater in his sight than the highest. ,born lady in the realm. He wrote with the enthu- siasm of the ardent lover, extolling her beauty, her grace, her sweetness and nobleness. He declared that, whatever her birth, Gwen was a lads, pure and noble, and grand-souled. Then he stated libw he had visited Lonemoor repeatedly in the hope of inducing the Quillets to tell him where she was, but they had refused He implored Squire Markham to use his influence with his Wier and housekeeper to induce them to reveal Gwen's whereabouts, and he begged the squire to eo.ramunicate.the same to him forthwith. te,otictittez,n.ip informed Squire Markham that he—the Nvtiter—m, viscount Chilton, his own -si master, and privileged to su icllil his mar- riage. He regarded Gwen as more than the peer of any lady in the reahn, and he again besought the squire to assist him to find her. The letter covered .several pages, was passionate and enthusiastic, showing his love for and faith in Gwen in every line. If any letter could ever move the hardened hearb of old Squire Markham, this was certainly the one. Lord Chilton added in a postscript_ that-he was staying for a fortnight at Beechinont—,the property of Miss Norreys—nettr Shrewsbury, Salop, to which address he begged that an anmer might be sent. Thia composition reqaiied conaideraide time, and when tinialied, its seve: al pages made quite a little ! nacket, which he sealed, summed, and addres.ied.</p>
        <p>The inmates of Beechmont were wont to deposit their letters upon the hall table, whence they were removed by the butler, who placed 'there' in the postbag for transportation to the Dunholm post office.</p>
        <p>The young viscount placed his letters upon the hall table, upon a little pile of similar missives, and took up his hat and strolled out of the park. A little later .Mr.Barsby departed, as we have said. Miss Norreys then entered the great hall, with the intention of seeing the croquet lawn to join her guests. As she passed the table the little pile of letters upon it attracted her notice. - She paused for a momeat., the bold chirography of Lord Chilton enchaining her rapid glance. She was too proud and too honourable even to look at the address of a letter not meant for her eyes, and how it happened she did not know, but every word and letter of that address stood out from the white paper as if written with living coals—" Squire Markham, Lonemoor, Penistone, Yorkshire." Miss Norreys's eyes were rivetted to the words, her face growing suddenly white, and her breathing became difficult.</p>
        <p>Then she put up her hand blindly, staggered back a pace, and dropped heavily into a chair. The hall porter ran to her with a glass of water from a table near at hand. She drank the liquid at a draught, dismissed him with a gesture, and muttered to herself that it was strange that she should have felt so faint. She abandoned the idea of joining the party at croquet, and went up to her room, shutting herself up for hours in solitude. Yet, when the hour for dinner approached, Miss Norreys admitted Naya, and made a grand toilette, descending to the drawing-room in a peach-coloured silk, trimmed with innumerable fine plaitings, and wearing a parure of diamonds that would have excited the envy of nearly every woman in Eng- land.</p>
        <p>Her father had leen dead more than a year, and she had laid aaide her mouriaing for him on enter- ing society in London. She was, the life of the little company that even- ing. She sang and played like one possessed with the soul a Musk. She inaugurated little social games, such as have been popularised by royalty— Twenty Questions and the like: The various mem- bers of the party wrote down the names of their favourite heroes and favourite authors, and so forth, and Miss Norreys examined the different handwritings and singled out Lord Chilton's a* beingidentical with the address upon the envelope that had attracteil her attention. She had des- troyed the various letters of acceptance to her. invitations, but this little ruse put hey in possessim of the information•she wanted.</p>
        <p>Yet she made no remark to the young viscount concerning his letter or his personal affairs, although she had opportunity enough that evening. But the next day, when Mrs. Kenright was. taking her morning nap, and Miss Kenright had taken Colonel Warburton to drive in a pony car- riage, and Sir William Ensor had enticed his sister and Miss Milly Kenright down to Dark River, and to row thereupon in a gay little barge, Miss Nor- reys wandered in the gardens, and Lord Chilton was at her side. - - - .</p>
        <p>There were dark shadows under the dusky eyes of the East Indian heiress, but her pale olive face was bright and animated, a restless flush burning fitfully on her cheeks, making her lookk, strangely lovely. "I am in a match-making mood' this morning, Lord Chilton," she said gaily. "Has not my party so far been a complete success! You know that Colonel Warburton proposed to Miss Kenright last evening' do you not,. and, that Mrs.. Kenright is delighted ?" "Yes, I know," assented' Lord' Chilton.. "It will be a very suitable match--and a love-match also. But how is it that you are in. a match-making mood, Miss Norreys ? Is not one-success enough?' "No," she answered. "Besides . I. had• no. thought of match-making when I rule&amp; you all here. I deserve little credit for bringing Colonel Warburton and Miss Kenright together. They have long been fond of each.other. But I fancy Sir William and Milly are growing to like each other.. Now, my lord, if you and Miss Ensor--2' Lord Chilton laughed and looked grave:. "My dear Miss Norreys.,' he-said,.." do not Ova for me. lam betrothed, you.know„to•the loveliest. girl in England—if she is in EnglancL?" The two w4lked downthe wide level path between: the trim box borders, aethe lady rejoined— " Tell me something about her,. Lord Chilton.. What should cross the 'course of your true love? The lady could not have proved, false„ else- you. would have ceased to love her."</p>
        <p>. The young viscount hesitated.. The, .mood wan upon him to tell Miss Norreys all. She was so sympathising, so like a dear elder sister,. and he longed for wise council, such as her clear womanle head might well give him. But how could he declare to her Gwen's history? It seeme,i impos- ed&amp; to do it. Yet that history was no secret at Penistone, he remembered. If she were ever to know Gwen, •she would be likely to hear that story. It would be no Betrayal of confidence to tell it. And she was too noble to visit upon: °wet— as the world had done—the sins of her parents. "I- will tell you the story, Miss Norreys," he exclaimed, acting upon his overmastering impulse. "I have carried my trouble in my own heart for months, aixl it is like the fox of the Spartan lad. Not her story—thank heaven, I don't care for that --but her loss-rher strange disappearance. Sit down on this garden-seat, Miss Norreys, and let me tell you about the girl I loved and lost, on the wild Yorkshire moors—the loveliest creature ever born r</p>
        <p>CHAPTER /WV. A CONFIDENTIAL COMMUNICATION.</p>
        <p>Miss Norreys and. Lord Chilton sat down side by side upon a wide garden seat. The sun was at their backs, their faces being in shadow. They were silent for a brief space, a look of intense interest on the face of the lady. She had grown to entertain a strange liking for the young viscount, and she longed to dissipate that shadow of melan- choly that darkened his face. It was she who spoke first, and she said, gently— " You speak of having lost your betrothed, Lord Chilton, and you speak of her also as living. Pardon me, but was there not eomo misunderstanding between you, which a few words would have • cleared up t.!</p>
      </div>
