        <p>CLOSE CALL FOR " COLIN ROSLO,' LIM AUSTRALIAN "WIZARD," CON- " ROR AND ILLUSIONIST.</p>
        <p>—`• N. Ward ("Colin Roslo "), of 14, Vestry-st., Shef- old, atates that: "Nature endowed me with a strong tenstitution, enabling me to endure fatigue .ani irre- t'llla!hours, common to those of my piofeestou, pre- teorimg through all a good constitution which seemed "fr ailments. All at once, however, I began to feel rat I had less energy than formerly, that my appetite . .7y,s uncertain, that I had pains in the head—and pains 7." "et, with a general unaccountable weariness and esan.ervation. I was carefully examined by a doctor, who ~id, • What is that lump on your liver?' I replied, '1 not.knoNv it was there.' It is a sack,' said the doc- !'°r, ' either filled with water or blood, and you must nodergo an operation and no physic in the world will do !; anY good.' He said to my wife, "rake him home, .tess.liim in thick flannels' put him to bed, and let him ,ternur ain there for he will never get better.' We re- tothome,' must confess feeling,' despondent. I went edand nursed myself 'carefully ; took the physic troescribed by the doctor, but grew weaker. My atten- •4 beillg called to Warner's Safe Cure, I said,' I will just have a try at it.' After the very first dose I ruenced to improve. I have taken 14 bottles, weigh .8: more than I ever did in my life, and am not dead Yet in spite of the frightful verdict."</p>
