        <head>"CAPITAL" CHAT.</head>
        <p>"CAPITAL" CHAT.</p>
        <p>h The 1139nY members of the Royal Family who teave just left Windsor occupied their usual quer- air, at the Castle. There are no apartments lotted to her Majesty's children as their very own, Batten}Vith the exception of the suite appropriated by the , But when they are on a visit they ano • •. aro st invariably gravitate to the same corners, so j lich so that, except on an occasion such as the uoilee visit of the Kaiser, the Queen seldom clis- ebs the well-known practice. Thus, the Tapestry N,A°lll is always sacred to the Empress Fiederi* In en visiting Windsor, who occupied it before her arriage. The Prince of Wales dislikes this, and refers the apartment at the corner of the quad- ,,atlßle, which has an outlook straight down the %.astle-hill into the town. Anotl</p>
        <p>.her member of the Nationalist party whose 'ife a an " Crilly, as he is colloquially known. Persona grata at St. Stephen's is Mr. froan,'Y comes of an old Irish family, and having 4.7 ner childhood breathed an atmosphere of a -blown. Mher sympathies and prejudices are Mr. Gladstone's scheme of conciliation 22 taken the " divilment " out of the Hibernian vettrerais..us_, and therefore Mrs. Crilly and ladies of BtareP can now meet the Sassenach on fairly atnicabl e terms. But she can still sing a good "lan song. One of the most regular frequenters of the Ladies' eGuallery this session is the wife of Mr. "Torn " Lira"e . the juvenile member for Kilkenny Cit 3. her husband, Mrs. Curran is a keen politician, ;iv like her husband she is full of enthusiasm; and in heugh by birth an Australasian,she has managed the short space of five months to develop very great interest in the Home Rule controversy. Mrs. rran is a Roman Catholic " 'cert.," and, as might 110 expected, e,lpected, she is as ,diligent in her devotions as "e la painstaking in her political studies. Her ap- pearance in the lobby ,or on the terrace is always in"c? 81.04 for a symposium ; for the clever little si; Y a great favourite with the party, and when tiveei.e about even MT. T. P. Sullivan becomes talka-</p>
        <p>Mr ei h ' • " • Kent, who is stroking the Leander t at Henley this year, is one of the best-known oi7roen of the present day. He is a member of an welland Popular Molesey family, and so has been Up acquainted with the Thames from his youth 13 went p to Brazencrse College, Oxford, in t." Qeu, and was veryiquickly in with the " rowing 6e - in He assisted B.N.C. to win the 'Varsity Fours „1887 and the Visitors' Cup at Henley in 1888,and 1 - 0 rowed bow in the winning Trial Eight in the battle Year. Next year he stroked B.N.C. when that oat went head olthe river, and kept them in their proud position in 1890. He crowned these successes !)Y stroking Oxford to victory against Cambridge in 1891 and Leander in the Grand at Henley in the B:_letntwar and 180. He has also led Molesey to many times. Much of his success has been °lne to the pinck and determination he possesses. His l'aPUrts at the end of punishing races have fairly isonished the critics. " Colly Wobble," as his rienda call him, is well known on the Stock ~,!`enange,and is one of the directors of the "Thames</p>
        <p>A Labor MX. broken down by the strain of Parlia- mentary lire and descending into a premature grave ! Filch is the sad news brought by a recent Australian mail. At the general election in Victoria last year North Melbourne returned Mr. D. H. Wyllie, the fotinder and president of the Tinsmiths' Society, and its delegate for ten years on the Mel- bourne Trade Hall Council. Now he is dead at the early age of thirty-seven. The breakdown of his health is attributed to the "constant worries of his Parliamentary position."</p>
        <p>When, as Viscount Lymington, Lord Portsmouth aatfor the South Molton. division of Devonshire, he had the reputation of wearing the shortest trousers of any member of the House of Commons. Ilia coats were always of the latest cut, his hats _Were of the most approved shape, and in other ttw.a.Y3 he was essentially a dandy, but his continu- elle were invariably braced up to the utmost. Was in the lobby for some time on Tuesday --adt. and sartorial critics noticed that his old Pteuliarity still clung to him. Some .members make a Practice of turning up their trousers in all sorts °f Weather, kilt on the muddiest day Lord I orts- - Th"3atil does not need to take this precaution.</p>
        <p>The ---- the Queen ‘is getting more and more particular in aro Inatter of .counterfeit presentations of herself, IC' "as been muh interested in the reports that Jive reached her as to the merits of the Kensington a_Leatue f •Torn the artistic standpoint. With some r_ cent portraits of herself she has not been very lawnell satisfied, and is apt to compare the work of tistried lingers with one or two favourite' composi- 8, such as Angeli's " 1885" likeness of herself lat Ich is much prized at Windsor. The bust of the pi e Duke of Clarence. which was supposed to be gna„e;ed ill position at Frogmore on the last death en ".versar3-, of iier consort, was retained in the 8t le at the last moment. On other occasions, also a 4 Rood deal of alteratiun-- admittedly for the better ex as been suggested by her Majesty, who has an recet)ti°nally keen eye for bad drawing and bad s odelling—of which, all the same, there are con- examples at Windsor and elsewhere.</p>
        <p>Th Queen's connection with Kensington is of (nirt'e longer than loyal subjects care to think. Wilb „ erforce visited the Duchess of Kent there when _',l_e•Princess Victoria was only a twelvemonth old, 7_llcl wrote a letter to Hannah Mote, saying how he e::nd by h the " tine' auiniateci child " playing on the e ce.Aen-air breakfasts taken by mother (1" er motber's side. Charles Knight used to th 'mad child in Kensington Palace gardens in the early eruing, before the world was astir. It was here int the duchess remarLed to the youthful princess ;as Caroline Fox relates--" I am anxious to bring Ill) as a good woman, d then you will he a I4C" Queen also." The apen-sir habit formed thus e,rlY Under the shade of the Kensington trees has ung to tier Majesty eves since.</p>
        <p>rs_P erean resident Clld is t itnr to reduce his weight zolh. a cs , di., month by aj n udicious regulatio of his an'd'arS'. lie only takes two meals per day, t!reakfast all light qinner,has no supper or luncheon,eschews gr..statellY foods, and takes ftuicls or liquors in the eatest mcderation. lie r6eis fromhis food hun- and he is hearing the new order of things with Partan resignation. •</p>
        <p>Mr. William Abraham, who was on Tuesday elect ed by a convention of Nationalists to succeed vr. Michael Davitt in North East Cork, is a County :Illerick man, having been chairman of the netick Board of Guardians for many years, while 1118 connection with Nationalist politics in Ireland haatb°,en Ing and honourable. lie is a Protestant , been a. Home Ruler, and while he repre- West Limerick in Parliament, from 1885 to C!'4, was one of the most popular of the Irish ment- ara who habitually addressed English meetings in Of the Home Rule cause. In appearance Mr. Abraham is a 9)lendid specimen of a robust Irish Fentleman farmer. lie is middle-aged. and possesses dark complexion and strongly marked features.</p>
        <p>Mr i ----.- n ,• -ohn F. Taylor, who was called to the Inner b rir by the Irish Lord Chancellor on Monday kW, . as obtained the honour of a silk gown after the "usually short apprenticeship of eleven years at the Outer Bar. Air. Taylor is known in Ireland as ".‘n_e of the most prominent members of the Con- :14110a Circuit. His reputation has, however, ex- . tended beyond the Channel, as he is the Duhlin .9orrespc,nl ent of the "Manchester a uard tan," whose letters on political matters, attract so ranch atten,</p>
        <p>tion. Mr. Taylor is a tall, slfght man, well under forty, with high complexion and large brown eyes and very intelligent and pleasing expression of countenance. His energy and power of work are simply boundless. Long after he had been called to the Bar, and when he was in extensive practice, he matriculated at Trinity College, Dublin, submitted himstlf to its discipline, lectures, and examinations for three years,and now holds an Arts degree in the , University of Dublin.</p>
        <p>Dr. Andrew Commins, who has been unanimously selected as the Nationalist candidate for the South- Eastern divisien of Cork at the Nationalist conven- tion On Monday last, is well-irnown in the 119 use 9f , Commons, having represented IlosConlolon ously from 1880 till the last General Election, when he was ousted from his seat by Mr. Luke Hayden in the Parnellite interest. Dr. Coramins is a tall, thin man, with sandy hair, blue eyes, and aquiline nose. He is a little over sixty years of age, is an L.L.D. of London University, and a barrister of Lincoln's Inn where he was called thirty three years ago. He has a large commercial and criminal practice in Liver- pool, where he has worked at the Bar with great success, and has acquired the reputation of a care- ful and able advocate. His speeches in the House of Commons were somewhat recondite, a fact which gained for him from the Tories the sobriquet of " The Chancellor." Dr. Commins threw himself with great vigor into the struggle in Committee room 15, and was the proposer of a motion for the deposition of Mr. Parnell.</p>
        <p>THE PROPOSED REDUCTION IN MIN EWS WAGES. -- 300,000 MEN AFFECTED. The notification a hich has been forwarded by the Coalovrners' Federation to the Executive of the Miners' Federation of Great Britain, inviting them to a joint meeting for the purpose of considering a request for a reduction in wages of 25 per cent., is the most serious step that has been taken in con- nection with the coal trade since the wages settle- ment in 1888, For some time past the downward course of prices in the coal trade has made it evid- ent that a reduction in wages would, sooner or later, have to be seriously considered. In the min- ing districts which are outside the control of the Miners' Federation of Great Britain, very considerable reductions in wages have already taken place, and the colliery owners in those districts, with the advantage of lower wages, have been competing for business at exces- sively low figures, with the result that during the last two or three months the colliery owners in Lancashire, Yorkshire, and other mining centres where wages have I been kept up by the Miners' Federation of Great Britain have found themselves placed in a most difficult posi- tion in tendering for the large contracts for locomotive fuel and gas coal that have come upon the market. To secure locomotive contracts, they have had to give way fully is. per ton on the prices obtained last year, whilst for gas coal contracts prices have been cut dawn is. to ' Is. 6d. per ton upon those which were got last season, and in all descriptions of round coal for ironmaking I steam, and general manufacturing purposes, sales of anything like quantities have only been possible at prices lower than have been known for a considerable time past. In view of this very serious fall in prices the coalowners of Lancashire and other districts have since early on in the year been considering the advisability of taking action to secure a substantial , reduction in wages, but so far no really definite step in this direction has been possible owing to the coat- owners of Yorkshire holding back from joining in the movement, and without the co-operation of the import- ant mining district of Yorkshire it was regarded asl inadvisable to proceed any further. The Yorkshire colliery owners would, however, seem now to have decided upon takingloint action with the other mining districts in a movement for reducing wages, the notifica- tion which has been forwarded to the executive of the Miners' Federation being signed by Mr. T. R. Ellis, of Wigan, who represents the Lancashire coalovi ners, and Mr. Parker Rhodes, who may be regarded the'repre- sentative of the Yorkshire coalowners, and who are the joint secretaries of the Coalowners' Federation. This notification, which is dated from Westminster Palace Ilotel, June 23, 1593, and addressed to Mr. Thomas Ashton, secretary of the Miners' Federation of Great Britain, is as follows Dear Sir,—The coalowners of federated districts, at a meeting held there to-day, have directed us to ask you to convene a meeting of your executive committee, to 'meet a committee of their body at the Westminster Palace Hotel, London on Friday next, June 30, 1893, at 11,30 in the forenoon, for the purpose of considering a request by the owners for a reduction in wages of 25 per cent." On the receipt of this notification a consultation was held with the officials of the Miners' Federation, and it was resolved that Mr. Ashton, the secretary, should call their executive committee together to meet the commit- tee of the CoalownerS' Federation, at the Westminster Palace Hotel, as invited. The Executive Committee of the Miners' Federation will, however, have no power whatever, even if they were inclined, to agree to any reduction in wages. All that they will be enabled to do will be to hear what the committee of the Coalowners' rederation have to say with regard to the proposed re- duction in wages, and if this is persisted in, to then convene a s?ecial conference of the Miners' Federation, which will be attended by representatives appointed by the workmen from the various districts, and who would decide upon the action that should be taken with regard to the proposed reduction in wages. The proposed reduction is 25 per cent, and applies to the whole of the Miners' Federation of Great Britain, and will affect some :300;000 men. So far as can at pre- sent be gathered., there is a very strong feeling against accepting the reduction,which the officials of the Miners' Federation do not consider wilt at all improve the posi- tion of the coalowners. Their view of the matter is that the reduction in the price of coal might have been pre- vented if the colliery owners had only combined together to maintain winos instead or combining to reduce wages, and there would certainly seem to no prospect of serious opposition to any such reduction in wages as is proposed. ST. HELENS MINERS AND THE UNION. At the district meeting of miners' delegates at St. Helens, on Mondayoit was reported that the miners were joining the union in a satisfactory manner, and, con- sidering the bad state of trade, it was deemed injudicious to precipitate a struggle. It was decided to give the men—principally drawers and daywagemen—who have not yet joined another month in which to do so. The reply of the Home Secretary to Baron de Worms regard- ing the circulars issued by Mr. Glover, J.P., was received with satisfaction. It was thought in St. Helens on Tues- day that the movement on the part of the coal-owners to reduce wages by 25 per cent. will meet with stubborn resistance, and that in Laecashire as well as other places a great strike may ensue.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>TROUBLES OF THE HOUGHTON FAMILY.</head>
        <p>TROUBLES OF THE HOUGHTON FAMILY.</p>
        <p>At the Newton Petty Sessions, on Saturday, be- fore Mr. T. J. Gillespie (in the chair), and Colonel Wood, Mary Houghton, a married woman, of Lawrence-street, Earlestown, was charged with being drunk and disorderly at Earlestown on June 3rd. Defendant also took out a summons against P.C. Mason for assaulting her at the same time and place. Mr. A. Browne, Warrington, represented Mrs. Hough ton, and Mr. Stuart represented Mason. Mason stated that about 11 o'clock on Satur- day, June 3rd, he received instructions to go to Lawrence-st., and saw Mrs. Houghton drunk and using foul language. Ile asked her for her name and took hold of her arm at the same time and told her to go inside the house. lie was in plain clothes at the time. In answer to Nir. Browne, he said be was not aware that be had stuck his finger nails into the defendant's arm as she .complained. He went to her at the instrootion of Sergeant Longwoith. Evidence for the prosecution was given by Thomas Pea rson,Eliza Ann Ogden and P.C. Green. The defendant in court. caused much annoyance to the magistrates by her obstinacy in answering the questions. Mr. Browne said that as his client had not instructed him to plead not guilty, he should throw up his brief, but reserve the right to reply. The defendant would have to call her own witnesses.— Mr. Stewart complimented Mr. Browne upon the hide- pendent and highly professienal step he had taken in withdrawing from the case.—The defendant was fined 2s. 6d. for drunkenness.—In the cross-summons for assault, Wm. Houghton, Arthur Houghton, and othir witnesses were called. After a considerable amount ef time had been spent in hearing the case, which was con- siderably delayed by the dettit..iry manner in which the complainant gave her evidence, the charge against the constable Was dismissed, the Bench expressing their conviction that he had simply done his duty.—Mr. Stewart applied for costs.—Mr. Browne obje,cted to this, on the grounds that the costs were never allowed except in special cases. Costs mere eventually allowed by the I Bench.</p>
