        <p>SAD FATALITY AT THE WARRINGTON BATHS. TUE INQUEST.</p>
        <p>Mr.S. Brighouse.county coroner,lield an inquest at the White Dart Inn, on Tuesday, on the body of William Arthur Jeffery, who as drowned in the ‘Varrington Baths, on the previous day.—The Town Clerk (Mr. J. Lyon Whittle) and Mr. Stanley Wilson (deputy Town Clerk) represented the Cor- poration, and there were also present Councillor Dr.Cannell, Mr. T. Longdin (borough surveyor), Mr. Tuft, Dr. Starkey Smith, and Mr. William Jeffery (father of the deceased). Willirn Jeffry §aid tid ana wal 22 yet a of:age. : and resided at iiis housC at 121, Church-st. ITs Vl' a Ifitter by trade. Witness last saw his son alive at 520 p.m. on Monday, just before he left to go to the baths. Deceased had always enjoyed good health, and never had any sicknrss. He was not a good swimmer,and had only learned during the last month, since the new bath was opened. Before going to the baths, he drank half a cup of tea and had a piece of bread, which was his custom ; he intended to have tea at seven o'clock. He was rather hurried on the Monday night. He had gone to the baths almost every night. Frank Williamson, Thelwall Vicarage, said that he was at the Warrington baths, on the previous night. He saw deceased out of the water before the accident oc- curred, but did not see him go into the water. Witness was talking to Fairfield Turner at the three feet end of the bath, whets a boy drew his attention to deceased in the water. Deceased did not appear to be in a natural position, but lay on his side with his face downward. Witness jumped into the water and pulled deceased out, and with the assistance of Turner got him on the side. tie was close to the three feet end when witness saw him in the water. When pulled out Dr. Guest Gornall and Dr. Starkey Smith were immediately on the spot, and did all in their power to restore animation. Fairfield Turner gave corroborative evidence. He did not see deceased before he went into the bath. It was about 6 15 p.m. when deceased was pulled out. Dr. Starkey Smith said that he was the family medi- cal man. He had never attended deceased at all, and had never known him to have been ill. He was a well- developed, muscular, man. Witness was called into the baths about 6 25 on Monday to the deceased, and even after deceased appeared to be dead he and Dr. Gornall kept at work to restore animation, as there were some leases when a man who appeared to be dead had been restored. The cause of death was inexplicable. No one , saw deceased get into the water. The general opinion I seemed to be that he dived in at the deep end, ax d that Ihe tell on his stomach, which caused a sudden shock to the nervous system, particularly to the sympathetic nerves behind the stomach. This was probably the cause of death. Harry Clayton, who had been sent for from Messrs. Crosfield's Soap Wcrks, said that he had known de- ceased for seven years. Witness was in the baths when deceased came in on Monday night, and spoke to him as he came along the side. He seemed to be in his usual condition. Witness saw him jump off the lower board of the seven feet end into the water, and swim to the other end and then get out. He then plunged into the water again from the seven feet end, and again swam across apparently all right. He got to the three feet end again, and then stood up. This was about five minutes before the alarm was raised. Witness did not think that he would have time to go back to the seven feet end again. Deceased had a very awkward way of diving, often falling flat on his stomach. The last dive ! that witness saw him make was a very awkward one indeed, although he appeared to swim all right after- wards. The Coroner said that that was all the evidence the police had to put before the jury to arrive at the con- clusion as to how deceased came by his death. It was a very unfortunate occurrence, and outside his legal duty he would express his and the jury's sorrow that the father had sustained the loss of his son under such sad circumstances. The jury had a legal duty to perform— to find how death came about. There was nothing to show that there were any suspicious circumstances. The Warrington Corporation had very properly provided the baths for the inhabitants. He had been to inspect it, and he was quite pleased to see such an establish- ment, which was provided with everything that was proper and useful. The deceased seemed to have been very fond of bathing, he having just learned to swim. The evidence of the last witness and Dr. Smith showed that deceased had made a very awkward dive, falling on , his stomach, instead of going head first, which had caused shock to the nervous system. There was no blame attached to anybody nor any suggestion of blame. Tlie jury returned a verdict of " Accidental death." The Coroner, addressing the father, begged him kindly tc accept their sympathy in the loss he had sustained from the death of his son. The Town Clerk also said that the Corporation sympa- thised very much, and they deeply regretted that this death had occurred.</p>
        <p>The deceased was the youngest son of Mr. William Jeffery,head manager for Messrs. Rylands Bros. ,Limited. He was a very promising young man, and had been a diligent student for several years. Ile attended four science classes last winter, and since his death, word has been received that he had passed second in applied mechanics (advanced). Be .waS exceedingly popular wherever he went, and was looking forward to a happy marriage in the early future., THE FUNERAL. The funeral of thedeceased took place at the cemetery on Thursday afternoon. The cortege arrived at the resi- dence of the deceased at 2.45 from the mortuary, and a procession was then formed consisting of the fellow workmen in the engineerinc, department of Messrs. Rylands Bros. Ltd,, followed 'by about forty members of the Young Men's Christian Endeavour Class, and the 2nd class males of Wycliffe Sunday school who preceded the hearse. The chief mourners which excupied-the mourning coaches were Mr. William Jeffery, Miss McLarty, Mrs. P. T. Winskill., Mr. .3. W. Jeffery, Mr. S. W. Jeffery, Miss Ethel Jeffery, Miss Edith .Jeffery, Mr. P. T. Winskill (brother-in-law), Mrs. 5. W. Jeffery, Mrs. S. W. Jeffery, Mr. Joseph Whitfield, Mr. T. P. W inskill {Liverpool), Miss ICate McLarty, Miss E. McLarty, Miss Whalley, and Mr. D. McLarty. The bearers were Messrs. H. Clayton, F. A. Rathbonv, F. Booth, J. Knowles, R. Sanson„ and C. F. Taylor. The following were also present Dr. T. Starkey Smith. Messrs. J. Haynes., G. Wallington, K. Wyllie, E. Yreka- vance, R. Atkinson, and others. The coffin which was of polished oak with brass mountings bore on the plate the inscription" William Arthur jeffery, died. June 26th. 1893, aged 22 years." The service at the cemetery was conducted by the Rev. J. Yonge, assisted by the Rev. J. Warnock, M.A., B.D. The following sent floral tributes The family, Miss Maggy McLarty, fellow-workmen in the engineering department of Messrs Rylands Bros., Lel., Mr. and Mrs. Wm. Richardson, Mr. E. lichavance, Mr. and Mrs. W. IL Hurst, Mr. and Mrs. Ed ward Nash, Mrs. and Miss Gibson, Mr. T. Woods, Miss Whalley, Mr. F. A. Rathbohe, Young Men of Wycliffe. Mr. and Mrs. McLarty, Miss E. McLarty, Mr. Harry Clayton. and Mrs. Brundrett- and family. Mr. John John Mourdield had charge of the funeral.arrangeinents.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY; JULY 1, 1893.''</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY; JULY 1, 1893.''</p>
        <p>THE GREAT SHIPWRECK.</p>
        <p>THRILLING SCENES.</p>
        <p>THE BRAVERY AND DISCIPLINE OF THE</p>
        <p>SAILORS. The following telegram from Port Said, supplied by an officer of the Barham, was published on Tues- day night "The crusher Barham was a short distance from the Victoria when the accident occurred, and I was standing well forward with a glass in my hand at the moment. The evolutions of the fleet had been going on for some titian, and had been up to that instant succes•fally and brilliantly conducted. The' gridiron " manceuvre had been clone, and was being repeated at the time my atten- tion was centred on the double line of YeaSele. "When the signals was given for the vessels to change over into each other's former position, the Camperdown swung entirely round, and then it seemed to me that she either lost control of her rudder or that the Victoria swerved slightly out of her proper course, because an instant previous to their coming together there was a noticeable distance between her, and a small patch of clear water. I had just put the glass to my eye, being desirous of witnessing again the graceful movement of the fleet in its transposition, when the two vessels upon which my attention was naturally centred, as holding the head of the respective lines, came together with a tremendous crash that for a second caused each to re- main motionless on the water, and then a shiver passed through both the enormous sbips,and they slowly backed away from each other through the force of the impact. " I could not appreciate, even after what I had just seen, the terrible significance of the situation until the Victoria swung round so that her damaged broadside was presented to our ship, and I realised the certainty of the fate that came five minutes later. It seemed to me that the change in the position of the Victoria after she was struck resulted trona the tremendous force of the blow she had received. The collision and the result up to this stage had not occupied more than a single minute, but to my mind, which now grasped the fright- ful danger that threatened, it seemed an eternity. " Before the Victoria had fully exposed her side to the Point where I was standing,her men had commenced to spread the collision mat over the gaping hole in her side and I could clearly distinguish Vice Admiral Tryon giv- ing orders, apparently as calmly as he had done a quar- ter of an hour before, and the crew working with the same discipline that would have prevailed in the quietest weather and under the safest conditions. "In another minute the Admiral aptly realised the futility of the efforts to keep the water out of the vessel, and I could tell by the action of the men on deck that he had ordered them to save themselves in any way they could. " Signals had been run up on the doomed vessel call- ing for aid from the rest of the fleet, and as quickly as possible boats were lowered, and the entire squadron seemed to realise at once what was passing before their eyes. But events now happened with awful rapidity, and men could be seen leaping from the side of the Victoria into the ocean, and swimming away. In fact it was only those who sprang away from the vessel almost at the first alarm, certainly at the very first order of the Admiral to do so, that were saved. The magnific- I ent discipline and the loyalty of the crew prompted many for the first time in their naval life to disobey the order of the Admiral and remain with their brave com- mander on the deck. Unhappily those who delayed in that way were sucked down by the terrible whirlpoel made by the vessel when she turned over." AN AMERICAN ACCOUNT.</p>
        <p>NEW YORK., TUESDAY.—The "Evening World" issues to-day a special edition giving a telegraphic despatch front Tripoli with an account of the wreck of the Victoria. The despatch says, after describing the manceuvres and collision :—The discipline of the crew on board the vessel was perfect. The Vice-Admiral re- mained upon the bridge until the last moment. Five minutes after the collision a diving suit was brought on deck, and a diver hastily put it on and attempted to go over the side of the ship to ascertain the extent of the injury. At the same instant the head of the ship was turned towards the shore, and in the ten minutes that intervened between the delivery of the blow by the Camperdown and the sinking of the Victoria the latter steamed two miles towards land. Suddenly, with a great roll and a wild plunge, the enormous vessel buried her bow beneath the surface of the sea. There were a few wild cries, the ship trembles, and then turned over with her keel high in the air, her screws rapidly whirl- ing. Those en deck leaped into the sea as the vessel careened ; those below had no opportunity to close the doors or to reach the open, and thus they perished. Commander Jellico was lying in his berth sick with fever, but he rushed on deck in his pyjamas and sprang overboard. A lieutenant, also struggling in the waves, put his arm around Jellicoe and thus enabled him to get away from the rapidly sinking ship. Presently the huge hull turned completely over. Several hundred men fully dressed were thrown off the deck as the Victoria careened and were sucked down into the hole made in the sea by</p>
        <p>the disappearing vessel. Another horror was aided. The powerful engine deep down in the water-tight compartment kept throbbing and working, and the formidable steel flanges of the twine screw whirled round and round at first high up in space, and then gradually came nearer and nearer to the surface of the water until they descended in the midst of the mass of human beings struggling for life, And then as it disappeared the suction increased until it became a perfect maelstrom, at the bottom of which these deadly screws were moving like circular knives, gashing and killing the poor creatures who had battled vainly for life. Then Came the scene that caused the officers on the decks of the remaining vessels of the fleet to turn sick. ' Shrieks were heard, and then .the waves and the foam were reddened by the blood, of the victims. All within reach of this great vortex dew seemed to lose their pre- sence of mind. Men who knew how to swim ceased swimming. They clutched at each other in their frenzy. When the ship had disappeared there came a muffled sound as of thunder. The water was tossed up, and a cloud of steam burst forth, sure signs that the boilers had exploded. Boats were soon on the scene and picked up those who had succeeded in keeping away from the suction of the sinking vessel. Several of the rescued were scalded by the water from the boilers. Admiral Tryon was seen to the very last on the bridge, and the diver, fully dressed except his helmet, stood on the deck, and would have been dragged to the bottom by his heavy leaden shoes had it not been for a seaman who stopped to cut him loose, and lost his own life in consequence.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>LATE MR. PATERSON.</head>
        <p>THE LATE MR. PATERSON.</p>
        <p>MEMORIAL SERVICE.</p>
        <p>A nvmaorial sermon on the late Mr. Paterson was preached at St. John's Presbyterian Church, Win- wick-st., on Sunday morning by the Rev. nook, B.D. Previous to the service the organist (Mr. Wadsworth) played I know that my Redeemer livetlC" Mr. Warnock took as , his text Hebrews Ile. I.ov. Referring to Mr. Paterson he said that this was the first time since he became the minister at St. John's, that one of the office bearers had I been removed by death. It was peoper that 'notice should be taken of his removal from amongst them. Their knowledge of each other was fragmentary at the best, but anyone who knew Mr. Paterson personally, would confess that he had many excellencies. His (the preacher's) intercourse with him had always been of the mcst agreeable and profitable character. Of Mr. hater. son's public career, tie did not feel himself competent to speak. It was very creditable to himself, and lie believed he was incapable of being an unfaithful servant He had known several men who had worked under Mr Paterson when he was . manager of the Gan Works., and they all bore witness of his invariable kindness to them. For the encouragement orally pining people present,it was worth remembering that Mr. Paterson's success was his devotion to his industry. Scotland had ever done well , for her children in this respect, and she had been amply rewarded for her pains by the distinguished career of S.(' many of her sons. One of their deceased friend's char- , acteristles was genuine piety. lie was always very generous, and was ever ready to give when occasion required. He may Wye made mistakes in giving, as would happen to those so disposed, but to many deserv- iny, people he was a true benefactor. Ile was no p,lorer for all he gave away : " There is that seattereth and yet I increaseth." Their friend's departure was altogether of a peaceful character. Very different in this respect owas that of the 400 or thereabouts of brave men on the Mediterranean flagship, the tidings of whose loss had just come to hand. Their dear friend Mr. Paterson had lived more years than the Psalmist gave as the usual limit of man's life. Ile lived to see his children's child- ren rise up before him. But death to the Christian was not loss, but great calm It was the putting off of what was corruptible arid putting on incorrtiption„ arid putt- ing MT what was mortal and ,putting on immortality.— At Ow close of the service the choir sang Pope's" Vital Spark." ,</p>
      </div>
