      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>BRITISH WOMEN'S TEMPERANCE</head>
        <p>BRITISH WOMEN'S TEMPERANCE</p>
        <p>ASSOCIATIOS.</p>
        <p>FORMATION OF A WARRINGTON BRANCH. A meeting of ladies was held in the Bold- street schoolroom, on Wednesday afternoon, to consider the advisability ,of forming a local 1 branch of the British Women's Temperance Association. Mrs. Mather and Miss Finney. of Manchester, and Mrs. Slii*on, of Liverpool, at- tended to give any information required. Among other ladies present were Miss Dutton, Mrs. Tonge, Mrs. Gunnell, Mrs. Kiitlan, Mrs, Asliton,Miss Kerry, Miss Bradbury, Mrs. Reid. Mrs. Ruff, and Miss Ben- nett. Miss Bessie Dutton was voted to the chair, and after opening the meeting with prayer, MY. Mather was called upon to explain the objects of the Association. She said that its object was by work, prayer, :example. and personal .intinence, to reform the intemperate, and by evrry legitimate means to seek the removal of the causes which led to Intemper- ance, and to encourage those of a counter-acting ten- dency ; to Strive to amend the drinking customs of society, especially among women, and induce all people to abandon the use of alcoholic beverages. Any one who joined must pay a subscription of not less than Is. per annum.—Mrs. Shilston howe4 the importance of forming a branch, as if the Local Control Bill passes, women would have votes, and they must be taught how to ass them on the side of temporatice.—After some further discussion the.following officers were chosen ; I President, Mrs. Garnett; vice-presidents. Miss B. Button I arid Mrs. Yonge ; secretarifs, Mrs. Gunnell and Miss I Bradbury ; treasurer, Mrs. Starkey Smith ; committee, Mrs.l‘rt I IRoberts,i‘s ir. Ahton, Miss ree (iMiss"K(eßrravilw,May issli Bennett, Mrs. Hoff. Mrs. McNaught and Mrs.- IVright.—Miss FinneV expressed her pleasure that a branch had been formed at WA rrington. There was plenty of work to do. They should make a point of personally visiting those whom they had persuaded to sign the pledge, as in this way they would help to keep them in the right way.. She then proceeded to give her experiences as visitor for the ANsociation in Manchester, and concluded by wishing the newly-formed branch in Warrington every success. 7-It was decided to hold another meeting shortly to he. gm the work in earnest. The meeting closed With the pronouncing of the benediction.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>'ROYAL WEDDING.</head>
        <p>THE 'ROYAL WEDDING.</p>
