      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>WHITSUNTIDE FLIGEIT</head>
        <p>A WHITSUNTIDE FLIGEIT</p>
        <p>WITH THE WOODCOCK.</p>
        <p>(BY MR. JOHN PEERS.)</p>
        <p>But now the strong N.W. wind and setting sun give chill Monition of approaching night, the Parade is less paraded, the niggers have ceased to crack their ever- green jokes—the bones have ceased to rattle and the harp is encased—the black watch have gone to fight the battle of life where the melee is thickest. And even the little weilders of spade and bucket are tired of working imagination into arenaceous shapes, and hasten to their lodgings—their shoes and their minds alike full of sand, and to bed to dream gritty dreams. So we too, like- minded minus the sand, had lodgings, which now we were fain to seek. The station was crowded with home- seeking " outists." Nevertheless Tommy and I were the only Sasenach in the compartment and Cymraeg was in full swing. Tommy confided to me, afterwards, his fear that these good people might have been anathema- tising him for anything he knew. If so we, like the fam- ous Jackdaw, were not " one penny the worse." Very pleasant was the walk from Prestatyn to Tan Lan. Albeit as the night advanced the rain came in showers. A mild moist night. As we climbed the higher parts of the road we heard, borne upon the sea, the mournful "tang " of the Dee buoy, while every now and again we could see the flash of the Dee light.</p>
        <p>Just before reaching Tan Lan, my companion ex- claimed" Hillo, what's this" ! pointing to a light in a small hollow by the road side. Stepping down to it, I found it to be a lambent, topaz yellow glow proceeding from among the herbage. Secur- ing it we found it to be the glow worm. Nature has such ways. Here is this insect, which by daylight is only a "nasty creeping thing," a "horrid in- sect" fit only for extermination by the foot—by Nature's wonderworking hand converted into "a thing of beauty" if not "a joy for ever." This lowly and repulsive-look- ing insect, in the dark, by her love lit lamp makes all bright around her by the mild effulgence of her glow, where the gaudiest butterfly of the Brazils would be unnoticed and unknown. With a little snow, a little hoar frost, or a sprinkling of dew, the same magician converts the commonest objects into bejewelled decora- tions of exquisite beauty. It was near midnight as we turned down the long tree o'er topped road to the stage, where we arrived, after stumbling over trolley tracks and sleepers, among coal wagons, shunts, and turn tables on the hither verge of another day. We arrived to find the tide out, everything aground, and our ship far, far down in the abysmal blackness of the gutter, ' and with no obvious means of access. The situation was interesting.</p>
        <p>The monotony of domestic routine—the clock-ruled regularity of bed and board—the tyranny of domestic habits—was here broken and set at nought. There, someivliere, down in the dogmouth-darkness of that rippling watercourse,lay.our lodgings. Here,hlgh above these too-too lowly lodgings,stood two belated voyagers casting longing, lingering, looks below. Talk of being locked out—of chasing the latchkey hole round the door with the incidental difficulty of deciding which was not the hinge side of the aperture—these little troubles pale before the darkling trouble of our plight. But help was at hand. Two other belated ones turned up who sug- gested the use of a long ladder,which they, being better acquainted, knew to be about. Down this upon the deck of one intervening flat, and over another, and we were at home. Rough and ready was the accommodation we met with. The "list" she had taken had deluged Tom's bedroom, so lie took the grating forward and slept the dreamless sleep of youth and fatigue. This profoundly peaceful sleep was destined to a rude awakening. The "Gutter" justified its name, and startled us out of our usual matutinal, leisurely arising by being " sluiced." This is done every tide at law water, by letting the impounded water out of a pond or dam through the sluice gate, in order to keep the gnu,. open. Beyond t he foundering of the punt by the sudden ru sh,no damage was received, so we turned in again. The "we" in this case is authoresque. Tommy and bed are inseparable upon such slight prevocation. Nothing short of actual foundering would make hint forsake his snug for peak berth. - But the colliery whistle announces the advent of another Norking day, and soon the trolleys were busy bringing coal from the pit and emptying themselves,by a comical process of kicking the hind wheels into the air, down the Shunts, into the hold of the fiat below, with much noise and clatter. We were told that the workings ran far tinder the wild waters of the restless Dee, out under the lightship so they said. This submarine groping for this precious mineral suggests some curious thoughts. The coal these Welshmen are following, beneath the salt water of Wild. road was once the surface and gladdened by the beam of a more youthful sun. It is the result of ark abund- antly luxurious low class vegetation—probably tresh water marsh-growing plants. Such are the mutations of terrestrial life. The solid earth upon which we tread live, move, and have our being si.m, not very long since —geologically speaking—the bottom of the sea. By the see-saw of nature what is up was then down—and conversely what is now down may be up in a while. Even now some future contknents may be forming at the bottom of the sea., in which the wrecks and relics of man and his handiwork will be found embedded. For the" making "of the world is not yet finished.</p>
        <p>Now this early morning broke out fine but with fre- quent squalls beating down from the hills above us which sang and whistled in the rigging of the craft around us. Thinking the afternoon's tide would offer a quieter passage across to Hilbre we decided to drop down the guiteram the morning's ebb and wait for the new tide. When the boat grounded, and just ere the punt did so, we took the latter and ran down to the mouth of this gutter to get to Point of Air by walking along the sands. And a glorious walk it was ; against a stiff west wind that blew theloose sand along in white sheets, pricking the bare feet like pin points by tke force of their impact. Bright, breezy, and refreshing—the strong wind tempered by a kindly sun—this little expe- dition was most enjoyable. The great mounds of sea- washed and wind-blown sands told their own tale of having been storm-bourn hitherwarct from the sea. The little red-banded lighthouse and, what we took to be the keeper's house, now deserted, desolate and dilapi- dated, had a very sea-worn appearance. Evidently they come in for their share of Ne.ptunic buffeting. The sands here are covered with sand and sea loving plants some of them amid and inhospitably aggressive to the bare-footed stranger_ After a carefully stepped tour of inspection we turned cur backs upon the wind and, chased by the still drifting sand, made our way to the punt. This we found had been left " high " if not "dry "upon the soft mud. Through the knee-deep ooze we slid the little craft down to water and tben—waited for the tine. As yet there was no sign of this. The Park-gate shrimpers were busy trawling, with greatly reduced sails as befitted the freshened breeze. In pur- suit of the same brisk crustaceans two ancient dam( s, whose hot too redundent costume allowed a liberal dis- play of sturdy nether limbs, were " trudging" their nets in the gutter. But even these nineteenth century Naiads Of the stream are preparing to desert us ; shaking their leaping prey out of the net into their creels,they shoulder their poles, and with dripping garments fluttering in the breeze they trudge, in picturesque raggedness, across the long stretch of sand to Mostyn. Surely it any of us deserve our earnings such as these ate they ! And now the Dee ship has swung to the incoming tide, the punt is afloat in the gutter and we are glad to be creeping towards our better furnished ship. It is long past Gin- ner time and our breakfasts long since things of the past. Now the tardy tide did not keep pace with our pre- p, andial desires—s we agreed—my mate and I to separate, he to walk up to the Woodcock, make a fire and cook the chops, while I brought up the lagging punt appetite prevailing over on top of the flood. lint, patiqnc,.. I made the punt fast and trudged over the mud to where nay friend was making meet savours—"TaiMing the gale " with perfumes, by me preferred to those of "Araby the blest." With N chop and a chunk of bread—WiAl not even chop-sticks to manipulate it—l returned to my charge munching these 6irnple viands with appreciative relish. Truly I have pad more for a less satisfactory meal. When the tide permitted I rowed the poet up to the boat and finished, in peace and satisfactiion, this migratory meal. And then Tom in the fulness of his satisfaction, gave vent to his hilarity by lifting up his I voice and vocally detailing the adventures of the soliva- gant benedict who rciaced so that " They are all at home." Here. where none but the whistling 'curlews on the beach can tie ei,turbect by the slentorian cliantings of my jolly mate—or may hap the fleeting wild ducks</p>
        <p>It cures BOILS CUTS BRUISES PILES nzEMA LUMBACO RHEUMATISM STIFF JOINTS SPRAINS WOUNDS SORES</p>
        <p>And Subdues all</p>
        <p>INFLAMMATION</p>
        <p>at once.</p>
        <p>impelled to quicker fight—here is the place—where neighbours are so distant and so few—to bring the amateur vocalist, who here can rend the welkin with his notes, unheeded, unchecked, and unchid. While waiting for the tide to have risen sufficient for our start, the punt of the flat " Chili "passed up the gutter and hailed us as an acquaintance, she having lately been up our river, at Atherten's Quay. It was getting late in the afternoon when we started, and having run down the perch-marked gutter and past its black buoy we stood over towards the red-rocked island, its white houses gleaming in the declining sun. The fresh breeze had raised a long swell, nothing to inconvenience us, especially as we were going in its direclion. It was a grand sail ; bounding over those green sees—the little punt racing after us with alternately tightened painter, and then as the seas caught her, rushing after us with the rope's slack in the water, only to be brought taut again with a gurge, that flung the sparkling spray from its dripping strands. My vocal mate did not sing dur- ing our run across—never does under these circum- stances. Whether he is afraid that the pitching of the boat might interfere with the pitch of his voice— whether he is modest and declines to compete with the music of the singing rigging and the breaking waves, or, mayhap he is too much engrossed In trying to make out " %6'hat the wild waves are saying." Be this it as will the fact remains—when the elements are 'piping their wild refrain Tommy is not leader of the chorus— but, with me, takes a back seat, as a delighted listener. But now indeed, with a leading wind, the " girls at home have got hold of the tow-rope," and merrily they are pulling. And the seas get bigger as we leave the sheltering Welsh side. Gaily over these we bound in futile chase of a pilot schooner which has been making the cireuit of West Hoyle Bank, and is now outside it standing to the West. Leaving her to her duties of guidance and guardianship, we dashed through the troubled waters off the Life boat house and into the quieter haven behind the Island. Over the pleasant heave of the rounded swells which gradually lessened as we neared the Red-rocks at the head of the Lake we again threaded the intricacies of the water-shed and, by following our old sailing directions, got through with bare water.</p>
        <p>On the lake we found preparations for a race being made—great display of bunting—enthusiasm afloat and ashore. So we brought up near our old berth and lent the competitors our eyes, that being the only way in which we could contribute to the festivities of the occa- sion. We had no flag to hoist—except a coloured handkerchief, and that had seen better days and might have been mistaken for a flag of distress and a sign of long departed washing day—beside this, we had no sig- nal hilliards. What we could do, however, we did. We raised a smoke, a steam, and certain culinary savours appropriate ia the hour. And then Tom indulged in vocal anticipation as to " The day we were going to have to-morrow." It was a nice sight to see the little white sailed craft—of the Woodcock type, but much more speedy but less stable—to see them flitting and hopping about like water fleas upon the moderate "lop" in the lake. They were little more than model yachts, requiring 'life belts and baling. Fond as lain of salt water,l have a prejudice in favour of its surface. I prefer being on the top rather than going through it. I However, it was a beautiful sight, illuminated by a declining sun, I have no doubt it made a pretty picture in the camera of the man on the beach. I rowed my mate ashore for the replenishment of the water keg and the bread locker--he, likn the good fellow he is, cheer- fully volunteering for shore service of this kind. Bare- footed and barelegged, with shouldered keg he awoke the sleeping echoes of the beach by telling how " We carved our way to glory." Next morning we ran out of the lake before a fresh westerly wind. In the Rock Chan- nel we found lumpy seas running, but as they were run- ning with us, we rose superior to them and parodied their crested pride by an emulous surging at our bows. Thus we ran into the river and took up our old berth at New Ferry. From here we started on the evening's tide of Friday homeward bound. As the shades of evening e closing around us, my musical mate having landed at Fiddler's Ferry, beaded upwards by himself im- pelled by the lessening breeze. With mutual congratu- lations as to the pleasures of the trip, we parted, he for the Quays—l for the latch key, each with the captive cockles in his charge.</p>
        <p>I were I IYIP</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>ANOTHER EIRE AT THE BOTTLE</head>
        <p>ANOTHER EIRE AT THE BOTTLE</p>
        <p>WORKS.</p>
        <p>Some excitement and alarm was occasioned on Monday, when it became known that another fire had broken out at the Glass Bottle Works of Messrs. Borron and Co., Newton. Only about a fortnight had elapsed since the last five, which destroyed one ot the warehouses took place, and much surprise was expressed at another outbreak occurring so soon. About two o'clock on Monday afternoon, some boys who were playing in a field adjoining the large packing ware- house, which runs en the outside of the works, observed smoke issuing from one corner of the packing ware- house, and at once raised an alarm. A large quantity of straw was stored in the warehouse for the purpose of packing bottles for export, and the gravest fears were at once entertained for the safety of the store. Within a few minutes of the alarm being given, a dense volume of smoke ascended, and it was at once seen that the fire had got a good bold of the straw. Word was at on e sent to the Viaduct Works, and to Messrs. McCorquor- dale and Co., for their respective fire brigades, and the former was on the scene about 2 25, the latter followine half-an-hour later. The building was then one mass cf flame, and the efforts of the brigades were directed to saving the large warehouse. The connection ‘between the two was cut off, and a good supply of water was poured on the flames. In a few minutes after the , outbreak the roof fell in, and igniting the floor, ' this also gave way soon af er, the inside of the building being then a seething mass of flame. The fire brigades under the captaincy of Mr. T. Baxter and Mr. A. Chisholm respectively worked very Lard but they were considerably hampered for want of water. Many willing hands rendered valuable assistance by pouring buckets of wat,r, wherever needed, but it was not until about six o'clock that the flames were fairly got under. The brigades, however, continued to play on the burning mass until eight o'clock, when it was considered that all danger was at an end. Inspector Kelly, Sergeant Longworth and a staff of police, greatly assisted operations by keeping' curious crowds from intezfering with the work of the fireman. The damage if 4 roughly estitnated at £15,000, which is I covered hy insurance.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>SHOCKING AFFAIR AT PORTSMOUTH.</head>
        <p>SHOCKING AFFAIR AT PORTSMOUTH.</p>
        <p>A SERGEANT IHOT DEAD. A shocking tragedy took pl ee at Portsmouth on Tues- day, the scene being Fiilsea Itiflir Range. It is stated that Private Mason of the East Surrey Regiment, had been reported by .9ergeant Robinson for regimental irregularity and sentenced to three days' confinement to barracks. The effect of this punishment would have been that between Tuesday and Thursday accused would have had two hours' extra drill a day, would have hid to answer to his name as a defaulter, and would not be allowed away from the battalion. The men were on their way to the rifle ranges after the orderly room charges had been disposed of. On the line of march Mason is alleged to have declared that he would have his revenge upon Robinson. During target practice Mason was standing a few yards behind Sergeant Robinson, and just wile.' the latter was firing at the target Mason raised his rifle and shot the sergeant through the back, the bullet penetrating the heart. Mason was at once arrested and handed over to the civil authorities.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>SIR G. TRYON'S SUCCESSOR.</head>
        <p>SIR G. TRYON'S SUCCESSOR.</p>
        <p>Sir Michael Cuirne-Seymour has been arppinted corn- mander-in-chief on the Mediterranean stxtioo, in *occa- sion to the late Sir George Tryon.</p>
        <p>BREACH OF PROMISE.</p>
        <p>DAMAGES £2,000.</p>
        <p>On Thursday morning, before' Lord Coleridge, the ease of Sainways versus Winch, which was an action for breach of promise of marriage, was called on. Mr. Lionel Hart, counsel for the defendant, said his client was anxious to consent to judgment for the wln.le amount claimed, viz., £2,000. A verdict was given a ccordinolv and judgment for the amount of damages. Partieuar; of the action did not transpire.</p>
        <p>IMPERIAL PARLIAM EN I'.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>MONDAY.</head>
        <p>MONDAY.</p>
        <p>In the Rouse of Commons on Monday evening, on the London rovements Bill coming up for considera- tion Mr. E. IL HOARE moved the omission of the "betterment" clause. The minion was defeated by 216 to 118. Ma. _</p>
        <p>-- Mr. GUI:MONS informed the House of the decision of</p>
        <p>the Indian Governme-n-t—oTth-;-C;;;Wc; question. The House afterwards went into Committee of Supply on the navy estimates.</p>
        <p>TUESDAY.</p>
        <p>In the House of Commons on Tuesday evening, on the consideration of the Weaver Navigation Bill, an amend- ment was agreed to, to enable Parliament to deal with the whole question of the constitution and management of the Trust, and the bill was ordered for third reading. Mr. VINCENT put several more questions about the de- sireableness of making the Royal wedding-day a public holiday, and was informed by the Chancellor of the Ex- CHEQUER that the wage-earning classes were decidedly opposed to such a course, and that the Government saw no reason to alter the alecision they had arrived at. Sir E. GREY stated that a further report in reference to the trials at Angora had been received from Mr. New- ton, which gave it as his opinion that the evidence was quiteinsufficient to justify the sentence passed on M. Thoumaian and M. Kayayan : and there was every rea- son to hope that the Sultan would exercise the preroga- tive of mercy without delay. The House went into Committee of Supply on the Army Estimates.</p>
        <p>WhDNESDAY. In the House of Commons on Wednesday Mr. GLAD- STONE, answering a question put by Mr. Goschen, said the power of the Indian Government to purchase silver and coin rupees whenever they thought fit was not altered. The House again went into Committee on the Home Rule Bill, taking up the consideration of dame 5, relat- ing to the executive power in Ireland. Two amendments were divided upon, and were rejected by majorities of 31 and 29. Mr.GLADSTONE announced that he would Thursday move a resolution for the purpose of expediting the progress of the bill. Subsequently Mr. MORLEY stated that the resolution would propose that the proceedings on the bill should be brought to a close in the following man- ner :—Clauses 5 to 8 not later than 10 p.m. on Thursday July 8 ; clauses 9 to 28 not later than 10 p.m. on Thurs- day, July 13 ; clauses 27 to 40 not later than 10 p.m. on Thursday, July 20 ; and the postponed clauses and new clauses, being Government clauses, schedules, and preamble, not later than 10 p.m. on Thursday. July 27. After the clauses were disposed of, the chairman would forthwith report the bill as amended to the House, and after the passing of this order no dilatory motion would be received unless moved by a Minister in charge of the bill. Proceedings under this order would not be inter- rupted under any standing order.</p>
        <p>THURSDAY In the House of Commons on Thursday evening, Mr. ASQUITH, replying to a question as to the railway insur- ance societies, said it had been decided to prohibit con- tracting out of the Employers' Liability Act, and to that decision the Government intended to adhere. Sir E. GREY informed Mr. F. Stevenson that instruc- tions had been sent to Her Majesty's Charge de Affaires at Constantinople as to the language he should hold with reference to the condemned Armenians. It was not ad- visable in the interest of the prisoners to say more. The hon. Baronet, replying to other questions, stated the position of the Franco-Siamese dispute so far as it was known to Her Majesty's Government, and said the !French Government had given Her Majesty's Govern- ment an assurance that they should receive notice of any orders given to the French fleet. Mr. GLADSTONE informed Mr. EVERETT that neither Lord Haftscnett's Committee nor the Indian Govern- ment were under the impression at present that there was likely to be any serious fall in the value of Indian produce. Mr. VINCENT moved the adjournment of the House in order to call attention to the dangers which, he said, would result from the carrying on of banking operations on the day of the Royal wedding. Themotion was opposed by the HOME SECRETARY, and was rejected by 297 to 254. Mr. Gladstone then moved his resolution for the closure at specified times of debate on the several por- tions of the Home Rule Bill. He stated that the Govern ment would not prorogue till the bill had gone through its stages, and that they intended to deal with certain British measures within the present year. Mr. liaLroult spoke at some length in opposition to the motion, and Mr. T. %V. RUSSELL moved an amendment declining to sanction the Government's propesals. Mr. T. H. BOL- TON opposed the Premier's motion, and afterwards Mr. CHAMBERLAIN spoke in warm terms against it. On a division Mr. Russell's amendment was defeated by 306 to 279.</p>
        <p>In the Rouse of Lords the Sale of Intoxicating Liquor (Ireland) Bill passed the report stage. Questioned by Lord DUNIZATILN, Lord RIPON said he could not pledge the Government to any future action with regard to France and Newfoundland.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>A. THREATENED TOBACCO FAMINE.</head>
        <p>A. THREATENED TOBACCO FAMINE.</p>
        <p>PARIS THURSDAY—After the fodder famine brought on by the drought comes menace of a tobacco famine. Prices are going up at an unheard of rate, and the Government have laid a demand for a supplementary I credit of four million francs on the table of the chamlPer. The tobacco sales at the commencement of this inonth</p>
        <p>showed an increrise (415 to 20 per cent. in the prfce of tobacco compared with last year.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>DEFIANCE FROM UNDER THE BEDCLOTHES.</head>
        <p>DEFIANCE FROM UNDER THE BEDCLOTHES.</p>
        <p>Amongst the applicaats to the magistrate at the Mary- lebone Police Court on Wednesday was a lady who asked what she was to do with her servant girl. She would not do her work and would not get out of bed.—The Magistrate: Where is she now ?—The Applicant : In bed, and she won't dress herself.—The Magistrate: Have you paid her all the wages due to her P—Appllcant : I have, and I have dis- charged her, and she won't go. She remains in bed,and I can't get her to dress herself .—The Magistrate : If the facts are as you say, she is as much an intiuder as the greatest stranger, and you can hare her turned out.— Applicant : But I cau't turn her out Until she has dressed herself.—Th. Magistrate : Exactly. This is cer- tiinly a very strange application. You had better en- gage some strong woman to dress the girl, and then you can turn her cut.—The Applicant: I thank you. I'll try that. The girl has no relatives in London, but she has friends.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>MESSRS. lITORQUOIjALE AND UNIONIST PRINTERS.</head>
        <p>MESSRS. lITORQUOIjALE AND UNIONIST PRINTERS.</p>
        <p>Mr. Woods will ask the Secretary to the Treasurer in the House of Ceintrions, on Tuesday next, whether he is aware that between 21st March and 6th April, 1893, ten of the most prominent workmen were discharged by Mr. Wood, the manager of Messrs. M'Corquodale and Co.' printers, at Newton, and elsewhere, who do part of Cho printing of her Majesty's Government, because they had joined the typographical. trade union, and that a num- i:)er of others received their notice, which was withdrawn only when the men signed a compulsory agreement to sever their connection with the above society ; whether he is aware that at the other branches of the same firm in London, Leeds, Birmingham, alid Manclester, the men were allowed to join their trade union, and in con- sequence they work shorter hours and earn higher Grag's than at Newton, mid whether in view of these circum- stances it is the intention of the Government to take such steps as will give these men full liberty to join their trade union, or in the alternative they ce:tse to employ this firm in the future to do their work.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>COLLIERY PITBROW FIRE AT HAYDOCK.</head>
        <p>COLLIERY PITBROW FIRE AT HAYDOCK.</p>
        <p>A great coal stack, consisting of 10,000 or 10,000 tons at the Legh Pit, Ilaydock, near St. Helene, be- longing to Messrs. Richard Evans and Co., was on fire on Thursday'. Several days since smoke was observed to be issuing from the centre of the heap, haviug apparently been caused by combustion at the base. A large number of colliers and others have been engaged night and day removing the coal in boxes, over a hundred railway waggons had bt.en filled up to Thursday morning. The work of loading every available waggon continued throughout d front Thursday, amid the suffocating smoke which emanate. the heap. At a lee hour laAt night the centre of the stack was reached, but hundreds mere tons of slack wilt have to be moved before the place can be considered ae safe. The vat s'ze of the heap is accounted for be the aceunnilation o`'. coal in consequence of the hot season. and lad trade. The Legli Pit is this week stopped for the purpose of putting new engines and headgear on the brow. The old headgear and pulleys were removed on Thuriday, and the pit is expected to start work on Monday.</p>
        <p>- RUPTURE CURED.—S. J. SHERMAN, Hernia Specialist. 64, Chancery-lane, London, sends Lis book on rupture post free. Branch Chambers, 26, Ktng-,.t., Mancliester.-4dvt. It is announced that a London firm have received in- strmtions from Viscount Combermere to sell the Com- , bermere Abbey estate, consisting of about 9,000 acres, in II Cleshire and Shropshire.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>MEDICAL</head>
        <p>MEDICAL</p>
        <p>A CARD. PEVEREND JOSEPH. HOLMES' improv-cl ILI., Mexican Prescription, as discovered in the hot table- lands of Old Mexico, is a go tranteod cure to ad those wb tuffer from the errors and Indiserettons of youth, nel yeus weakness, phvsical exhaustion, and enrly decay. To intro- duce It into England, the Ikf •xioart Prescr,ption will be sent free of charge. upon receidt .f a self e.ddresied stsmpsd envelope. Only address: JO•:KPII 1/01.MES' it:llledy Bloomsbury Mansions„ Bbornsbury-sqLare, London, !lentlon this paper.</p>
