        <p>THE EXAMINEV.</p>
        <p>SATURDAY, JULY 1, 1893.</p>
        <p>TEE Government have at Dealing with Obstructio determined to deal n. with the undisguised ca,- of their opponents in Parliament. On Wean' Mr. S. Woons, the Labour and Liberal - for ILICO, asked Mr. GLADSTONE if he had sidered any practicable steps ta bring the aim-i of the Horne Rule Bill to a close reasonable time, so that English, Scotch. Welsh business might be proceeded with' answer to tkis question,Mr.GrAnsrotin said Or intended to propose a resolution which wc the effect of expediting the progress of the d' and later on in the sitting the words of the tion were read out amidst the approving cf the Ministerialists. ' Briefly stated, the tion provides that all discussion on the clauses_ to the eighth must be closed by the sixth of and on all up to•the twenty-sixth by the thii of the month, and on all up to the fortieth---` is the last clause—by the twentieth. Another after that will be given for dealing with schedules and all new clauses, and completing, bill, so that the measure will pass the House of Commons on the twent: of July. This is giving a full month the debating of the Bill, and who can saY, sidering the length to which the discussion already been carried, that this is not amp/e for all honest and fair criticism ? The OPI will, doubtless, make the uttnost out of what will term a "tyrannical suppression of But, as a matter of fact, there are two stances which will take all the reality out °I grievances they may be pleased to formu/ate' the first place, in setting a limit to debate GLADSTONE is only following, with modific an example set by the late Mr. W. H. &amp;ran leader of the Tory party in the House of mons. In June of 1887 a Coercion Bill was through the House of Commons by the sr sion of all debate upon the clauses from twenty, end the Bill was reported forthwith.i that time Sir W. HARCOURT used language /I might now be regarded as almost prophetic. said "Perhaps the Government thought would always be a Tory party in office, al' supported by Liberal Unionists. That wig some foolish people who when they had got,a day thought it would never rain. But ' came over the political atmosphere. might come—he ventured to say it would in which there would be a Liberal supported by a democratic majority. they should ndt bP firwrY u re, lengths to which, for purposes of • the Tory party would catry the princil closure. .. He would paint a picture he was sure would edify the Chancellor ez Exchequer. He suggested to him a - future when there might boa Liberal Adtaie' tion numbering, say, 340; and there might Conservative party of 330. There might be great constitutional measure—perhaps Rule—and there might be a Minister stew the table, and saying I shall introduce a Home Rule Bill, and I shall accompany it declaration that the third reading shall be this day fortnight; the subject has boon for many years; the amendments you have down to the measure are frivolous; your resiL to it is obstructive; you are standing in the of a great reform ; you are opposing the la of the nation; it is our duty as a majority assert the rights of the nation, and to see Home Rule Bill is carried in a fortnight. might happen, and then they should 111 glorious example." Again, in August of lb- Parnell Commission Bill was forced through Commons by a similar summary process debate extending over three nights only. -It no doubt be argued that the cases of 1881 1888 are different from that of to-day. But differences there are, tell all in favour of the sent Government: As Mr. Gransromt SS or three weeks ago, there is no kind of leo' which requires to be more closely scan. thoroughly criticised than a Coercion Bill, WI is a measure for diminishing the liberty °I t subject. Whatever else may be said, it vain° asserted that the Homo Rule Bill Las that in view. And then, on the score Of 6 Mr. GLADSTONE is now allowing a of debate where Mr. SMITE only a week, and this month follows UP° discussion which has been Unprecedentedli tracted. Another circumstance which the grievances of the Opposition is the fast the debate is, according to their own decla. a sham debate, so far as they are concerned. have insolently declared, time after time, that amendments which they are so anxious to cl/' are not for tha purpose of improving the Bill from their own point of view. The Bill is WI they say, to be improved; it is only fit destroyed, and they will use their majority ill, House of Lords to destroy it, no matter how ' it may be discussed, and how much it tnsl', amended. For the Government to have all°' their own plans And the hopes of their foll°Yi in the country to be frustrated by an OpOsi which glories in and boasts of its obsti would have bean a regrettable display of ness. In their present action they will be ported by all who do not wish to see reduced to a state of impotency by the real abuse of its ordinary rules of procedure.</p>
        <p>• 11. seems that Whitechapel is n°t it Whitecha pel Vindicated. black as .it has been painted. has, at all events, found an genious champion in M. FICANCISQUE SABOICI, eminent French theatrical critic. M. SAROcI( 'heard a good deal about White Chapel. It . there that Jack the Ripper murdered his vie*.</p>
