      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <p>nd there—so he Igoe told—are hidden the most 1 Infamous lairs of robbery,and the most dismal dens •,ef nibierY and vice. At first, he was not very wil- ling to venture into the 'Whitchapel slums. But in the end, he allowed himself to be per- suaded, and due preparations were made for what 'was looked upon as a dangerous enterprise. . A Policeman—a big burly fellow, who could have knocked down an ox—was engaged as a private escort. First of all, the party went to a theatre, and here the disillusionment began.. The place Was crowded, but not with wild,unkempt, savages. The people appe aked to be dressed in their BtlndaY clothes, and they were laughing and enjoying the play in the most orderly manner. We didn't come to Whitechapel for this sort of thine said the Frenchmen,and they went out. The aide-walks outside were filled with a lively, noisy, laughing crowd, reminding the visitors of a Parisian thoroughfare on a fete day. The giant Policeman was informed that they came to White- chapel to see the slums, and they intended to see liei.4le. He took them some distance further and "cen:Waving his arm, pointed to a flag in the pave- fluent and said: "Et is here that one of Jack the "ZPPer's victims perished." "We stared," says B°ReRY, in recounting his experiences. "It 'Ora a street .like any other one, very neat and _ ean On both sides honest English houses, with look of calm and virtue. The nightmare that 111, weighed upon our imagination began to vanish,,„ and we began to laugh and be merry."' .L'uoy were shown a room in which another of Jack _ the Ripper's atrocities had been perpetrated, but tl'U i'ell here they were not imPressed. "Once in the Street.. we all laughed at one another for our gulli- b.llitY• All those stories about Whitechapel are a 'st hoax." Such was the conclusion they came 12 and it was not dissipated by a visit to some ;light ref uges, and a Salvation barracks. M. ~..altcnir declares that he learned one lesson. It ze to. reject legends and to turn a deaf ear neve: the horrors of Whitechapel are talked of to hioa.</p>
        <p>LOCAL NEWS.</p>
        <p>In TER VICTORIA DISASTER. wia_several of the churches and chapels in Runcorn and en Sunday references were made in prayers and to the fearful disaster to the battle ship etoria.</p>
        <p>~ HOLY TRINITY CHURCH RUNCORN. :. anniversary sermons were preached on Sunday . V the Rev - A. :et the leader:3.Np of Mr. Lancaster, rendered an anthem .... each aervice• ' 11tWilen in want of ft CHASTE and ELEGANT WEDDING or . RTIMAY PRESENT go to PRITOHARD's, 51, Ifigh-st.—.Adc T ar, FARNWORTH WESLEYAN CHAPEL. a T. tk_ he —nivereary services in connection with the above ! .Pel were held on Sunday, when the Rey. T. La•w late llg It; . ',lrtuit minister, occupied the pulpit morning and even- - i r. • %-.olleCtlo as were taken in aid of the Sunday school. The l FUNERAL OF MRS. WILLIAMS OF RUNCORN, ~ sa,a,ne or ~ Mrs. William Williams wife of Mr. of "e lid ig r,ewater Ar s Hotel, Percival-lane, were in- terred in 'n Runcorn Cemetery on Sunday. The funeral _ Was attended by numerous sympathising friends, and ' I„,fliany floral Offerings were deposited on the grave. Mrs. ' "dliarns had died on Thursday, after a brief illness front English Cholera. BpJOSEPH PRITCHARD. 51. keeps EcracLts to High-street, Runcorn, t suit all sights, from a shilling a 1 air.—Adv . RUNCORN C0M815510147E05 AND THE CO-OPERATIVE SOCIETY. A !Medal meeting of Runcorn Board of Improvement Commissioners was held on Wednesday under the pre- aidency of Mr. S. Taylor. It was resolved on the Motion of Mr. Fisher, seconeed by Mr. Hopkinson, " That the tender of the Runcorn Co-operative Seciety for the loan of £BOO at 34 per cent. interest for a period of seven years he accepted, and that the seal of the ;oard be attached to the bond for the same." Sr. Luxo's CONGREGATIONAL SCHOOL, RUNCORN. _ The anniversary of this school was celebrated in Bethesda Church, on Sunnay, when the Rev. J. D. Jones, Of Bolton, formerly of Runcorn, preached two impres- • aree sermons to large congregations. In the afternoon the rev, gentleman conducted a children's service in St. s 401001, Pennington's-row. At each service the 'Moir and scholars rendered specially selected music in an adle,,ahle manner. Mr. C. Le Couteur was at the The collect iota realised £8 2s. ed. ' it °SEER PRITCRARD, Watchmaker and Jeweller, 51, IIIgh- •• Enneorn.—ildrt • UNITED METHODIST FREE CHURCH. RUNCORN. 'litre annual school sermons were preached en Sunday t"h Ile°. 11. Ramer t' of Rochdale. In the morning 'e th°ll. gave an efficienti rern,l 's rendering of Mr. J. B. Calkin 511th gave :' Rejoice n the Lord,, and in the evening Dr. seh i 411 a " Benedictus in E." In the afternoon th• Girt noitrards and choir, under the able conductorship of Mr. , , interpreted Dr. Root's cantata "The Choicest great appreciation of a large assembly. The , rnnaeirre was occupied by Mr. Thomas Ogden, of RothdT ralh s. tliectio o„ were good congregations at each service. The ns realized £2l 2s. ed. Joh 'NE THerr OF A BOATRORSR AT RUNCORN. NOD ris. Hula°, a man of no settled abode, was again , et,tro`"ffeot before the Runcorn magistrates, on Thursday, a '" with haying on the night of the 16th June stolen I saan'' S'e, vlalue £(3, the property of Thomas Ciatten, boat- lOClap t°m• The horse was placed in a stable at Weston 814 w:" and next morning had disappeared. On the fol- -14 Sunday P.C. Gregory found the animal in the 8,1)855 of the Oddfellows' Arms, City-road, Chester. StaffetittentlY the prisoner was apprehended by the Nis °ord police and handed over to P.C. Gregory. The 0,1411 who had been six times previously convicted,o`"° of" Who was committed to the Assizes for trial. nu' pivr,t PRETcHARD's 'l' • Iligh-stre-t Railcars', for the best ' "NEra WaTcl; ')o. oorkingman Can have.—Adcf. At x BREAKING A MAN'S JAW AT RUNCORN. hi° tl nut4f°rd Quarter Sessions, on Wednesday, before lab° °ll°nr Judge Horatio Lloyd, James Finegan, 34, hasot;rar, was indicted for inflicting grievous bodily Itit iPon, Frank Lyden at Runcorn.—Mr. Burke Wood, IMblios,,Prosecution, saii tile,4c two men turned out of a (low -n --""t's,, together: and. Finegan knocked Lyden sevet • Not content with that he kicked him violently So „al times on the head and face, breaking his jaw. the Lril_°u4 were the injuries that Lyden was removed to Joe., r,v.erPool Infirmary, where lie lay from April 30 to preL,,.:, and though a good cure was made,. be would rese"i'"AY never quite recover from the injuries .le Prisoyel.---Prisoner was sentenced to six months' im- I, nmgnt. .D by skilled workmen Parre'ttmfaOrs"Cildß-rPAIRE At Airt'Lacian ItniasErts. OF A WATCH AT RUNCORN. Nayie uncorn Police Court on Thursday Elizabeth ley" r. aged 22 years was charged with stealing a silver Watch, vain() 15s d 6s. in silver, the property ~ t ' an Atiai.., - 4 of Eliza Smith, wife of John Smith, of 3, 9e.lith.rees. The prisoner was in the employ of Mrs. Plaoe,., „aa domestic servant. On the 11th Match she the et.i ner watch under the pillow in the bedroom. In lads:Ping of the satne day she sent the prisoner to Mr. (Li°lllC,lBl):,Woshop, with six shillings to redeem her Sons °caiil ,'"ateir ‘,, 58 'Aim Prisoner did not return and.afterwards the :,.- missed. The prisoner absconded from tf rinus.?i but on Wednesday she returned to her mother's Stone-street and P.C. Gregory apprehended her. rt,n4er) S', to the usual charge she admitted taking besoreY the out not the cvatch.—Frisoner was again brought (iaLs'illrilSiarnbaoguistrates yesterday, and sentenced to 21 s Arson , r• %alit.., Artr)l3 have pow stood the test for fifty year,* and aldat. and first-class work manahlp are still guaranteed.— The th!IUCORN RAMBLING CLUB EXCURION. 01 With B r'un ard outing of the present season in connection nolwh task swick-st. Improvement Class Rambling Club Place ie„,_, OD Saturday afternoon, when a large number for,b'iloners and friends paid a visit to Aston Hall, ' veyert"lg a collegiate establishment. They were co, t. aro, ,ii brakes, b supplied y B Mr. ooth, Ci o lurch-st. n Mr. 8,,,:ivt1, they were kindly sii;wri round the place by r. ,ca 0 gardener, Who made an excellent cicerone. The tzfit,,i of ~ the vineries, ferneries. and gardens were in- visite,c,with keen interest. The party subsequently neigh! °ll 43 ancient church, and afterwards explored the ,Densori,thood. They had tea in the Board School, clis- liishr,"Y Miss Spearing. At the call or Mr. Dante! . eoioai7;_n, a hearty vote of thanks was accrrded to Ur- Made ...5.7. e. for the admirable arrangements they had Mich bad so much contributed to the enjoyment by If afternoon, on the !nation of Mr. Povah; seconded halve,ri.k.john !help-qt. The compliment was acknow- -1 col. "Y Mr. Walter . Millington. TI.H party arrived " at nine o'cloek, after a thoroughly pleasant outing.</p>
