      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <p>ASSAULT AT WIDNES. At Widnes Petty Sessions on Thursday, James Elo:den, of 3, Carey-street, a young married man, was committed for trial upon a charge of indecently assaulting Catherine Rubinson,.aged 21 years, described as a woman of weak intellect. RCNCORN V. BIRKENHEAD VICTORIA "A." Birkenhead 'Victoria brought a strong team to Run- corn on Wednesday, and a good game ended in a victory for Runcorn, for whom the Rev. G. H. Kingston batted in fine form. Score :—Birkenhead 'Victoria " A," 72 ; Runcorn, 100. • CONFIRMATION AT FARNWORTH PARISH CHURCH. On Wednesday afternoon a confirmation service was held in St. Luke's Church, Yarn worth, by Bishop Roys- ton, the assistant blshop of Liverpool, assisted by the Rev. J. Wright Williams. There were 138 candidates prmented from Farnworth, namely, 81 females and 57 malcs, and 30 from St. Ambrose parish. ACCIDENT TO A HORSE TRAINER. On Wednesday an accident betel Thomas Parkes, f' Sutton-St , RUDCOrp, whilst training a horse belonging to Mr. Briscoe, butcher, of Church-st. He was riding it for the first time, so as to accustom it it to the saddle, i when it fell upon him. His leg and face were badly bruised, but fortunately no bones were broken. Ile is' under the care of Dr. McKenzie.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN BURIAL BOARD. A meeting of the ratepayers of the township of Run- cwn was held on Thursjay evening in the Be.thesda Schoolroom, to elect three members of the Runcorn Burial Board, in place of Messrs. Richard Morris, Samuel Taylor, and Thomas Handley, who wete eligible for re- election. Mr. William Mellor was voted to the chair. On the motion of Mr. unison, seconded by Mr. lAng- shaw, it was unanimously decided that the retiriog members be re-elected. THE BAKERS PISPUTE AT WIDNES. This dispute still continues. The Trades Council have passed a resolution sympathising with the men and announsing that they have the cordial support of the Council, who desire to be provided with ,a list of the names of those masters which refuse to comply with the just dem ands made. The Committee of the Co-opera- tive Society have communicated their resolve to abolish night work, which the main grievance, as early as possible.</p>
        <p>PROTECTION OF WAIFS AND STRAYS. Yesterday afternoon a meeting of the Church of Eng- land Society, for the Protection of Waifs and Strays was held in Widnes Town Hall under the presidency of the Mayor (Alderman F. H. (lossage J.P.) There was a good attendance of local gentlemen and ladies. Speech. 3 advocating the claims of the society were delivered by Mr. G. B. Lockett, Rev. F. It. Burrows, Rev. Canon Illogg,Rev.G.H.Garrett, and Mr.W.H.Wade Deac n J P. C.C. It was urged that it was desirable to form a com- mittee in Widnes. Ilmeawav HORSE AT RUNCORN. 03 Tuesday evening, sh-irtly after seven o'clock, an animal belonging to Mr. Walter Collier, grocer, whilst in Norman-road, Runcorn, became startled and took fright. It ran at full speed a considerable distance along the thoroughfare named, when the cart over- turned. The shafts were smashed, and the animal with these dangling at its heels continued its onward career along Mill-brow, and over Delph Bridge. In descending the hill it fell and was somewhat badly cut in several places. Fortunately no accident happened to any pedestrians. ' CORPORATION PICNIC AT WIDNES. The first annual picnic of the Widnes Corporation ser- vants took place on Wednesday, when about 250 work- men, their wives, and friends, went by special train on the Cheshire Lines to Belle Vue, Manchester, where a most enjoyable day was spent.A substantial tea was par- taken of at five o'clock, and subsequently responding to a vote of thanks on behalf of the Town Council, Mr. Alderman Sadler said he hoped in future the Corpora- tion would defray the whole of the cost of the treat in addition to giving the men their wages. Private firms did so and why not the Corporatien. Mr. Council! it G. I. Neil expressed similar views. DANGERS OF PLAYING " AUNT SALLY." Somewhatserious injuries were sustained by William Plant, aged 10 years, residing in Sandy-lane, Weston Point, on Monday evening. With some companions he was engaged in the pastime known as " Aunt Sally," when the missile thrown by one of the boys struck Plant, and lacerated his left ear and cheek. Fortunately Dr. McKenzie was in the locality, and he promptly attended to the injured boy, and stitched up the wound, which bled so profusely that if medical assistance had not been so near at hand might have terminated fatally. Plant is now progressing favourably. WIDNES COUNTY COURT.- .</p>
        <p>Yesterday at Widnes County his Honour Judge Shand, Mark Steinhardt, travelling jeweller, sued Thomas Leather, bricklayer, for the sum of £718.1. SI, balance of the price of a gold watch supplied. The de- fendant denied that he knew of the debt until he re- ceived the summons, and his wife said the plaintiff pressed her to take the watch, and three time he had refused to take it back.—lt transpired that the defen- dant had recently purchased two gold alberts, and the Judge remarked that he was a very foolish fellow to ore id his money in this manner. Defendant said he had better do this than spend it in drink.—The case was adjourned for the production of the watch, so that the Judge might form an estimate of its value. There were no ether cases of public interest.</p>
        <p>Arc Ex-Ttorcwaix FoLICEMAN AND Hrs WIFE. At the Crewe County Petty Sessions on Monday, be- fore Alderman McNeil and Walviortlt, James Woodrow, ez-policeman, was summoned by his wife, Annie Wood- row, for £3 63., arrears under a maintenance order which she had against him. Annie Woodrow said she obtained the order on the 9th of August, 1891, for 23. per week, and the arrears now due were £3 fis. The defendant pleaded inability to pay, and stated that though he received £BO from the Railway Company owing to an accident, he had spent it in paying a number of debts which had been contracted through his wife, who lie said was the cause of his leaving the police force through her drunken habits. Since leaving the force he was employed on the Crewe Railway Station, and there he met with an accident. He was allowing hie wife 14.5. per week at that time. The Bench refused to en- force the order. FUNERAL OF A RUNCORN SCwootnor. On Saturday afternoon the funeral of Thomas Gill, whose death was caused by drowning under circum• stances already reported in these columns, took place at the Runcorn Cemetery. Deceased was in Standard H. at the Greenway-road Boys' School, where he was a great favourite with his fellow scholars, and respected by his teachers. In token of the esteem in which he was held, and as a mark of sympathy for the bereaved parents, the teachers and scholars sent a beautiful wreath. In addition to the relatives, the funeral was attended by a number of the boy's schoolfellows, and also by Messrs. J. F. Moulton (head master), W. Kennan, E. W. Rosser (assist Ant and E. Bowyer (school visitor). The burial service was impressively read by the Rev. F. Wilkes, Wesleyan minister, and the hymn " Lead, kindly Light," was sweetly sung by the scholars of the Board Schools at the grave- side. A RUNCORN BITICIIER COMMITTED TO GAOL. At Liverpool City Police Court, en Wednesday, before Mr. V. J. Stewart, st ipendiary,Thomas Clough, butch er, 1 carrying on business at Runcorn,was charged with hav- ing on the 23rd inst. sent to Liverpool the dead carcase of a. pig which was unfit for human food. Mr. Moss prosecuted, and called Inspector Renton, who said that on Friday, the 23rd inst., in company- with Inspector Luya he visited the abattoir and saw the pig in question exposed for sale. 'rite carcase-was rotten, and totally unfit for human food. From inquiries he afterwares made he discovered that the pig had been sent there by the defendant, by whom .it had been killed and dressed. Ile (witness) produced a magistrate's order, and had the carcase destroyed. Inspector Luya des- cribed the pig as rotten from head to tail. He considered the animal had been in a diseased state for s,x months previous to its being slaughtered. In reply to the magis- trates, defendant pleaded that he thought the animal was fit for market. It looked fresh when killed, but 119 confessed that on arrival at Liverpool it was father soft. Mr. Stewart asked the defendant if he was aware that if any person had died from the effects of consuming the animal that he would have been liable to be charged with manslaughter. It was only recently that a man had been sentenced to eight months' imprisonment for such an offence. lie considered the present was a deli- berate offence, and defendant must go to gaol for 14 days without the option of a fine. BRASS BAND Cd'ONTBST AT Sr. HELENS. OA Saturday, the second annual brass band con- test, promoted by Thomas Boardman, of the George Hotel, St. IlelenB, was held in Littler'a field, with the following result :—Pirst prite. £l2 and a sheep, Pemberton Old Band ; second, £7, Platt Bridge Band ; third, £4, Gossage's Soapworke Band, Widnes ; fourth, £2, Wingate Temperance Band ; fifth, £l, Earlestown Viaduct Basal. The test piece was " Theodora " (11. Round), and waltz. There were till' entries, and the judge was Mr. J. 0. Shep- herd, Liverpool. A solo euphonium prize of .£1 was won by the Wingate Temperance Band, and the quickstep prize was won by Widnes Subscription.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>FitODSHAM.</head>
        <p>FitODSHAM.</p>
        <p>Niw SAIiITARYArs.—On Wednesday morn ' ing at Frodshain P«flty Sessions, the magigtiates affixed [heir signatures to a new sanitary rate of sd. in the 2, for the township of Prodsham, estimated to realise £25 13. 7i. PETTY SESSIONS. WEDYESDAL-13efure Messrs.. C. Reynolds, A. Thomas,</p>
        <p>and T. Rigby. LlCESSlNG.—Applications were made for the tempor- ary transfer of the license of the Bull's Head, Overton, from George White tb his widow, Elizabeth White ; and of the Weaver Tavern, Frodsham Bridge, from Sarah Edge to Joseph Faulkner.—Tbe requests were granted. CRUTVY TO A fionsa.—Thomas Lingard, Lyrnm, pig dealer, was summoned for cruelly ill-treating a horse, by working it in an unfit condition, on June &amp;b.—A fize of ss. and 10s. 6i. costa was imposed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>EX A-MINER, SATURDAY, JULY 1; 1893. HEA It D' AN D SEEN AT I WALIi.ING DAY AT RUNCORN. WARRINGTON. BY AN OCCASIONAL CORRESPONDENT.</head>
        <p>THE EX A-MINER, SATURDAY, JULY 1; 1893. HEA It D' AN D SEEN AT I WALIi.ING DAY AT RUNCORN. WARRINGTON. BY AN OCCASIONAL CORRESPONDENT.</p>
        <p>From a Manchester evening paper I learn that Dr. Mullen medical superintendent of the Salford Corporation Sanatorium at Ladywell, was last wet-k presented with the certificate of the Royal Humane Society for bravery in saving life. The ohmmeter:tees are thus described. "In Raster week Dr. Mullen was having a sail in a steam lannth on the Bridgewater Canal along with Air. Collier and other friends, theluding a by named Geoffrey Althed, a nephew of Mr. Collier. On arriving at Thelma! the .boy fell overboard, and Dr. Mullen; intnped into tie water to save him. He struggled to get the boy to the bank until be was much ex- hausted and both were rescued by another gentle- man."</p>
        <p>I • Oa the principle of giving honour to whom hon- our is due it ought to have been mentioned that another gentleman' was Mr. Frank Wiswall C.E. vx-chairman of Runcorn Board, of Commissioners. 'I he incident was reported in this purnal at the lime and equally us much credit is due to Mr. Wise- aul as Dr. A ulien, if not more and the honours of the aumane Society ought .to be distributed with au impartial hand. Dr. Mullen was certainly the drat to enter the watet but he was, unable to ac- complish what he sought to perform and probably both he and the boy would have perisheu had not Mr. sViswall who is a strong swimmer gallantly plunged into the water and carried both to the bank. The Humane Society ought to be made acquainted With these facts. Roth gentlmen were brave and galltnt and both deserve recognition.</p>
        <p>In introducing his legal pillory in " Truth " this week, Mr. I...bone:it-re says : "Tne performances of our great unjust are of the usmil type. Brutal assaults and acts of cruelty are treated with the utmost tenderness and consideration, while Inch offences as being homeless and pennilees,absconding from Workhouses,and so °mare visited wit h extreme severity." The Runcorn magistrates were placed in the pillory as follows :—ltuncorn Police Court,before J. Brunclrit and P.6pookman,Ann Ford charged with drunkenness and breaking the window of her cell. One month." In contrast with this case is the subjoined :—" Shrewsbury Police Court,before G. Evans (Mayor), V. Crump, T. Southain and other magistrates, John Plimmer,charged with assaulting Edwarhl Owen. Defendant knocked Owen down dislocating his shoulder, struck him in the mouth, knocked out two teeth nad also hit him in the eye, and seriously injured his nose. Fined £3 including costs."</p>
        <p>Runcorn Board cf Guardians appear to get no fur- ther ahead in their efforts to obtain free education for the poor children of Runcorn. There are a number of children attending the Denominational Schools, where fees are charged, whose parents are too poor to pay the fees demanded, and the Guar- dians are compelled to do so. A communication addressed to the inanag'ers of these schools request- ing them to allow such children free, met wit h a response only from the Brunswick Wesleyan School managers, who bimp!y point out that they are en- titled to charge. The Board on Tuesday decided to demand free places for the children througu the Education Department. Considering that prior to the passing of the " Free" Education Act, the Giiardians were not empowered to pay a higher fee than 3d. per week, no matter in what standard the child might be, and that the managers now re-1 ceived that amount for each child from the Im- perial Exchequer, it does seem both ridiculous and unjust that the local ratepayers should be collect upon for any additional Emu. . Of course the fault lies with the law; Government when it-, decided upon the payment of a free grant of 10s. per head per annum ought to have declared all schools free. Perhaps we may have this when Home Rule and a few other trifles are out of the way.</p>
        <p>The vacancy in Victoria Ward, Widnes, caused through the bankruptcy of Mr. Fitzpatrick, appears to be causing little interest at present. Mr. Hugh O'Donnell grocer, Widnes-road, formerly inspector of police at Runcorn, is the nominee of the Irish Naticualists, and he has held the field tor a week. It is practically certain that the Nationalists will not be opposed by the Liberal party, and rumours of a split in their own camp are denied. The name of Mr. T. Atherton, of Lacey-st„ has been promin- ently mentioned us probable candidate, and other names have been whispered. Monday next is the last day for nominations, and the election is fixed to take place on the Monday following.</p>
        <p>The-celebrations at Wicines, on Thursday next,ou the occasion of the marriage of the Duke of York, appear likely to be a great success, providing fine weather is vouchsafed. Subscriptions have poured in much more merrily than was anticipated a week ago the Town 1411 officials and the committee of representativesbave worked well and harmoniously, the arrangements are in a forward state, and an im- posing and worthy demonstration may be looked for.</p>
        <p>THE PROCESSIONS.- Yesterday's glorious weather crowned the Walk- ing Day gala with unqualified success. During the whole of the day there was no thre,at of .in- clemency to be seen in the skies, for the warm sun which rose upon the town early in the morning continued to pour down its welcome rays until evening. So early as eight o'clock in the morning the town was astir with eager youngsters, decked in their new clothes; for it is the custom among most parents to honour the occasion with new apparel for their children. There were others, too, besides those neatly attired and scrupulously clean children, abroad, long before nine, for the huudreds of employes from the variOus works were astir early to enjoy their holiday. From ten o'clock until half-past, the children be- longing to the various schools flocked frato the lawn before the hall. .About ten o'clock the procession commenced to move from the park, beaded by the Blue Coat School children, ann the children from the Padgate Industrial Schools. Tire route taken was across Sankey-at., down Arpley-St., along Wil- sun Patten-se, past Arpley Station, up Bridge-et. to Market Gate and thence to the Parish Church. It was a commonly-heard remark that never before have there been such large numbers of spectators in the streets. The whole length of the route was absolutely glutted with sightseers, and it was only by dint of much energy that'the police were able to keep a sufficient course open lor the procession between the thick line of people on each side of the street. Viewing the procession as it passed Market Gate, a more perfect sight of it was obtained than at any other point. The Blue Coat School children, who were conducted by Mr. and Iles. Rose lead the way, and behind them came the Padgate Industrial School children. Walking at the head of this school were the following members of th.e Board of Guar- dians : Messrs. Davies, Gill, Muir, W. Houghton, R. Ileaton, and Mr. S. Sutton (deputy clerk), and Mr. Ward (Workhouse master), Mrs. Ward (matron), and Miss Ward. The Parish Church Schools fol- lowed next, including St. George's Mission which was pleasantly conspicuous betbause almost all the children—not includding the boys though ! carried large Japanese shades, which were pretty to look at and no doubt acceptable protectors from the sun to the childree. Sr. Paul's and All Saints, and St. Ann's scholars passed next, and then came what has been described as the prettiest portion of the procession. It was the acholars of St. James Church School, who were arranged so as to form a pasteral4 representation. Preceding the ordinary scholars was the Boys Brigade, composed of fine healthy- looking young fellows, accoutred like infantry soldiers, and under the command of the Rev. A. V. C. liordern. Following these came the smaller female scholars almost all of whom carried bouquets of variegated flowers, or crooks entwined with creeping plants. Then presently came a small mail cart all decorated so as to seem as though it was completely built of flewere and verdant leaves. It contained a little boy and Watt drawn by four pretty little girls. Another batch of scholars intervened and then came another mail cart of a similar character, but containing two little girls, the daughters of Mr. C. S. Burgess. Behind this came a lorry containing a number of chubby-faced chil- dren, all at tired alike, and between whom sat on an elevated throne, Miss Slater. Several new banners were also displayed by this school. Amongst the other schools which followed was the Raga(' Sun- day School, under the divction of Mr. Sltellaud. Much notice was naturally (alien of this school, and considerable surprise was expressed at the neat manner in which the children turned out. In reality it was no loager a "ragged school "; but this was owing chiefly to a number of kind persons having supplied the children with carious articles of clothing. The scholars! from St. Paul's and St. Ann's attended divine service at St. Paul's Church, whilst most of the other Church of England scholars attended divine service at the Parish Church, conducted by the Rev, C. C. Meuse, In the afternoon the scholars resorted to their various fields, with the exception of several of the Nonconformist bodies who left town in the morn- ing for different places. AR in former years, the Salvation Army occupied the lawn opposite the Teem Rail, where they fed more than 1,100 children and provided amusements for them. This is a Course adopted by the Salvationists which is com i - mendable inasmuch as it provides a treat for hun- dreds of children who would otherwise enjoy nothing of the day's festivities. Captain Morelien was in charge et the arrangements. Below we give particulars with regard to each of the schools : PARISI! CHURCII SCIIOOI. (Chuicli-St.).--Number of scholars on books, 230 boys ; 108 in young men's bible class ; number of teachers, 14 male, 2 female ; minister, the Rev. Thomas W. Cook; superintendent, Mr. James Scot-son Sr.• EO p 0 E's missioN ROOM SUNDAY SCllool..—Number of scholars on books 406, boys 151 girls, 255 ; average attendance 110 boys, 190 girls' number of teachers 22, 7 male, 15 female ; number of books in library 180 ; minister and superintendent, the Rev. Charles E. Jack- son. Both schools joined in the procession which wai followed by the service in the Parish Church. St. Elphins Park was adjourned to at 2 o'clock. Tea was provided, and the day was spent in games and other outloor pas- times. The return home was made at 9 p.m.</p>
        <p>Sr. JAMES'S. SUNDAY SCHOOL.—Clergy : Rev. Francis Slater, M.A., and Rev. Arthur V. C. Hordern. The scholars met at St. James's Church, headed by the band of the Volunteer Battalion, marched to the Vicarage, round the garden, and thence to church for a short ser- vice. The hymns were accompanied by the band. They then joined the general proci ssion through the town,and returned to the school, where they separated for dinner. The afternoon was spent in a field the Causeway, kindly lent by Mesqs. Greenall, Whitley, and Co. Tea was provided in the school, and the day finished with a dance for the elder scholars. A new and very handsome silk banner, made by Messrs. Tattersall and Co., was used for the first time, insteal of the well-remembered old one. The Church Lads' Brigade made their first ap- pearancr:in the pr6cesaxin. Sr. PAUL'S CITURCH SCHOOL9.—Number of scholars on the books, male and female, 1,200. Clergy, Bev. H. V. Pigntt, Rev. T. M. R. Young Hughes, Rev. J. Forbes Superintendants, Messrs. Worsiey, and Siddle, and : Messrs. Buckley, J. Hal!. Banner, and Hough, sides- men. The day was spent in a field at Pailgate. ALL SAINT'S Missicd4.—Nulflber f scholars 430; No.of teachers, 5 male, 10 female; clergy : Rev. H. V. Pigot, M.A., Vicir ; 8.-iv. T. M. It. Young Hughes, 8.A., J. Forbes, 8.A., curate's; superintendent, Mr Peter Sudlow. The children met at All Saint's Mission at 8 30 ; marched to Kendrick-street Schools, they joined St. Paul's Schopls and spent the day with them as in former years. . .</p>
        <p>- - Sr. lIARNABAS Fenoot.—Number of scholars on books 90 kora, 100 girls ; and a number of infanas, average attendance, 60 boys, 70 girls, number of teachers, 6 male, 7 f,male ; minister, the Rev. G. Allton, Rtendrretu r t, .he childrenboysMr.R ml,d . Ja.e NsieTo n 1 !lace() at tt, girls, Miss A. Wilm- h. the scliool, 9 15 and Imded by Fletcher, Russell and Co'.9 bend, marched to the park, and joined in the procession to Buttermarket, returning up Froghall-lane to church at 11.15, where a shortened service, with sermon, was held. The children reassembled at 2 o'clock, and adjourned to: the field adjoining time schools, where sports and various games were indulged in. Tea refreshments was provided and the party broke up at nine o'clock. Sr. MAItYS D.C., BUTTPAMARKET•STRUN'T.—There are 340 boys and 302 girls on the books in this school, the average attendance being 559. The number of teachers is two males and eleven females. The superintendent of the school is the Rev. Father Duggan O.S.H. In the procession there were about 950 persons. They were preceded by the League of the Cross Band. In the pro- cession there were the Rev. Father: F. W. Sumner, Wray. M. E. Duggan, Turner (St. Ann's, Liverpool), Councillor J. 11. Flowing, Mes.rs. G. T. Moore, T. IL Kiern,n, W. Kiernan, R. Ecck•s, T. Mather, W. B. Iloor,hton, G. end A. Vernon, and Dr. McCarthy. After the procession the scholars entered St. Mary's' Roman Catholic Church, Buttermarket-street, when the Benediction was sung accompanied by the band of the League of the Cross. The scholars tifterwards went : home for dinner, and re-assembled at-two o'clock, when j they proceeded to a field at Orford, ''emit by Mr. John Caldwell. The afternoon was spent in games Szc., and tea was provided. ST. ALBANS AND ST. BANEDICT.S ROMAN CATIZOLTD.— The number cf scholars in these schools is 700. There are two male and 19 fetnale teachers. :These schools led in the Catholic procession and were heeded by the War- rington Borough Band, which for the first time used the new inst ruinents. In the procession were the Rev. Fathers Feeney, Cody, Craven, DArby; Myerscough, and Ryan, Mr. Giligan, Miss Jo-e, and Miss J. Bellian (superintendents), and Miss Orealey (infants). After parading the miecipal sl-reets the scholars dispersed until two o'clock, when they again assembled and marched to a field at Orford, kindly lent by-Mr. John Caldwell. Here tea was partaken of, and various games indulged lin. The return journey was coinmenced about eight [o'clock.</p>
        <p>ST. MARY'S ROHAN CATHOLIC. L iTCHFO RD.—This schocli followed (ho St. Alttates school in the procession. A.hot.it 200 scholars where preset.t. Father Hennelly Is 10 charge of this school. 10 the anon:don the scholars pro c'etted to a field at lAtchfor,l where tea was provided. Gatnts were indulged in until dark.</p>
        <p>EMMANUAL REFORMED CIBM2CH OR ENGLAND SUNDAY SCHOOL—Number of scholars on books, 130 buys 129 girls ; average attendance • 115 boys • 110 girls ; number' of teachers, 9 male 7 female; number of books in library 500; minister' Rev. Edward T. Reed ; superintendent, Mr. John Allen. The children assembled at school at nine o'clock and at 9.30 the proce4sion started for the Park via Bridge-st. and Sankey-st., where they joined in the general prom. sion. A beautiful new banner bearing the inscription " Our Christian Endeavour Society," was used for the first time. After parading the town the children were dispersed, reassembled at school, and at 12.30 started for Hill Warren (kindly lent for the occasion by Mr. Da rbyshire) in 'busses. Upon arriving there dinner was served. Games, &amp;c. were afterwards engaged in. Tea being served at five o'clock and at eight o'clock, the signal was given for returning home, arriving there at nine o'clock, after having spent a most enjoyable day. The day's proceedings were brought to a close with the singing of the doxology and the National Anthem. WYCLIFFE SUNDAY SCHOOL—Number of scholars on books, 319 boys ; 258 girls ; average total 369; number lof teaahers, 25 male, and 16 femalf. Minister : Rev. T. Yonge ' • superintendent, Mr. Geo. Wallington. The scholars left for Dunham Hill by the 10 o'clock train and returned at 8.304 Dinners and tea was provided and sports both for boys and girls were arranged, including a cricket match. WYCLIFFE COCKILEDGE MISSION BRICK-STREET.—NUM- bier of scholars on hooks 450, boys, 240 girls, 210; aver- age attendance, 200 boys, 190 girls ; No. of teachers, 12 male, 12 female. Superintendents : Messrs. John Plinston and H. Maybury. Tha day vhs spent in a field in Padgate. BOLD-ST. WESLUYAN.—Number of scholars on books, 242 boys, 270 girls; average attendance, 140 boys, 180 girls; number of teachers, 18 male, 24 females; number of books in library, 430; ministers, Revs. J. Kirtian, A. Freeman and Thomas Ross; superintendent, Mr. Wm. Silcock ; secretarie.s, W. A. Mellor, and F.T. Wilson. The teachers, scholars and friends journieed by special train at 9.40 from Arpley station to Danham Massey, and then proceeded to Dunham Park, where they spent the day. Dinner and tea were provided in the farm buildings of Mr. Warburton and the return was made from Dunham Massey about eight p.m. SILVER-ST. WESLEYANS.—NOMber of scholars MI books, 204 boys, 217 girls; average attendance, 152 boys, 135 girls ; number of teachers, 15 male, 19 female: number of books in library, 480; ministers, Rev. J. Kirtlan, Rev. A. Freeman and Rev. T. hose; superintendents, Mr. .1. W. Hayward .LP., and Mr. J. Shaw Green. Warrington was left at 10 for Ilelsby. The return was made at 8.20 p.m. The scholars were provided with dinner and tea, and football and cricket gaines, &amp;c., were indulged in. ELLESMERE-ST. WESLEYAN MISSION.—Number of ! scholars on books, males 140, females, 182 ; average attendance, morning 68, afternoon 196 ; superintendents Messrs. W. Gregson, John Barlow, and Joseph Dobson, secretary, Mr. John Lingard, and Mr. John Ellison. The scholars met at the school at 8 0 a.m. and formed in possession and marched direct to a field situated in Grappenhall, kindly lent by Mr. L. James. The younger children were conveyed on lurries lent by Messrs. Howard, Emmiss, and Grainger ot Rail-at. Dinner and tea were provided and the day was spent in the usual games, racing &amp;c. SANKEY BRIDGES WESLEYAN.—Number of scholars on books,l4o boys; girls 80; eo infants ; average attendance morning 90, afternoon 220 ; number of teachers, 27 male ; female 7 ; average teachers attencling,morning 12; after- noon, 25. Ministers : Itev.J. Kirtlan,A. Freem en, and T. Ross, Superintendents, M•s-irs. J.L.George, W.Braugh ; secretaries, Messrs.S.Blythe, and F. Light ; treasurer,Mr. W. Stimmerfleld. The day was spent in a field at Pen- keth,kindly lent by Mr.Pownall, where games of various descriptions were indulged in.</p>
        <p>Sr. JOITIN's PRILSBYTICRIAN SUNDAY. SCHOOL.—Number of scholars on books 60 boys, 40 girl;; average attend- ance. 45 boys, 20 girls ; No. of teachers 7, male 3 female ; No. of books in library, 600 ; minister : Rev. J. Warnock ; superintendents : Messrs. T. Longdin and C. Sudlow. The day was spent at Dunham Park, the journey being made by rail. 0914)010.11-6TSEET PAPTlST.—Ntimber of WAITS 0.T.1 book, 82 boys •70 girls aerage attendance, 103 scho- lars ; number of teachets, 14 mile, 10 female.•Minist•r : Rev. D. W. Roberts ; superiniendent. Mr. S. Fernloy ; secretary, 11.8. Crowder. A journey on lorries, com- mencing at 8.30, to a tif.ld at Appleton, kindly lent by Mr. Pimblatt, of Burley Ileyes, occupied a good portion of the morning. Dinner and tea were provided for the scholars, and past-times such as cricket, rounders, &amp;c., were freely indulged in.</p>
        <p>CAIRO-STREET SUNDAY SCHOOL—Number of scholars on hooks 142 boys, 169 girls ; averave attendance, 204 mornings,27o afternoons ; teachers,l3 males,l3 females ; no.of books in library,l,372. Minister, Itev.W.H.Drum- mond, B.A. Superintendent, Mr. Thomas littrywles. The scholars of this school met at Cairo-street at 6.45 am, and left the Central Station at 7.12 for Southport where they arrived at 9 a.m. A very enjoyable day was spent at the many attractive resorts of the beauti- ' ful watering place. Dinner and tea were provided at the Portland-street Schools. The arrival home was made at 9.15 p.m. • METHODIST FREE CHURCH MISSION (BUCKLEY-ST.).— Number of scholars on books, 180 boys, 180 girls; average attendance, 120 boys, 120 girls; number of teachers, 6 male, 7 female. The superintendent is Mr. George Urmston,and the secretary,Mr.J.E.Ttoman. The scholars met at the school at nine o'clock, where they formed in procesMon and paraded the principal streets headed by the Buckley-et. Brass Band. They then pro- ceeded to the Park where they joined in procession with the other schools, after which they returned to the school where they partook of a good substantial dinner consist- ' ing of meat pies, ham and beef sandwiches. After dinner they went to a field kindly lent by Mr. Robinson where they enjoyed themselves in various games as football, cricket, Ste,. About five o'clock they returned to the school where they partoek of an excellent tea.</p>
        <p>ERICK-STREET, AND FRIARS' GREEN SUNDAY SCgOOTe. —Number of scholars on books, 180 males, 160 females ; average attendance, 170 males, 136 girls; number of teachers 15 male, 15 females; number of books in libi ry 400. Minister : Various ; superintendents : Measrs.Joeeph U. Critchley and Arthur Jolley. At nine o'clock the scholars acsembled at Friars' Green chapel and marched ' thence to Brick-st. From this point the elder schiiiars journeyed to a field at Orford, kindly lent by Mr. Wm. Bennett, and the younger children were conveyed to the same field on lurries. The day was spent in an en- joyable manner. Teas and ditmers were provided, and the band rendered several selections of nirric: The return was made al9 p.m. EBENEZER Cnunca.—Number of scholars on books, 70 ' boys 98 girls; average attendance 120 boys ; number of teachers, 12 male 7 female ; minister, various ; superin- tendents, Mr. W. Miller avid Mr. Nathan Cook. Ihe school joined in the procession headed by the Friars- Green bre ss band. The procession v.-as.enlivenecl by two I beautiful new banners,worked by Miss Lillian Thornton, one of the teachers. Alter tile proce:sion a field in Marsh house-lane was adjourned to, cricket, football, and race running were the chief modes of enjoyment. The children were served with dinner and tea and other refreshments. . . . _</p>
        <p>BLUE COAT SCHOOL —Number of scholars on hooks 33 boys, 24 girls ; average attendance 33 boys, 24 githi, number of teachers, 3 male ; number of books in library 350. Superinteatient, Mr. F.,lWilrd Hose. The school joined in the procassion, and attended the service at the Parish Church. Afterwards the scholars had an enjoy- able gala in their own erounds,which consisted of various games and a tea party. " •</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON UNION.—N MIAMI' of children boys and girls, 36; Hey. B. V. Pigot. chaplain. Mr. and Mrs. Werd (Master and Matron), Miss Lee, childrens• attend- ant. The children formed in procnsion at 9-30 and at. tended divine service at the P.rish Church. The remainder of the day was spent in a field at Padgate kindly lent by Mr. liuughton.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON RAGOFD SCHOOL—NO[2II)er Of scholars on books, 420 ; hoes, 200 ; girls, 250 ; average attend- ance, 120 boys ; 160 girls ; number of teachers, 26 • 10 male, and 16 female. Superintendents, Messrs. Skelland and Owen. After the procession, the scholars were taken to the school, where they were provided with a dinner. They then marched to the Perk recreation groued, by permission of the Corporation, and the remainder of the day was spent enjoyably in games and other amuse- ments. nil was provided.</p>
        <p>WEEKLY REPORT OF THU BRALTII OF WAILRINGTON.</p>
        <p>During the week, which was the 25;11 of the Current year, the following births and deaths wen registered :—Births : Warrington, 35 ; Latchford, 5; total, 40. Deets: iVarrington, 28; Latch forsl, 2, total 30. The excess of births over deaths was 10. The death-rate from all causes was equal to 283 per 1.000, 'against 237 the average of the previous tour weeks, and against 18.2 the corresponding week of last year. The death-rate from the seven chief zytuotic diseases was equal to 12.2 per 1,000, against 7'2,the average rate of the four previous weeks, and ' against 1.9 the corresponding week of 1892. The ages of mortality were :—Warrington : 0 to I, 12; 1 to 5, 9 ; 5 to 15, 1 ; 15 to :25, 0 ; 25 t o GO, 5; GO up 3, total, 30. The deaths included-7 from measles '5 from diarrilea ; 1 from whooping cough; 5 from liear. disease ; 2 from brain dis- ease; 2 from lung d6ease ; The following cases of in fret Mils disease have heel) reported during the week :—SinallpeX,- nil ; scarlet fever, 1; diphtheria, 0; typhus :ever, 0 enteric fever, 0 ; relapsing fever, 0 ; puerperal fever; 0. Metereolog3. ~The mean temperaturi3 was 604 Fah. and therefore 12 degrees colder than the preceding week. The miximum temperature SOP., was. recorded on the 19th inst., owl the minimum temperature 47P., on the 21,t inst. The rainfall measured 48 inch. The general direction was LW.. the reading of the Anetnotneter denoting 905 mile.•:--Jonx IL GOILNALL, Medical Officer of Health.</p>
        <p>THINGS HEARD AND SEEN</p>
        <p>AT WARRINUTON..</p>
        <p>1 As I thought, the Royal wedding celebrations are I not going to take place in Warrington. A meeting of the Committee appointed to make arrangements, was held on Thursday, and decided " to take no further steps in furtherance of the celebrations." This was done on the of the Rev. D. W. Roberts. This was, under the circumstances, certainly the best conclusion to come to ; and, indeed, it was the only one open to them seeing that it would hare been impossible to carry eut a decent programme of" celebrations" on the magni- ficent rum of .£22 10i. 61, the total amount of the donations.</p>
        <p>Some members of the Sankey School Board appear not to believe very much in the value of punctuality. Its meetings are held monthly and it is a common thing for them to be adjourned in consequence of a quorum not" turning up" to time. On Thursday another meeting had to be adjourned because the Chairman and the Clerk were the only persons who attended to transact business. The common excuse given by the absentees, is, I believe, that they arrived five minutes late and found the meeting over! They do not seem to think that it is necessary to "turn up at the time the meeting is convened for, but at the There which is most convenient fot themselves. ihere are many sage remarks on the merits of punctuality scattered about in the shape of pro- verbs; it would perhaps not be II bad thing to supply unpunctual members of public bodies with a choice selection of these.</p>
        <p>-- Mr. A. IL Lee, of Latchford, has just issued the report and balance sheet of hi a summer camp for Warrington boys. From it I gather that la 4 year he collected subscriptions to the amount of .£2O 14s. 4d. from Warrington gentlemen, in order to take for a week to the seaside some of the poorest of Warrington youths, and 61 of these, ranging in 'age from 9 to 14, were sent to Ankle!), near Lyth- am, at the camp of the Boys' Refuge' Manchester Many more, t qually pour and in need of change, were wishful tu go. Great care was taken to select only the poorest,und no distinction of sect or creed was taken into account. The.Boys'Refuge, Man- chester take, it seems, Warrington boys at the rate of 6. per head, per week, including railway fare, from Manchester to the camp and back. The bays are under efficient control, and get a taste of mili- tary drill and discipline, with sea air and change of life. It is an effort well worthy of support, and it is to be hoped the number of lads sent from the Warrington " slums " this year for a week's fresh air will be an increase on last year.</p>
        <p>During, the hearing of a case of disorderly conduct ' in Wilderspool-road, tit. the Borough Court, on Mon- day, the Chief Constable referred to letters in the papers on disorderly conduct in Warrington streets, and particularly to one by " Decency," which ap- peared in the " Examiner.' This letter mentioned the disgraceful ectuluct onWilderspool-rd.on Sunday evenings,and the writer's assertions were prettyvvell confirmed both by OP. Chief Constable and the pre- siding magistrate. Mr.flunt said that the case then being dealt with was a specimen of the conduct complained of, and Mr. Monks, the chairman of the magistrates, remarked that there was not only disorderly conduct committed, but also coarse abu- sive language wed. the expressed a hope that Mr. Hunt would put a stop to it if he c.)uld. The Chief Cens•able seemed to be wishful to enlist a little outside help, for he said it would help the police materially if dime who wrote to the papers would. come and give him information personally upon the subjlect,</p>
        <p>" Five shillings and costs.!" That is a very cons- , mon formula at the end of the reports of police cases, but it sometimes imolies a good deal. At least, the man who was charged with stealing a 1 bicycle at Dareshury Sessions, on Tuesday, will be disposed to think so. Ile perhaps thought he was getting through his difficulties very cheaply when he heard the sentence, but whPn the costs were detailed as amounting to £2 13+. he would pro- bably change his opinion. By the way, what are "costs.'? Or, rather, I should say, where d 6 they go to? Do they go into some public fund and thus help to lighten the rates, or do they, for the most part, find theicway into the pocket of some official or other? In some courts they seem to expand with a facility that must alarm the poor defen- dant.</p>
        <p>'the Scotehman Donaldson who was given until mid-day to leave the town, by the Warrington I/migrates on Thursday. did not appear to be par- ticularly thankful for their offer. fie has got so used to getting lodgings rent free in -diffeWent parte of the country, that he probably could not under- stand the magistrates when they did not again give him a period of rest. His life since he left Scotland had apparent ly not been a happy one to judge from his statement to the police. To be put into Dudley Gaol, Denbigh Gaol, and carnarvon Gaol, successively, and each time for not finding other lodgings, must make him wish himself in bonnie Scotland again, away from the hard-hearted Southron. lie has, however, not had much time to trouble the inhabitants of this borough, as, after being in one day, he was accompanied to the boundary by a P.C., and there told to go else- where.</p>
        <p>The people of ‘larrington—especially the rate- payers—must feel gratified by the. evident desire there is amongst their representatives to gather knowledize—to be u4ed in the interests of the town. The agenda of business of the Council meeting to he held next Tuesday, is quite eloquent on that, pint. No fewer than five of the Commit- tees hare appsiuted deputations to e,tek enlighten- ment out of the town. The Schord Attendance Com- mittee will psy its annusl and time-honoured visit to the " Clio," on the day after tbe Royal Wedding. This Cimmittee does not make invidious selections; it goes ea mass., to the " Clio." Three of the other Committees send smaller deputations to Con- gresses and Associations, and the General Purposes Committee deputes a fairly numerous sub-commit- tee to inspect the Town !falls at St. Helens and Bootle. That, no doubt, is in view of the incipient scheme fer building municipal offices in Warring- ton. Nothing like being in time. But, wasn't it at 5!... Helens that they complained of the capacity of some Warrington councillors for piling up .the " entertaining bill.</p>
        <p>Tli e Women's Temperance Association of Great Britain, under the presidency of Lady Henry Somerset, has become a very flourishing and influential association, and has branches all dyer the kingdom Warrington, how- ever, lagged behind, but at length some temper- (um t ladies in the town put their heads together und decided to see what could be done to form a local branch. They accordingly invited Mrs.Shilton from Liverpool, and Mrs. Mather and Miss Finney from Manchester, on Wednesday, to explain the lines on which the association ran. I believe that the meeting was only intended to be an informal one, but those present go,. to business and actually formed a branch. Miss Bessie Dutton was asked to be president, but she had so many other engage- ments that she begged to be excused, and was made vice-president. Failing Miss Dutton, Mrs. Garnett was unanimously chosen, and she will without doubt make an excellent president, and will have good assistance from Miss Dutton and Mrs. John Yonge the vice-presidents. Now that the branch is formed it would receive a fine impetus if it could succeed in inducing Lady Henry Somer- set to visit Warrington. MERLIN.</p>
        <p>• THE OPIUM TPAPPIC. At an opeuzair meeting held in Warrington on Sunday it was decided so send a petition to Parlia- ment condemning the opium traffic in the East. The petition was signed on behalf of the Meeting by Mr. It. Storey, and it hag during the week been presented to Parliament by Mr. lt."Pierpoitt, M.P.,</p>
        <p>CURING BY ELECTRICITY. During the past month the well-known Medical Electrician, Mr. J. C. Miller, who for several months has visited and practised in Widnes. Runcorn and the neigh- bourhood, has occupied 4, Lyme-street, Warrington, where, sufferers of. long standing have under his skilful treatment received benefits and cures. Testi- • monials have poured into his possession from every quarter, many of which are from well-known local i persons. By his advertisement n another column may be seen testimonials ifrory such as those men- tioned. Mr. James Cran..-agan of 49,Bewsey-street, who has been deaf foi four or five years has, niter a few weeks compliance to Mr. Miller's treatment received great benefit. Mrs. Vernon of Aikin-street subscribes another testimonial, she having been cured of rheumatism in the hands and knees. 'There are many other cases we could mention, quite as extraordinary. Full particulars will he. found in our advertising columns.</p>
        <p>Wd.RRINGTON TOWN COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>At the monthly meeting of the Warrington Town Council, to be held next Tuesday evening, the fol- lowing minutes of committees will be submitted for consideration:—</p>
        <p>STAGE PLAYS. The Watch Committee recommend that licenses be granted to Messrs. Wm. Johnson and Jos. Potter, for the performance of stage plays in the Theatre Royal, Scotland-road, and in the Royal Court Theatre, Rylands-st.,for twelve months from the Bth day of June.</p>
        <p>NEW BUILDINGS, &amp;C. The street Improvements, &amp;c., Committee recom- mend that the plans of the following buildings,&amp;c., be approved :—Mission chapel in Sharp-et., for Mr. J. Mounfield, jun., seven cottages in Knutsford- road, for Mr. John White ; ten cottages in West-st., for Mr. Cross; five cottages in Longford-st.; for Mr. Cross; seven houses off Knutsford-road, for Mr. Richard Miller; four houses for 31esers. W. and J, Richardson, in Springfield-st. ; new warehouse and shed for the Longford Wire, Iron, and Steel Co., Limited.</p>
