      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>_ WARRINGTON BOARD OF GITARDI.A.NS.</head>
        <p>_ WARRINGTON BOARD OF GITARDI.A.NS.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the Warrington Board of Guardians was held at the Town hail, on Wednesday. There were then present: Mr. (J. Rat- cliffe (in the chair), and Messrs. it. Heaton. W. lioughton, E. Aukland, W. Balharry, J. Clarke, J. Pobson, J. Hill, J. Houghton, A. Jolley, II J. Long- ton, J. Muir, It. Owen, O. Pilling, J. Turton, W. J. Shaw, and Dr. Patterson; also Messrs. J. C. Sutton (clerk), S. Sutton (deputy clerk), C. 11. Ward (master), 11. M. Mines (superintendent, Padgate Schools). TIIR ROYAL WEDDING.</p>
        <p>The CLERK read a circular from the Lon! Government Board asking the Union to make certain modifications on the 6th July, with regard to the discipline of the poor persons in the House, and also such substitutionof better provisions as the Guardians might think fir.—A discus- sion followed upon this point, and it was understood that the Union had no power to expend money upon anything save the special fool.—Mr. lIRATQN said "leaving to the discretion' of the Union seemed an absurdity in the circular, for they were only allowed to use their discretion in a matter which was pretty well cut and dried for them. (Hear, hear.) Most other Unions were giving the inmates a trip or soinething special to commemorate the wedding, and it 'seemed to him a great pity that they could not do likewise.—Mr. DANIEs said they had had in mind something of the character Mr. Heaton had suggested, but as their Clerk decided that they could not incur the expense, lie could only say that it was a very, very hard case fur the in- mates.—The CLERK e7cplained that in the towns Mr. Heaton had alluded to where an extensive treat was being given to the inmates, the expense was being borne by the public.—Mr. HEATON : Well, what I suggest is that as the Local Government Board have left it to our discretion' we give them a treat.—The CrAma : Of course the Lecal Government Board point out that your discre- tion is to be used simply in the relaxation of discipline, and in providing better provisions.—This qoestion was left over to the Committee.—ln reading the minutes of the School Committee the Clerk read a resolution from that Contnittee asking the Board's sanction to the itsß ex- penditurespo pointed (1, 5u t for the iisn resolutiongtle wilroeunl da haves toTbee ' eliminated, as it was entirelriliegai.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>TAILORS SCr.RCE.</head>
        <p>TAILORS SCr.RCE.</p>
        <p>The CLERK read a resolution drawn up by the llmse Committee,recommending that an advertisement should be pat in one of the Manchester papers and another in a technical paper, advertising for a tailor for the house. This, it was stated, was in consequence of the bird getting no local applicants for the position.—Mr. Snaw expressed indignation at the system at present iii vogue. How could they expect to get applicants, he asked 2 They compelled the tailor and his wife to live 021 the premises, and act as foster parents to the children, and if there was any addition to the tailor's family he had to leave. It seemed to him that this ought to he remedied.. —The CLF4I7IL poicted out that they could engage a tailor to do the work and yet live out of the house.—Mr. Mum Wll,this Is extraordinary. It is the first tame to my recollection that this Board has been infirmed that it might engage a tailor to live out of the house to do the • work.—Mr. SEWS What ! Is that---Mr. Mom : You be good enough to sit cl)wn, sir, arid do not interrupt me. As 1 was going to remaik,the matter is a most extraordinary one, and in Winging it up I think Mr. Sinew has shown his Wisdom. (Laughter.)— An amendment was moved by Mr. 'Shaw, ro the-effect that the advertisement be placed in the local papers,and stating that the tailor could live out of the house, and that a separate foster mother be engaged.—This amend- ment was rejected. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>Ex-OFFICIO 3IEMBERS.</head>
        <p>Ex-OFFICIO 3IEMBERS.</p>
        <p>-- -- The CLERK said he had received a COM mnnication from another union containing a rasoltition protesting aeainst the abolition of ex-officio members by the Parish Council' Bill, and asking the Board to endorse the resolution.— The CLERK said that so far as Warrington Union and most other unions were concernatl,the ex-officio members were of no use whatever now.—Mr. bfcia !,:ivc it as his opinion that the ex-officio members shoul7l vot be con- tinue.l.—lt was resolved to let the communication lie on the table.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>CORPORATION PROPERTY.</head>
        <p>CORPORATION PROPERTY.</p>
        <p>The Street Improvement &amp;c., Cwannittee, have re- solved that the application of Mr. R. W. Marsh, for the renewal of his tendency of the premises occupied by him at Bridge Foot be granted, subject to three months' notice on either side to terminate the tenancy ;and that the Town Clerk give notice to all the tenants of pre- miere other than cottages purchased for street improve- ments to terminate their rebpective tenancies in order to, create new tenancies, subject to three months' notice.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>SLAUGHTER HOUSE.</head>
        <p>A SLAUGHTER HOUSE.</p>
        <p>The Health Committee propose that a license under the Publicllealth Acts Amendment Act,lB9o, be granted to Jacob Hewitt, of 12, Watkin-st., to slaughter cattle in premises in yard behind the British Tar.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>MEDICAL SETPBRINTBSDENT OF THE SMALLPDX</head>
        <p>THE MEDICAL SETPBRINTBSDENT OF THE SMALLPDX</p>
        <p>HOSPITAL.</p>
        <p>The Committee upon the termination of the appoint- ment of Dr. G Guest Gornall as Medical Superinten- dent of ttn Smallpox Hospital and Assistant Medical Offic r of FlealLl), have much pleasure in recording their warm admiration of his excellent and untiring services during the recent epidemic. The Committee further de- bite to express their approval of the valuable and most comprehensive report presented by Dr. Gornall on the outbreak.</p>
        <p>THE PAVING OF BRIDGE-STREET. The Paving Ste.,Committee have resolved that Bridge- • street, from Friars Gate to the nortl; side of Bylatads. street, be paved with 6 by 4 specially dressed setts. THE FOREMAST SCAVENGER. The Paving Committee have resolved that Joseph Whit field be appointed Foreman Scavenger at 303. per week.</p>
        <p>THE SCHOOL ATTENDANCE. In his report to the School Attendance Committee the Inspector says the average attendance at the public ' elementary schools, during the month of May, was, ,403; corresponding period last year 8,460; decrease,s7. GIFTS TO THE MUSEUM. The Museum Committee reports the following gifts : Piece of flexible sandstone from Agra, India ; from Councillor F. W. Monks; collection of American mosses. arranged and named by Mr. IVilliain Wilson : (145 species), from Mr. Robert Davies ; insects (348 speci- mens), from Mr. Kendrick; compound cocoon of moth from Chanak, Turkey, from Mr. George Ellison ; Parlia- mentary reports and other books (18 vols.), from Mr. It. Pierpoint, M.P. The Committee has decided that in future discarded books may be given to the local hospitals.</p>
        <p>TEACHERS FOR THE SCHOOL OF ART. The Museum Committee have resolved that the fol- lowing teachers for the School of Art ha appointed : Miss A. Chrimes, assistant teacher, £35 for session ; Miss B. Lees, pupil teacher, £l5 for session ; Miss Laverock, teacher of needlework, ss. per lesson.</p>
        <p>THE CEMETERY. From the report of the Registrar to the Cemetery Com- mittee it appeared that the interments for the past four weeks were—adults 45 • minors, 68; stillborn and pre- mature, 12. Corresponding period last year—adults, 43 ; minors, 49 ; stillborn • and premature, 7. The re- ceipts for the past four weeks were £93 18s. 3d. ; corre- sponding period last year, £75 is. 21.</p>
        <p>TRU BATHS: GREAT INCREASE OF INCOME. From the report of the bathkeeper te the Town Hall, &amp;c., Committee, it appeared that the income from the baths for the past four weeks was £255 Is. 51. ; and for the: corresponding period last year, £43 ss. The Committee have decided that the season tickets be issued to the members of the police force at ss. each, and that the charge for single admis- siens be al. each ; also that arrangements be made for the admission of school children to one of the public baths on Friday and Saturday afternoon, from 2 to 8 o'clock, at the charge of one penny. each, including the use of one towel ; the children in all cases to be in charge of the teacher or other competent person.</p>
        <p>TEM PARE AND BRASS BIND CONTESTS. The Town Bail Committee report that a deputation from the Workingmen's Committee of the Warrington Infirmary attended, and asked for the privilege of using part of the recreation ground for a brass band contest, • the park.to be closed to the public, and a charge for admission made in aid of the funds of the Infirmary. The Committee promised to take the opinion of the Council on Gls matter, and informed the deputation that If permission were granted it would be subject to the programme of the proceedings being first submitted to and approved by the Committee. TUE MCNICIFAL OFFICES. The General Purposes Committee recommend' that • premium of £25 he offered for the third selected compe- titive drawing of the new Municipal Offices. ' wARRiNoToN AND ELEcnocrTy. The General Purposes Committee have resolved that the Town Clerk's London agent be instructed to attend the Joint Committee of the Lords and Commons now sitting to inquire into the provisions to be inserted in Bills for the protection of Telephone and Electric Light- ing Companies, and to watch the interests of Warring- ton so far as they may be affected by the proposed pro- tective clauses, widen unless watched may seriously interfere with the use of eledricity for tramways, lighting, and other purposes. TENDERS ACCEPTED. The following tenders have been accepted by the Corn- ' mittees named Sanitary Works Committee : Of Messrs. E. immins and Sons, Limited, to sink a well and guarantee a continuous supply of water of 4,000 gallons per hour, at the Longford I'Verks, at an estimated cost of £237 10s., subject to the terms of an agreement to be prepared by the Town Clerk ; and of Mr. Jac,&gt;b Sankey, to purchase stable manure at 3s. 61. per ton for the en- suing twelvemonths, be accepted. The Town Hall and Baths Committee : Of Mr. J. Smith to supply 1,000 bath towels at Rd. each, and 250 drawers at std. each; and of Messrs. Wm. Jones and Sons to repair No. 2 boiler in Mersey-at. at £42 105.</p>
        <p>TRIPS AND DEPUTATIONS. The School Attendance Committee have resolved that the members of the committee visit the training ship "Clio" on the 7th July, and the Boys' Refuge Industrial School at Liverpool on the 11th July next. The Health Committee propose that the chairman (or in the event of his inability to attend, the deputy-chairman) and the Medical Officer of Health be appointed to attend the annual Congress of the British Institute of Public Health, to beheld in Edinburgh from the 27th July to Ist August. The Sanitary Works Committee have decided that the deputy-chairman and Mr. Deas be appointed to repre- sent this Committee at the annual congress of the British Institute of Public Ilealth.—The Museum Com- mittee proNse that Mr. Roberts and the Curator be appointed delegates to the hlusonms Association.—The General Purposes Committee had decided that the following members be appointed a sub-Committee to inspect the Town Halls at St. Helens and Bootle, viz The Mayor,Alderinen Chandley, aud Wright, Councillors R. T. Faircicugh, Roberts, Heaton, and the Borough Surveyor</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON SWIMMING CLUB. The Snretary reported to a meeting on Monday night that the membership roll had reached O. Ti• annual subscription was fixed at 23. fid., and the club night every Thursday evening. It was also resolved to join the Northern Counties Amateur Swimming Association, for the purpose of obtain- ing the many advantages of the association when holding galas. The Secretary submitted a draft of the rules of the club, which were approved: and it was decided to make arrangements for a swimming gala at an early date. It was also resolved to invite Air. Alderman Chandley to become president of the - club, and the members of the Towu Hall, Park and Baths Committee, vice-presidents. The above pro- ceedings will be brought before the general meeting of the club to be held on IVeduesday, st.h July, for confirmation. ,</p>
        <p>BIIRTONWOOD SCHOOL BOARD. The usual meeting of the above Board was held in the class-room of the Bard Sehools,Burtonwood, on Tuesday evening, when there were present, Messrs. James Warburton (chairman), Thomas Perks, Thomas Norcott, William Pairclough, John Forster (at tendance officer), H. Clawthorpe (school master), William Webster (clerk). The correspond- ence since the last meeting at which there was a quorum, April 4Th, was read and discussed, and it was resolved not to join the Association of School Boards. The resignation of Miss Haslam as candid- ate for pupil teacher was accepted, and the school- master instructed to find a suitable candidate to supply the vacancy in the school etaff caused thereby. The form authorising the Education Department to pay the fee grant to tba head office of Parra Bank in London was signed. The follow- ing receipts—sale of books lls. 2d., and overseers' contribution £loo—were ordered to be debited inthe cash book. Accounts and salaries amounting to £72 9s. were passed for payment. The school master reported that the epidemic of measles had caused a falling off in the attendance. •</p>
        <p>CHARGE AGAINST A SOLDIER. Edward Carney was charged at the Borough Police Court on Saturday, before Mr. John Burgess (in the chair), and Mr. L. Voisey, with stealing from the barack room at Orford Barracks, about 8 o'clock on Wednesday, two military shirts value 9s. 6d., the pro- perty of the military authorities. From the evience of the Chief Constable it appeared that the prisoner be- longed to the 4th Liv:rpool Regiment. On IVednesday evening Carney was seen by a young recruit named E. Worthington putting some shirts on, two besides the one he was wearing. Ha told another soldier named Joseph Jones what he had seen, and Jones immediately went to his kit and found that one of his shirts had been taken. Alfred Hennessy Llso found that one of his shirts had been taken from his kit. The prisoner returned to the Barracks drunk the same evening. When charged by Detective Monaghan with stealing the shirts he replied " I never saw them." The shirts had not yet been re- covered. Joseph Jones said he followed the prisoner up to Liverpool-road, where he saw him enter a public. house. He went in the public-house after him, but the publican said he had gone as he had refused to serve him as he was drunk. After evidence by Alfred Hennessy and Provost-Sergeant Stacey who said the prisoner had admitted selling one of his own shirts, Chief Constable Hunt asked for a remand until Tuesday which was granted. On Tuesday further evidence was given by Private Hughes who said that he was in the barrack room with Private Carney who was the worse for drink. He was out for only five minutes, and never saw Carney with the shirts. Private Worthington came into the room before Carney went out, and therefore could not have seen him put the shirts on. The magis- trates dismissed the case as there was not sufficient evidence to prove prisoner's guilt. HE WA.S GOING TO PAY NEXT MOUSING. At the Warrington Police Court on Monday, James Shaw, 9, Orchard-street, was charged with stealing from the Market Place on Friday, four lbs. of butter and an handkerchief valued 4g.—Christian Bowers, butcher, in the Market Hall said that the butter was left by some- one in a handkerchief at his shop on the Friday in the week previous to the theft. On the 24th inst. the butter was missed and the matter was reported to Detective Monaghan.—The detective said that he received informa- tion which threw suspicion upon defendant and he went to the house. When asked about the butter, defendant very straightforwardly gave up the butter. When charged with the theft he said that drink was the cause of it.—Defendant at the court said he intended to pay for the butter the next morning.—The magistrates fined defendant 103. and costs or 14 days' imprisonment. OFFERED THEM • " LIFT." At the Warrington Borough Court on Monday, Patrick Hanlon, 8, Halifax-street, Alice Rums, Oliver-street, and Betsy Ryan, 15, Dolman's-lane, were each charged with stealing troin the warehouse of James Toole, on Saturday afternoon, six bags valued Is. Alice Burns was also charged with assaulting DetectiveMonaghan at the satne time and place. The Chief Constable said that coin- plainant was a well-known marine attire dealer in Dolman's-lane, and employed the two female prisoners. The male prisoner was a well-known character and was at one time a marine store dealer.—Detective Monaghan said that Inspector Cox and he were coming from the direction of the "Three Pigeons" when they saw Hanlon with a bag on his back. When they got to Dolman's-lane, they saw Burns helping him to put the bag, which was filled with articles, on his back. When Hanlon saw the officers he dropped the bag. Monaghan, however, said " Hold on and I'll give you a lift," but when he got to them, he asked them to give them an account of what they were doing. The prisoner Ryan was also there. Monaghan then kept charge of prisoners while Inspector Cox went for Mr. Toole. While that officer was away, Burns took hold of a bag and gave Monaghan a hit in the face, filling his eyes with lime dust. On Inspector Cox's return, the prisoners were taken to the police-station.—Mr. Toole, who was called, said that Hanlon had no right to be in the shop at all.— The magistrates sent Hanlon to gaol for 14 days, Burns 14 days for the theft, and seven days for the assault,and Ryan was fined 2s. 6d. EXCITING CHASE AFTER A RAILWAY PASSENGER. Francis fluster, 5, Charlotte-street, Widnes, was brought before the Warrington magistrates on Monday, on the charge of travelling from ‘Vidnes to Warrington on the L. and N.-W. Railway without ticket on Saturday night. —Ticket-collector Bennett said that he was on duty on the low-level on Saturday night, collecting the tickets from the 10.45 p.m. train from Lime-street. He noticed prisoner and another man get out of a third class compartment. Whilst collecting tickets, he over- heard the other man say to prisoner " You are a fool if phi do pay." When he had finished collecting tickets he noticed the two men walk up the platform. Witness followed and saw them go out at Slutcher's-lane Cross-</p>
        <p>-- TUESDAY.—Before Mr. R. Garnett (chairman), Mr. E. J. Guest, and Dr. Starkey Smith. SUSPICIOUS CHIARACTER.—JOIM McLoughlin was charged with being a suspicious character, and with fre- quenting the Market Place on Monday, with intent to commit a felony. P.C. Hollingsworth said that about four o'clock on Monday, Inspector Place drew his atten- tion to the prisoner who had been moving in and out among the women at a sale that was held in the Market Place. his movements being very suspicious. Inspector Place said the prisoner appeared to be trying to pick pockets, but he did not see anything taken.—Mary Paw- son and Benjamin Jefferies, also gave corroborative evi- dence, the former stating that the auctioneer was told about the prisoner, and when he warned the people, pri- soner moved away. Inspector Dc,wty said that he had frequently seen prisoner following women in the market on Wednesdays; lie hid a peculiar kind of movement,as though he was finding where the pockets were. The magistrates considered the case to be a very suspicious one, but discharged the prisoner, taking into considera- tion his family. DESERTER.—John Wilson was remanded to Strange- ways Gaol, he being a deserter since 1885, from the East Surrey Regiment stationed at Kingston-on-Thames. SLEEPING IN AN OCT BCILDING.—JOSepiI Ellison and Ellen Coyne, were sent for a month's imprisonment, for sleeping in an out building in Rose Yard on Tuesday morning, without any visible means of subsistence. A Yountrut DELINQUENT.—Thomas Hands, aged 12 years, was charged with stealing 7s. 63. in money, the property of I homas Morris, on Sunday morning. The Chief Constable said the boy was an orphan, and lived with Thomas Morris, who was his uncle. The money was stolen out of a box in the bed room of Mrs. Sheldon who lives at the same house at Ileathside. Considering the youth of the defendant, the magistrates discharged him, but recorded a conviction against 11;in. SCHOOL CASES.—The following were fined for neglect- ing to send their children to school :—Jolin Fiddler, Brow, ss. ; and Wm. Lomax, Glazebrook-st., Thomas Lusby, Dial-st., John Naylor, Naylor-t., Patric k Sheen- ing, School Brow, Peter Smith, Dial-et., and Elizabeth Wilson, Tilley-St., were each fined 23. 63. Mr. George Riley prosecuted.</p>
        <p>RICH, ME, AND FRAGRANT 1 S ec 1 s 4d. PER LB.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="0" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
