      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>UNIVERSAL BOON.</head>
        <p>A UNIVERSAL BOON.</p>
        <p>It seldom that any discovery of science is such as will I 'benefit all &amp;Ewes of people. Some of the greatest discaveries of the age, while proving beneficial to thousands, have also at least for a time, injured other thousands. By, n some Of the best medicinal preparations, however, much they may do to remove one class of disease or suit some constitutions, at the same time either deepen the hold of another disease, or leave it entirely untouched, or, if they benefit one class of patients they may injure another. Now,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>GREAT NEED OF THE AGE.</head>
        <p>THE GREAT NEED OF THE AGE.</p>
        <p>Is some scientifically compounded preparation which will effectually cope with the prevalent diseases of this country. which will be certain to do good when fairly tried, which will be equally adapted to the needs of the merchant, the profPs- sional man, and he who wins his bread by the sweat of his brow, the clerk, th- factory hand, the miner, the workers in metals, and the roadside labourers. It sh, uld also be such a preparation as contains no injurious ingredients. and which may be taken with impunity by the weak and sickly child or the delicate female, as well as by the stronger constituted mats. Such a preparation would indeed be</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>BOON TO HUMANITY.</head>
        <p>A BOON TO HUMANITY.</p>
        <p>After long-continued and diligent research, this much- needed boon has been found, and repeatedly tested by analysts, who declare it to be pure and harmless. This desirable preparation is that wellknown Vegetable Tonic called Gwilym EVRIIs. Quinine Bitters, and it has gained such a widespread reputation for efficacy that many unprejudiced doctors, who have given it a fai: trial, frequently recommend It to their patients in cases of great Weakness. Nervousness, or any disorder of the digestive organs. Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters Is so invariably efficacious and produces such satisfactory results, that when once tried it is always recommended. It has proved successful when all other medicines had failed o give relief. It has also in many cases been found to be perm inently beneficial when other preparations at best only give temporary relief. Gwilym E 7ans' Bitters is purely vegetable, and contains neither _ _ _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>MERCURY NOR IRON.</head>
        <p>MERCURY NOR IRON.</p>
        <p>_ nor any Toisonous or other injurious substances, and thou- sands who Nye benefitted by its we agree in saying it is a specific for all symptoms of Indigestion. Nervousness. and Weakness. It is also confidently recommended fur Low Spirits Liver Disorders. and Chest Affections. The unparalleled success of Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters has induced unprincipled persons to offer base and spurious Imitations, somewhat similar in name and appearance. but possessing none of the virtues of this great national remedy. Remember that none are genuine unless the name Gwilym Evans is on label. stamp, and bottle. Sold by all Chemists, in bottles, at Is. lid., 23. 9d. and Is. 6.1. each, or may be obtained, carriage free, direct from the Proprietors :—Quinine Bitters Manufacturing Company. Limited. Llanelly, South Wales.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</head>
        <p>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>Only</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="2" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <p>logs into Wilson Patten-street. Witness caught up to them, and they theta inquired the way to Sankey Bridges. Witness told them he had come for the railway tickets. They refused to give him any and he blew his whistle. No one came,however, and the two men ran away. Witness ran after them and caught them in Bold-street. He again asked them for their fares, but Hunter said be would not pay a halfpenny. The other man escaped, but witness held prisoner fast, and took him towards Market Gate where he met P.C. Dean. In the meantime a crowd had gathered and someone on hearing the cause of the dispute gave prisoner Bd. ,which prisoner then offered wit- ness. Witness, however, refused to take it as lie had come so far. Prisoner said that he would know the ticket collector as,rain and he would do him one another time. On bring searched, a return half of a ticket from Widnes to Liverpool, was found in prisoner's possession. It was a common practice of persons to get the return tickets from Widnes to Liverpool and then say they were over-carried.—The magistrates fined prisoner 10s. and costs or 14 days for the attempt todefraud the company and also ordered him to pay the fare or in default seven days' imprisonment. THE THEFT OF I BICYCLE. John Mulvery, a discharged soldier, was charged at Darcsbury Petty Sessions with stealing a bicycle, the property of Dr. Parker, Stoclton Heath, on the sth June. Dr. S. J. Parker said he had been to Latchford hospital, and left his bicycle at the door of his house. He missed it about an hour afterwards. Thomas Broady and Joseph Hankev of St.ocktan Heath deposed to seeing the prisoner walking with the machine along the New-road. P.C. Brew said he took the prisoner into custody, and in reply to the charge said " All right you will have to prove it." The prisoner in his defence, said he did not intend to take the bicycle. He had had some drink and it had affected his head. He had been discharged from the army. He bore a previous good character. The magistrates fined him ss. and the costs 13s. Bd. BREACHES -OF THE HIGHWAYS ACTS. At Daresbury Petty Sessions on Tuesday, William Brookes, owner of a locomotive travelling on the high- way, was charged with allowing the steam to blow off unnecessarily in the public highway on the 9th May, at Whitley. Mr. A. Browne prosecuted on behalf of Mr. I. Marson. He said on the 9th May, Mr. Marson was out driving when he meta locomotive standing in the high- way at Raddle-lane, Whitley, letting off steam. Mr. Marion called to the driver to turn off the steam, but the driver did not do so. Mr. Marson went to him, and soon afterwards the driver of the engine stopped the steam. Mr. Marson was kept waiting in the road for five minutes. The horses became very frisV; and very hard to manage because they were frightened by the steam. David Meach, coachman for Mr. Marion, cor- roborated Mr. Browne's statement. Mr. J. Swinton, surveyor for the Highway Board said the engine stopped opposite his house. Several horses went past at the time and they became very frisky. He went to the driver and told him he would have to keep the steam down. The magistrates fined the owner 203. and ccsts. William Johnson, Warrington, was charged with driv- ing a locomotive engine at Walton on the 17th May, after the hours prescribed viz., 7 a.m., to? p.m. P.C. Shetwell said he met the defendant with his engine draw- ing three waggons of switchback railway and several other waggons at 8.20 on the 17th May. The defendant said he had been unable to get a place to stop at. He (the constable) asked Mr.Broady of the Wal- ton Arms to let them put up which he did. Ile had since learnt that they strewed their cinders all over the yard, and went off the next morning without paying anything. He was fined 20s. and costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>MINOR CASES.</head>
        <p>MINOR CASES.</p>
        <p>WEDNESDAY.—Before Mr. John Pairclough (in the chair) and Councillor Fleming. DRUNH AND Disciansom—Martin Curley was charged with being drunk and disorderly in Arpley-road. He was fined 5.3. and costs or seven days. THURSDAY.—Before Alderman Chandley (chairman), Councillor James Fairclough and Captain Ridgeway. AN UNFORTUNATE SCOUCIIMAN.—John Donaldson was charged with being a suspicious character, frequenting Palmyra Square at 11-30 on the previous night. Pri- soner was seen by P.C. Clough walking about in a very suspicious manner in Palmyra Square on the previous night. When the officer went to him he refused to give any account of hirnself,and he was brought to the police station, and on being searched, his pockets were found to be full of pieces of dirty bread. He then told Inspector Spinks that he belonged to Edinburgh, but came into England where he has had lodgings successively in Dud- ley Gaol, Denbigh Goal, and Carriarvon Gaol, for sleeping out. He had walked from Chester to Warring- ton on Wednesday. and wanted t get work. His hands, however, showed that he had done no hard work re- cently. P.C. Lewis had cautioned him the former part of:the day for begging in Knutsford-road. The magis- trates decided to allow prisoner until 12 o'clock t) clear out of the town. DESEUTER.—WaiIor flumphries,was convicted of being a deserter from the East Lancashire Regiment stationed at Warrington, and was handed over to the military authorities. Detective Monaghan who captured him, received 10s. reward. BODELIA IN THE COURT AGAlN.—Bodelia Knockton, the well-known police couit character, was again alter two days' freedom, sent to gaol for 14 days for being drunk and disorderly on the previous night in Erwood-at. MAINTENANCE ORDER.—Thomas Bellis, 80, Battersby• lane, was ordered to pay 2s. 61. per week towards the maintialance of his mother, and was also ordered to pay the cog of the summons. WARRANTS.—Warrants were granted for the apprehen- sion of James McCarthy, Warburton-St., for committing an assault on his wife, Mary Ellen McCarthy,on the 19th ult.,and for Wm.Curley for assaulting his inother,Bridget Curley, on the 23rd ult.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN PETTY SESSIONS. AIONDAY.—Before Mr. Jas. Handley and Mr. G. L. Davies. TEIIPOEARY AUTTIORITY.—James H. Percival was granted permission to sell at the Mersey Vaults under the license lately held by Thos. Davies. DRUNRENNESS.—the fillowing were fined for this cffence :—Mary Woolgar, Anne Jane Massey, John Green and Catherine Bowden. TitErrs.—Daniel Massey (14), Wm. White (13), and John T, Price, were convicted of stealing 12 pairs of bathing draws value 35., belonging to Mr. Jabez Unsworth, hosier and clothier, High-st. Massey and White who had previously been before the court, was each sentenced to receive six strokes with the birch rod. Price, on the application of the prosecutor, was dis- charged with a caution. SLEEPING Our.—John Christie and Ellen Coffey, were each sentenced to seven days hard labour fc.r sleeping , out.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOROUGH POLICE COURT. MONDAY.—Before Mr. J. Fairclough (chairman), Alder- man Harrison and Messrs. F. Monks, T. Birchall, W. Pierpoint and J. W. Hayward. DRUNK AND DISORDERLY.—WiIIiaM LSOM.,,ne, 48 Dallam Lane, ss. and costs or seven days ; John Kelly, 17 Lower Ship Yard, 53. or seven days' imprisontnent ; Peter Smith, Backhouse Yard, Foundry-St., 53. including costs. RUFFIANISM IN WILDEDSPOOL ROAD.—Peter Knight, 40 Tanners Lane, Patrick Carey, [tack Dolman's Lane, and Patrick Thomson, 4 Back Haydock-st., were each charged with being drunk and disorderly and also with smashing a 'bus window the property of Edward Connor, on the previous night.—P.C. Woods said that be recf ived a' complaint from one of Connor's drivers named Mitchell, that the three prisoners had smashed a window of the , 'bus. Ho took the three into custody near the football I field. They began to swear, and wanted to fight Mitchell. They also gave the wrong names when asked, and witness krought them to the police station with the assistance of P.C. Meacock.—Mitchell said that he had been with a party to Chester. On stopping at the Saracen's Head, the three prisoners got inf and were very violent when asked to get out again, and broke a win- dow of the 'bus.—The prisoners were each fined ss. and 'oats or seven days' imprisonment. FIGHTING.—For this offence Annie Speakman, 59 Foundry-St., and Sarah Millar, 26 Foundry-st., were ordered to pay the cost of the summor.q, and William Smith, 7 Buckley-St., was dismissed. OBSTRUCTION OF TUE ROAD—For allowing a quantity of coal to remain in Horsemarket-at. on the 23rd inst., Ann Kershaw, Blue Bell Inn, was fined 2s. 6d. and costs. TIIROWINO'STONES.—For throwing stones in Winmar- leigh-st. on the 17th inst., Alfred Junes, Legh-st., was fined 3s. 6d. _ _</p>
        <p>IN'IDNES PETTY SESSIONS. THURSDAY.—Before Messrs. H. Wade Deacon (chairman'</p>
        <p>and G. Pilkington. FRANSFERS.—ThGmas Edwardson was granted a tem- porary authority to sell at the Clap Gate Hotel, Ditton, under the license lately held by Ann Taylor, and John Tollit was granted temporary authority to sell at the Volunteer Arms, Lugsclale-road, under the license lately held by Thomas Edwardson. ADULTERATED MiLx.—James Gildin, Lugsdale-road, was summoned under the Foods and Drugs Act, with selling milk which the public analyst certified contained eight parts of water to every 100th parts of the poorest milk. A fine of El and El is. Bd. costs was imposed, Cite chairman remarking that the Bench looked upon adulteration of milk as a very serious offence, because a number of people, especially children, had to live on it. P.C. Thompson proved the case. DRCNKENNESS.—The following persons were fined for the above offence : Win. Beasley, jun., Croft-st. ; Ber- nard Cowley, Warrington-road ; Bridget Mitchell Moore, Caroline-St. ; Catherine Owen, Elizabeth-st. ; Ann Walsh, Oxford-st. ; Mary Burns, Walmsle:--st., and John Ryan, Brides-st., on three charges. BREACHES OF .rnE PEACE—For disturbing the peace the following persons were bound over :—Bartholomew I Cunningham, Caroline-st. ; Ann Daley, Caroline-st. ; Catherine Mathew, Nelson-st.; Catherine Murphy, Well- ; ington-st. ; Margaret Gleeson, Barn-st. ; and Elizabeth ' Stanley,Beamont-st.,were ordered to pay costs for similar offences. FURIOUS DRIVING.—Henry Sterrett, Mersey-road, pleaded guilty to furiously driving a horse and trap on the 19th inst. He drove at the rate of 12 or 14 miles an hour, and was thrown out of the trap and rendered un- conscious. Fined 10s. and costs. PITCH AND Toss.—For this offence Richard Lofthouse, Saxon Terrace ; Patrick Douglas, Catherine-st. ; Joseph Whitky, ; Jas. Vale, Eleanor-st. : Thos. Hogan, Gerrard-St. ; John W. Keenaghan, Widnes-road ; Samuel Smith, Gerrard-st. ;Joseph Mulligan, Midwood-st. ; and Frederick Temperley, Post Office Entry, were each fined is. and costs. Richard McLeed, Thomas Murphy, and • Peter White, all of St. Michael's Terrace, Ditton, were . dealt with in a similar manner for a like offence. • DRUNK.—Catherine Sinnott, of no fixed address, was I charged with being drunk while_ in charge of a horse and cart.</p>
