        <p>Total 100 Total WARRINGTON V. WERNETIL—The Warrington olub missed the services of Messrs. R. Smith and W. H. Pod- more on Saturday, when they suffered defeat at the hands of Werneth. Warrington can ill afford at present to lose the services of such capable batsmen, but with the Warrington captain being engaged at Manchester against the Oxford University, the visitors to the Old- ham suburb could scarcely he called representative. Starting the batting the Messrs. Crosfield first repre- sented Warrington, the -bowling being' shared by Hall and Hodgkinson. With only four runs on J. J. Crosfield was completely beaten by Hodgkinson, and although Sugg improved matters, the score had only reached 19 when an alipeal or lbw was answered against Mr. S. M. Crosfield. Sugg returned one to Hodgkinsen at the same total, and with George Clarke and Clark together, the players were compelled to beat a hasty retreat, rain falling heavilv. A delay of half an hour occurred, and on resuming the two batsmen put on runs at a rapid pace. The half century was quickly hoisted, but two minutes later Bedford took the ball from Hodgkinson. At 57 Clark called for a short run but his partner failed to respond, the icket having ;teen responsible for 38 runs. Clark's contribution was 20, made up by sound cricket. Two runs later Clarke was smartly taken at the wicket, and the remaining batsmen failing signally before the deliveries of Hall and Bedford, the venture realized the paltry total of 85. Werneth for six wick- ets ran up 137. • WARRINGTOY. WI:WO/TH. 31 Crosfield lbw b Ha 11... 3 .7 Parkes aJJ Crosfield b I 37 Crosield b Hodgkin- Bugg son 2 A Lowe)) 8 M Crosfield... 0 Suggc aed b Hodgkinson 12 Bedford c Parry b Snag— 23 G Clarke it Thomas b Bed- Hall c Clarke bSM Cros- - ford , Clark run out 2O S Haggas bJ Crosield... 23 H It ymids b Hall 40* IL A Volsey b Bedford Jefferson b Hall 1 G Braddock not out P 8 Tvler not out 1 .7 Wilson not out 23 B W Pletry b Bedford 3 EWoodblfsll 0 Nau