        <head>DARESBURY PETTY SESSIONS..</head>
        <p>DARESBURY PETTY SESSIONS..</p>
        <p>TIIESDAY.—Before Mr. W. Long (in the chair), Messrs. H. Neihi, and H. Talbot. Don OWNERS FINED.—For keeping dogs without licenses, Mary Ann Campden, at Preston Brook on the 4th May was fined the costs 3s. 6d., and Moses Little- more, boatman, at Preston Brook on the 25th April. 2s. 6d. and costs. Thomas Bland, boatman, was fined the costs 4s. 6d. for allowing his dog to be at large at Preston Brook on • the 22nd April, without having his name engraved OR the collar. For a similar offence on the Bth June, at Antrobus, John Brocklehurst was dis- missed on payment of costs 3s. 6d. SLEEPY DRlVERS.—Frederick Sankey, employed at Wilderspool Brewery, was charged with being asleep whilst in charge of a horse and cart at Walton Superior on the 7th June. He was fined 53. including costs.— Abraham Joynson, employed by Messrs. Rigby and Sons Ifelsbv Mills, was wlered to pay the costs 43. 6d. for the same offence in Runcorn-road, Bartington on the 15th June. BREACH OF THE FACTORY ACT.—Messrs. Pierpoint and Bryant, of Latchford, were charged with having in their , employ, a youth, 16 years of age, named James Jones, ! residing at 567, Knutsford-road, Latchford, without 1 having obtained a medical certificate, and having failed ! to enter his name in the register, as prescribed by the Factory Act. A representative of the firm admitted the offence, but said it was an sversight, and was not inten- tional. The case was dismissed on payment of costs lie. J. DRUNK AND DISORDECLT.--JR MCS Garrity, a farm labourer, was charger with being drunk and disorderly at Appleton on the 16th August, 1891. The else was brought up in August 1291, but the defendant left the district and failed to appear. He had recently com- menced to work for Mr. Ditchfield, Appleton Thorn, and was arrested that morning. He was fined 158. including costs.</p>
