        <head>LAWN TENNIS.</head>
        <p>LAWN TENNIS.</p>
        <p>NORTHERN v. LATCHFORD.</p>
        <p>A team of Northern players consisting oi N. Meßand. P. Schill, G. King, and F. W. H. Cottam, played a match at Old Trafford, on Saturday, against R. Crossley, G. R. Williamson, T. P. Williamson, and the Rev. F. C. Timins, representing the Latchford Club. Results :—N. Melland (N) beat F. C. Timins-9 7, 6 0; 'l'. P. Williamson (L) beat P. Schill-7 5, 63; G. R. Williamson (L) beat G. King-4 6, 64, 8 ;F.H. W. Cottam (N) beat L. Cress- ley-7 5, 61. The home club, therefore, although they had only two matches of the four to their credit, were 5 sets to 4 of their opponents' in the singles, and 52 games to 44. The double matches also went slightly in favour of the home team. First round: G. R. and T. P. William- son (L) beat G. King and F. H. W. Cottam-6 2, 7 5; W. Melland and P. Schill (N) beat F. C. Timins and L. Crossley-8 6, 36. 97. Second round: W. Melland and P. Schill (N) beat GR. and T. P. Williamson-6 3, 63; G. King and F. H. W. Cottam (N) beat F. C. Timing and L. Crossley-8 4, 64. The Northern team won three of the four matches,,securing six sets to three, and 51 games to 46. The match totals show a defeat of Latch- ford by 5 matches to 3, 11 sets to 7, and 103 games to 90.</p>
        <p>ST. HELENS FOOTBALL CLUB.</p>
        <p>IMPROVED CONDITION OF AFFAIRS.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the St. Helens Rugby Football Club was held on Wednesday night at the Duke of Cambridge Hotel, St.. Helens, Alderman T. C. Wilcock presiding over a good attendance of members. Mr. .7. Tyrer, financial secretary, submitted the annual report and balance sheet. He was glad to say that the club was in a better financial position than it was twelve months ago, though the improvement was not se marked as he had anticipated. Still, he thought, they might congratulate themselves on the high position the club had attained in the football world, and that they were steadily progressing. Had it not been for the few wet Saturdays at the commencement of the season, the loss of the Manchester Rangers' sate on Boxing Day through the frost, and the loss on the tour (some £35), he felt certain that they would have cleared off the whole of the deficiency. Several members responded to the appeal made last year for them to try and get friends to join, as was evidenced by the increase of £24 12e. in subscriptions over last year's total. He made another appeal on similar lines for the next season. The appeal to the playing members to play a combined game had also been successful, and the team concluded the season with a majority of 263 points over their opponents' score. A strong list of fixtures had been arranged for the coining season., He appealed, to the players to do their,lttmost to head the second division competition, thereby gaining honour for the club and for themselves, and proving that they were entitled to their claim to be Included in the first division. The hospital match re suited in the handsome, balance of £l5 18i. 114. being hem :ed over to the Providence hospital, .and £lO was contributed out of the club funds towards the erection of a memorial stone over the grave of the late John Brown- -bill, one of their old players. The balance sheet showed that the *gate money had amounted to £334 2;. 81. against £25 lie. 101 ; sub- scriptions £75 ss. 6.1., against £5O 13s. 61. ; and the team's travelling expenses were £154 is. 2d. against £l3O 14s. 3d. The total expenditure had been £520 19s. 9d., •leaving an adverse balance of £95 12s. lid.. against an adverse balance the previous year of £ll3 is. 3d. The report and balance sheet were then adopted. Mr. F. .1. Dindett, hon. secretary, referred to the brilliant display the team had given during the season. He said if they searched the annals of football in Lan- cashire and Yorkshire they would find that tne St. Helens team's doings were as good as any. True, one team had onlylcst two matches, but they had not got such good majorities, and had certainly played some " toffee " teams. The result of the season was : Played, 45 : won, 30; drawn, 11 ; lost. 4 ; 38 goals 67 tries for, 6 goals 13 tries against. The whole of the fixtures for next season, with the exception of the open- ing match, have been arranged. A pr3position to increase the number of the com- mittee from eight to twelve was defeated by 43 to 39 votes. Councillor J. P. Mearns was re-elected president.</p>
