        <p>An unusually large number of sudden were reported to the police on Monday. of the death ot a man named Thomas Prout,3o • of age, ,a labourer, who watt employed on the Canal at Acton Grange was received. The dree'd who lived in Rill-st.,Warrington, was found dea? the lane behind the cricket field at Arpley.oll night at twenty minutes to twelve, lie VI" time a soldier, having served in India for s° years, and since his return home he has freq' suffered from ague and severe pains in the st°n't He went to work as usual on Monday nigh 8. lo'ciock,but owing to the heavy rain operations suspended at 1045. when the deceased and commenced the return journey to Warril IWhen near the cricket field the deceased told ills, pinions that he was suffering from cramp. 'him to walk, but when he had proceeded a 100 P' expressed his inability to go further, and asked leave him in the lane while they went to VVaxrial his wife and a policeman. They left him as desil seeing Constable Wood at Bridge Foot, infornied,„ what had occurred, as well as Mrs. Prout. "; officer returned to the spot indicated, he foull" lying in the road dead. Ann Leatherbarrow (53), the wife of a &amp;cutter, at No. 5. Rigby-st.,died on Monday afternoon ' o'clock. She had long suffered from heart dise. Ifour o'clock she sat down to tea, after which sh° into the scullery to do some washing and while over the dolly tub fell and expired in a few onnut the presence of a neighbour. Aied John Redfern (38), a bricksetters' Labourer, twe suddenly at his residence, 45, Orford-bloet lminutes to six on Monday evening. • Mire et:',,pd' the 15th inst. at Messrs. Orme Old fingers' irGo.',4l 'Manchester, he fell off a ladder and hurt one 03 111 He was also so badly shaken that he had t01 Warrington, where he has since been under: 1 treatment. On Monday evening he was sittelc, table in the kitchen when a neighbour named l'esshe who was in the house at the time, heard a fall, nee into the kitchen and found that deceased 