      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <p>RUNCORN RURAL SANITARY AUTHORITY.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting was held at Dutton Workhouse on Tuesday, under the presidency of Mr. J. Mclntyre. Present Rev. J. W. N. Tanner, Rev.J. Hanby, Messrs. E. Aaron. J. Stokes, W. 11. J. Jenkins, T. Lewis, T. For, C. T. Whitley, J. M Wil- liams, E. Bratherton, 11, E. Heywood, John Weir, S. Woodward, J. Brandreth, J. Parkinson, J. Ashton (clerk), G. Ashton (deputy clerk), Mr. Dansey (Government Inspector), Dr. Adams (medical officer) J. Farrington (sanitary inspector).</p>
        <p>URBAN POWERS FOR WESTON AND HALTON. Mr. Aaron moved " That application be made to the Local Government Board under section 276 of the Pub- lic Health Act 1875, to declare the provisions of the said Act in force in urban districts to be in force in the con- tributory places of Weston and Halton, and to invest the Guardians of the poor of the Runcorn Union as a Sanitary Authority for the Rural Sanitary district of that union with all powers, rights, duties, capacities, liabilities, and obligations of an Urban Authority under the said Act and within the said contributory places of Weston and Lialton.—Mr. Aaron in moving the resolu- tion said they wished to have control of the erection of buildings so that persons could not as was now the case, erect places for human habitation which were not fit for piggeries.—Mr. Dansey said the powers sought ought to be specified if they wished to be successtul in their application. Mr. Jenkins described the powers sought, and said Weston was rapidly increasing in populaticn and rateable value, and was expected to be a second Chicago. (Laughter.) He considered that if some of the powers sought were not needed now they would be in the near future.—The Rev. J. Hanby seconded the resolution. After further discussion Mr. Aaron with- drew his motion and "substituted another that Weston and Halton have the same byelaws as Frodsham, and this resolution was carried unanimously.</p>
        <p>A NEW PAROCIIIAL COMMITTEE. The Rev. J. Hanby gave notice that at the next meet- ingle would move the appointment of a parochial Com- mittee f.r the townships of Halton, Weston, and Clifton.</p>
        <p>HALTON BROOK. Messrs. Potts, Potts and Gardener, solicitors, Chester, wrote stating that Mr. Evans, agent to Sir Richard Brooke, was not disposed to allow the drainage of any houses to be carried into Halton Brook other than those now erected, or in course of erection.—The letter was re- ferred to a local committee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>LOANS.</head>
        <p>LOANS.</p>
        <p>Messrs. Stanley and Mocara, of Liverpool, wrote offering to lend £3,800 at 31- per cent, for the construc- tion of Water Works at Frodshain. They were also pre- pared to consider the smaller loan of £1,500 required for sewerage Works at Hull and Appleton. Toe repayments might be spread over a long period. INFECTIOUS DISEASE. Dr. Adams reported that a case of typhoid fever bad occurred at Banks Row, Weston ; the patient was con- valescent. The back premises were in an insanitary con- dition. Pigs and poultry were kept too near the houses and the privies and ashpits required emptying and cleansing. The typhoid fever case at Rocksavage had terminated fatally. There were two more cases of scarlet fever reported from Whitley, the schools should not yet be re-opened. The whole of the cases at the Smallpox Hospital were progressing favourably and if no fresh cases occurred the hospital would be empty in a fortnight or less.—The Clerk said he had written to the clerk of the Whitley School Board, but the communica- tion had been returned by the postal authorities.</p>
        <p>THE SMALLPDX HOSPITAL. The Sanitary Inspector presented a tabulated state- ment showing that there had been 43 cases admitted in- to the Smallpox Hospital at Moore. Of these 22 were females and 21 males. There had been two deaths.</p>
        <p>_ A COMPLAINT. Dr. Ormsley, of Dalton, wrote complaining of the insanitary condition of Holly Bank.—The Inspector said the nuisances arose through the primitive nature of the plumbing in the house. The owner declined to make any alterations, and the tenant said it was not his duty to remedy sanitary defects.—The Clerk said it appeared to him that the Authority could not at present interfere. —The Medical Officer said he would snake an inspection of the property, and then the Authority could act upon his certificate.</p>
        <p>THE MEDICAL OFFICER. The Local Government Board wrote approving of the reappointment of Dr. Adams as medical officer of the Authority, at a salary of £l2O per annum.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting was held at Dutton Workhouse on Tuesday, Mr. J. Mclntyre presided and there were present the Rev. J. W. N. Tanner, the Rev. Dr. Gastaldi, the Rev. J. Hanby ; Messrs. S. Taylor, T. Handley, S. Yarwood, T. Lea, D. IL Jones, A. R. Norman, E. Aaron, J. Stokes, W. H. J. Jenkins, T. Lewis, T. Ford, C. T. Whitley, J. M. Williams, E. Bratherton, 11. E. Heywood, John Weir, S. Wood- ward, J. Brandreth, J. Parkinson; J. Ashton (clerk), G. Ashton (deputy clerk), and Mr. Dansey (Govern- ment Inspector). DR. BURTON. Tho Lop,ol (Invooorop9t. Board wrote approving of the payments by the Guardians or za • officer, for the Frodsham district in consideration of the extra services rendered I.y him in the case of an out- break of smallpox °tithe Ship Canal Works.</p>
        <p>THE DUKE OF YORK. With a view to the observance of the forthcoming royal marriage in workhouses and other establishments for the in-door poor, the Local Government Board issued an order enabling Guardians to make modifications in the discipline and diet of the inmates.—The Rev. J. W. N. Tanner said the Visiting Committee had considered the matter, and had come to the conclusion that some- thing similar to the rations given to the inmates at Christmas Day be given on the date of the Royal mar- riage, but that the day be not recognised as a holiday.— Dr. Gastaldi moved that the day _ be recognised as a holiday.—The Rev. J. W. N. Tanner accepted this amendment and the Board unanimously approved.</p>
        <p>EX-OFFICIO GUARDIANS. The Lewisham Union forwarded for the approval and support of the Runcorn Guardians a resolution pointing out that by Clause 19 of the Local Government Bill, ex-officio guardians were abolisbeg and urging the reten- tion of Justices of the peace as ex-officio guardians.— The communication was received with loud laughter and, on the motion of Dr. Gastaldi, seconded by Mr. T. Handley, the letter was laid upon the table. FREE EDUCATION IN RUNCORN. .</p>
        <p>With respect to the provision of free places at Run- corn, Mr. John Simpson wrote, stating that thore were no free places at Bruns*ick Wesleyan Schools. He added —" You are, of course, aware that it is quite optional with denominational scho.ils whether they provide free places or not."—Dr. Gastaldi said this was evading the question.—The Rev. J. Hanby said this was the only answer Mr. Simpson could give.—ln answer to a ques- tion, Mr. Hanby said the school was more than full.— Dr. Gastaldi said the managers of these schools in their capacity of public educators, might have provided free places for the poor, considering that they received a free , grant of 10s. per head in the same manner as other schools. All schools ought now to be free. (Hear,hear.) —Mr. Brandreth moved that the Clerk make an applica- tion to the Education Department for free education for the children in question. (Hear, hear.)—The Rev. .1. Hanby seconded.—Dr. Gastaldi said it was useless, the Department would simply reply as on a previous occa- sion.—Mr. Norman concurred.—The motion was carried. —Mr. Brandreth moved that for the future all people requiring school fees Ahould come before the Board.—Dr. Gastaldi seconded.—Mr. Handley thought it was unrea- sonable to drag the parents to that house.—Mr. Bran- dreth withdrew this motion, and substituted that the inquiry officer attend to explain the various cases. He received £sa year.—Mr. Taylor said the Runcorn Guar- dians carefully inquired into each case, and they were all genuine cases. He asked Mr. Brandreth to withdraw the motion.—Mr. Brandreth declined,and remarked that Runcorn was the only township for which they paid school fees.—The motion was carried without dissent. UNPAID CALLS. Mr. Brandreth, chairman of the Finance Committee, reported that the following townships had not paid their calls : -Acton Grange, Alvanley, Bartington, Bud- worth, Frodsham,Kingsley,Preston-o'th-Hill, and Sutton. —The Clerk was instructed to give notice of legal pro- ceedings, if the calls were not paid within seven days. A VACANCY. • The Clerk read a communication from Dr. A. Argo, re- signing his appointment as medical officer and public vaccinator of the Budwortn district. It was decided to advertise for a successor.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>" pitIME ENGLISH BEEF." How IT IS LIBELLED.</head>
        <p>" pitIME ENGLISH BEEF." How IT IS LIBELLED.</p>
        <p>- Instructive evidence was given on Tuesday b efore the Select Committee inquiring into the advisability of marking foreign meat. Mr. Coggan, president of the National Association of Butchers, said the notion that Scotch beef was the best was an old-fashioned idea to which he, as a West of England man, strongly objected. Mr. D. Davies, a Liverpool butcher, said he had seen very poor Canadian cattle and old cows labelled as prime English in " foreign " meat shops ; but he had never seen old cow and old bull beef sold as foreign meat. He had seen " prime Canterbury" placarded over a shop stocked with River Plate mutton. He said he had no objection to the licensing of butchers. He urged that the railway companies and steamboat owners should be compelled to provide better accommodation 'for the cattle trade, remarking that cattle "were often shunted about as if they were coals." The inquiry was again adjourned.</p>
