      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <p>itetttr9 to the Otitor.</p>
        <p>KINGCRAFT.</p>
        <p>Sir,—At the present time when our feelings and our pockets are being appealed to to provide a present, on a large scale, for a Royal Prince and Princess on their marriage, it may not be inopportune to offer a few re- marks on the kinrly institution. The origin of clic Jewish monarchy is fully set forth in the eighth chapter of the first Book of Kings. There we learn that the chosen people, having grown tired of the Theocracy under which they had lived for four hun- dred years, oemanded a king, after the pattern of the surrounding nations, that he might judge them, and go out before them, and fight their battles. This conduct was highly displeasing to God, as their rejection of Samuel, his anointed prophet and judge, was a virtual rejection of himself as head and ruler of the nation. As a punishment, therefore, for their rebellion he granted them a king, anci here is the divinely inspired descrip- tion of what their klng 9liIdJClO for them.—" He will yOut sons and appoint them for himself, for his chariots, and to be his horsemen ; and s. me shill run before his chariots. And he will appoint him captains over thousands, and captains over fifties ; and will set them to ear his ground, and to reap his harvests, and to make him instruments of war, and instruments of his chariots. . . . And he will take your fields, and your vines-arc's, and your olive yards, even the best of them, and give them to his servants. . . . And ye shall cry out in that day la-cause of your king which ye have chosen you : and the Lord will nothear you in that day." The old Testament and the pages of Joseplius bear awful testimony to the way in which this punish- ment was carried out from the days of Saul to the destruction of the Jewish po ity by the Romans. And surely it would be unreasonable to suppose that a , system ot Government which was so clearly opposed to the Divine will, and so disastrcus to Israel could be a benefit to any other nation. The kingly system springs from the pride, selfishness and ambition of men; and can never be anything but an evil, and a sore horde.. to the people who have the misfortune to live under it. Has not the past history of mankind been mainly a long record of the tyranny, grinding oppression, and murder carried on by Kings and their followers in all parts of the world ? From the time of Ninsrod until now the inhabitants of all lands have groaned beneath the rule of Royal despots, and patriotic men in all ages have cried out for deliverance from them, and vainly striven to restrain their boundless waste of blood and treasure. Nor does Kingcraft in our own day and in our own country offer many attractions to the reflecting and just mind. It is true it is not surrounded with the scaffolds and other sanguinary horrors of the jaget ' • but it still retains its love of plunder, and seems to take a special delight in the art of war. The prophet Samuel's des- cription of kingly doings is, in its main features, so applicable to ottr own case that it might have been written in London in 1893 and not in Ramah three thou- sand years ago. Here we have a Royal Family who neither judge the people nor go forth with our armies to battle, and yet are not ashamed to absorb among them a iniHion a year of the earnings of the industrial classes. And large as this seems, it does not appear to be enough for the Royal wants ; for very few sessions of Parlia- ment are allowed to pass without fresh calls for money. Surrounding this Royal group, we have quite an army of dukes, earls, lords, and so forth, descendants of men whose services, famous or infamous were rewarded by our Kings with the large estates into which the land is now divided. These noble or ignoble landowners must have princely revenues to enable them to flutter round the Court and enjoy the intimacy of Royalty and its , surroundings. For these revenues they neither toil nor I spin •, but with the concustenCe and aid of what le CaliSq law they deliVe Many Millions a year in the shape of rent charges-upon the lands which they call their own. This unrighteous system of land tenure is highly bene- ficial to those exalted idlers, but the amount of wrong which it inflicts upon the people, who are the rightful I owners, is beyond calculation. Let it suffice to say that the men, women, and children who, as cultivators of the soil, are entitled to the largest share of its produce, live in a state of semi-starvation, and not a few .1 than] end their days in workhouses provided at the cost of another class of toilers. A noble lord who condescends to live in idleness on ten thousand pounds a year considers 12s. or 143. a week an ample provision for a poor labourer and his family. Then fleets and armies have to be maintained that the glory of Royalry may stand high at home and abroad. 'the cost of these branches of whit I may sarcastically call "the public service" involves a loss ss no less than thirty-two millions a year to the wage eari.ing classes. In addition to this fighting expenditure they have to pay some twenty-six millions annually for interest and management of a national debt of more than seven hundred millions contracted for war purposes. When William the Third, "of glorious, pious, and immortal memory" came to the throne in 1638 cur national debt stood at £684,263, but at his death it had risen to six- teen millions. During the interval of 200 years from the! Orange King to Victoria it has gone on increasing until it has reached the enormous sum above mentioned. The spectacle ot a nation calling itself Christian saddled with a war debt of seven hundred millions gives rise to many disagreeable thoughts, and proves how valueless are the sermons of the bishops, priests, and deacons who pose before SID as ambassadors of the Prince of Peace.</p>
        <p>But an expenditure infinitely worse than that of money invariably follows in the wake of Royalty. The cost of blood which our rulers have incurred during the past two centuries has been horrible beyond description Millions of our fellow countrymen have been taken from the peaceful pursuits of life, and trained in the use of the most destructive implements which a perverted science could supply, that they might go farth to slay and be Wain to gratily the whims or ambition of Royalty and its advisers. Multitudes of them Ile entombed in the bottom of the sea; and the bones of far greater mul- titudes lie bleaching in every land, upon fair fields that were once reddened by their blood. And to this awful cost of human life let me add the tears and agony of the millions of men, women, and children who were reduced to desolation and beggary by the warriors' infernal work.</p>
        <p>With the facts I have stated staring them in the face it would neither be wise nor patriotic for the working classes of the country to make jubilation over the com- ing Royal weddiag. And would it not be absurd on their part to contritoute even the smallest coin out or their wages to glorify a young man and woman whom they have never seen, and of whose merits or demerits they know absolutely nothing.—Yours truly, JOHN JONES.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>EDUCATION QUESTION AT EARLES-</head>
        <p>THE EDUCATION QUESTION AT EARLES-</p>
        <p>TOWN.</p>
        <p>Sir,—After repeatedly asserting, that no cfild had been sent home, from the Wesleyan school in c.in- sequence of is not having its fees, the Chairman and others, at the ratepayers' meeting, challenged me to give facts to the contrary. A lad was sent home with the following letter, when his father, by a note, sought a free place for him in the said school.—</p>
        <p>[Copy] " Wesleyan Day Schools, Earlestown, Dec. 5, 1892 [qr. Elsrards, Dear Sir,—You have been mlainforirsed. There are no fres schools in Newton and Earlestown except infants' schools. Your course is, as you state. to make application to the Education Department, but until the managers here grant the free places, I am under the necessity of asking you to pay the fee.—Yours truly, (signed), George Burge.s. Writing to me on October 27, 1892, the conventirn of school managers said : "Provision for public school accommodation without payment of fees had been arranged for prior te the receipt of your communication to the extent of 10 per cent, on the average attendance for the last sclociol year." Enclosed is the address of the Mr. Edwards to whom the above letter was sent.— Yours truly, THOMAS COLLINS, Cross-lane, Earlestown, June 29, 1893.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>EXAMINEE,, sAITEDAY, ju1,17 1, 1893 NOT h 8 ON GAMES AND AriILETICS.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINEE,, sAITEDAY, ju1,17 1, 1893 NOT h 8 ON GAMES AND AriILETICS.</p>
        <p>CRICKET.</p>
        <p>It seems th me that Warrington first eleven are some- what errat.c in their play. Fancy Werneth, for eight wicktts bating them on Saturday, by 137 runs th 65. A close observation of the game showed that there were two things responsible for this. They were, firAly, the limpid, lax, and very loose manner in which Warrington fielded, and also the obscure connection which the players—some of them, anyhow—thought existed .be- tween the bat and the tall. They wezit in for carving the air a little t,-)o much to merit for themse:ves the praise of having played a good gatne.</p>
        <p>The fielding was particularly faulty. Misses of an important character were made by Jefferson, Tyler,and Reynolds. Specimens of the batting are as thus : Wood, Voisey, and Reynolds, ducks' cgs ; Jefferson and Tyler, one each; J. J. Crosfield two, and S. M. Crosfteld and Parry three each. The two professionals and G. Clarke did all the batting that was not poor—absolutely poor. Asa contrast there were five out of the eight Werneth players who scored over 20.</p>
        <p>The second team in their meet with the Wernetii second met with a very similar fate to that enjoyed— was it enjoyed, though ?—by their seniors. They scored 92 against 108. The only two batters who did anything like rational batting were Dutton (29) and Smith (28). Five of the players including—(oh ! my crushed and withered hopes !)—including Bibby, who scored nothing .ach and ttml. gaVe 111: their bats. The howling was done chiefly by T.L.:16rWC,0,1 and Diatton,but there was nothing particularly bright about it.</p>
        <p>An unambitious life has been described by some sage, or some parsley, (I forget which is the proper term), as a " musty egg." Therefore the life of the Sank.ty Wesleyan Cricket Club may aspire to a more dignified appelation for it contains ambition. Sixteen of its members played eleven of the Warrington first eleven on Tuesday, and from the result which ensued thereon, I would suggest that they describe the life of their team not as " a musty egg," but a "duck egg." Ten of their wickets were taken by w. Podmore for six runs ! Which member of that mighty fraternity will not now hide his diminished head when he espies approaching the digni• and form of a Wire Mill man ?</p>
        <p>The remainder of the wick.As were taken by G.Clarker and the total raised by the Wesluaus was 48. Fancy, ten wickets for--0!I ! all right. I'll let it rest. Three of the Warrington eleven ran 78, and then time was called. W. Podmore and Bibby made 40 bstween them, for Warrington, and the retnainder was made up by Jolley, G. Clarke. The game was renewed on Wednes- day and for seven wickets Warrington scored 120.</p>
        <p>The Wasleyans and Earlestown met to settle their differences at Mill-lane on Saturday, and the game was of a very pleasant character from beginning to end. Earlestown played well for their 58, the top scorer being Sudworth with 12, T. Clare, J. Doran, and J. Wads- worth, also contributing 9 each. Four bowlers helped to get them out, but Watkins captured the most wickets having four to his credit. Three of the Sankey Bridges team got into double figures viz., T. Simpson, 14, E. Clare, 13, and A. B. Bail,y, 12. W. Miles and J. fl,)otli also nicking 9 and 8 respectively. The whole team was- out for 73 thus winning by 15 runs. T.Clare for &amp;ales town was the most destructive with th, ball taking 8 wickets. After the match the teams adjourned to the schoolroom at Sankey Bridges where they sat down to a substantial tea,after which congratulatory speeches were made by members of each team.</p>
        <p>The second team surprised themselves by bating Earlestown Wesleyans 2nd, by 81 to 42. L Iwton 110 the/ principle share in the bowling, Highley came out of his shell, and far surpassed the rest of the players by scoring 26. the next highest score being Light 12.</p>
        <p>Pearson an..l Knowles met A team tti " Mr. McCues Cisven." I never heard of this team before, but it was beaten by 51 runs to 77. The number of teams which have come to light recently is appalling.</p>
        <p>A Correspondent writes :—" Alas ! What's become of the Parish Church Choir team this year ? As I thought, they have sustained a heavy blow now that Walter Dennett and - Chas. [lampoon have left the Choir. The former I hear is playing for Pearson and Knowles second team, but has not shone so much yet, but there is plenty of time for him to shine. As a well wisher of the team last year, I would like to hear of his doing the hat trick and carrying his bat out with him, which he did on above one occasion last year."</p>
        <p>AT OUR LOCAL CHURCHES,</p>
        <p>THE P S.A. AT WYCLIFFE. There was a largely attended Pleasant Sunday After- noon meeting at Wycliffe Church, on Sunday. The mem- bership of the brotherhood has now reached 643. 'the chair was taken by Alderman Holmes, J P., who ex- pressed his sympathy with the P.S.A. movement, and hoped it would still further prosper. The speaker for the afternoon was the Rev. A. Freeman, Wesleyan minis- ter. He delivered an address on the importance of churches. Mr. T. Wallington, secretary, read a letter from the Rev. J. Yonge, in which he hoped-the follow- ing resolution would be put to the meeting. "That this ineeting of working-men assembled at the Pleasant Sun- day Afternoon Service held in Wycliffe church, Warri tig- tan, desires to express its deep feeling of sorrowful sym- pathy with the widows,- orphans, and families generally of the 430 men and officers who suddenly perished in the foundering of H.M.S. Victoria, en Thursday last. The ' meeting earnestly trusts that prompt FIN o.C(itlittO means will be, devised to alleTt?.l2: Sorrowa and meet the necessities st ttlio ralmeroUs families thus suddenly bereft Of tilf.;l- broad winners; and that every sailor's family in the kingdom affected by this terrible disaster, will Elia that the nation take them into its sympathetic protec-. tion. This meeting also believes that if public appeals for national assistance in this behalf should be found necessary, British working-men will not be behind other and wealthier classes in ready and generous re- sponse." The resolution was proposed by Ur. W. Hick- man,seconded by Mr. G. Wallington,and carried unanim- ously. Mrs. Cleave and Mr. Cook each contributed a solo. The Rev. T. Wilkinssn, Southport, and Mr. F. Brown also took part in the meatier' -</p>
        <p>ThE RIGGED SCgOOL. In St. Paul's Church on Sunday afternoon the Vicar (Rev. H. V. Pigot) preached a sermon in aid of the War- rington Ragged Sunday School. The Ragged School children along with the officials, attende4, and there was a fair attenclanets'c! the DUblie. -The children `sang several hymns in a creditable manner. Thezollections in.the church amounted to us. 4d. SCHOOL SERMONS AT THE PARISH CHURCH. ' Special sermons were preached at the Parish Church on Sunday on behalf of the day schools. The morning preacher was the Rev. J. D. Best, principal of Chester Training College, and in the evening the pulpit was occupied by toe E:.".". • Canon Armour, heat:l-master Merchant Taylors' School, Liverpooi. service wat held in the afternoon. The collecti.Zsr4, realized £2O.</p>
        <p>CEIRIST CHURCH, CROFT. The annual sermons on behalf of the Sunday school and choir were preached in the above church on Sunday by the Rev. Richard Tebbs, M.A., vicar of St. Thomas's, Wigan, when there were good congregations, especially in the evening. The Rector of the parish, the Rev. H. I. Wadeson, M.A., presided at the organ, and his mani- pulation of the instrument showed him to be a musician of great ability. Mr. Cawthorne, organist and .choir- master, was in charge of the mus,cal part of the service. The collections amounted to £ll Ss. SCHOOL SERMONS AT ST. ALBAN'S. The school sermons in aid of the St. Alban's day schools were preached on Sunday at the St. Alban's Roman Catholic Church, Bewsey-street. The preacher in the morning was the Rev. Father Darby 0.e.8., of St. Ann's, Liverpool, and formerly of Warrington. The evening preacher was the rev. Father Richards, 0.5.8., of Wootton. In the morning a special mass (Haydyn's 16th) was sung in a very creditable manner, Miss Cavanagh taking the soprano solos with great ability. Mr. John Caldwell presided at the organ. A "BANNER SERVICE," Special sermons were preached at the Workingmen's Mission Chapel, Bank Quay, on Sunday, Vie proceeds of the collections being devoted towards purchasing a new banner. The preacher in the morning and evening was Mr. George Dale, who had for his morning subject " the woman of -Samari 1," and in the evening " The Good Samaritan." During the services, anthems were sung and a solo was contributed by Mr. Samuel 11358811.</p>
        <p>CROFr UNITARIAN -CHAPEL: The annual Sunday school sermons were preached in the above Jhapel on Sunday, afternoon and evening, by the Rev. W. H. Drummond, 8.A., of Cairo-st., Chapel, Warrington. There were large•coneregations,the chapel being filled to overflowing in the afternoon. A portion of the choir of Caire-st., Chapel, Warrington, conducted by Mr. H. Wright, rendered the musical portion of the service in an admirable manner. Mr. Gandy was at the organ. The serviee in the after- noon commenced with the singing of the anthem " Arise, shine, for thy light is come," and before the sermon the anthem " 0 praise the Lord, all yo heathen" (Earl of Wilton), was given. In the evening a quartet, • He shall feed his flock "(Handel, was sung in fine styie at the commencement of the service by the Misses Allen (soprano and alto), Mr. H. Wright (tenor), and Mr. C. Allen (bass), and before the sermon the anthem "send out Thy light" (Gounodl by the whole choir, who acquitted themselves in an efficient manner. The col- lections, with donations, amounted to gll 2a. 21 Tea was provided as usual for friends from a distance.—The annual Sonde,- school tea party in connection with the above took place on Monday afternoon, when about 40 seholars, teachers and friends partook of tea in the schoolroom. Afterwards a number of games, &amp;c., took place in Mr. Welsby's field until dusk, an nuts and sweets were distributed during the evening by the Rev. W. Mason, of Leigh.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>CYCLING.</head>
        <p>CYCLING.</p>
        <p>' The local cycling world has been almost et a standstill . this past week. Several members of the ‘V.C.O visited Mickle Trafford, on Satureay, and enjoyed themsAves as well as they were able. Oa Thursday night two de- tachments laft Warrington on a tour in Vl:deg. One party has gone in the direction of Conway, and the other away to the Wye Vallay. A man who on one occasion had his leg broken bye cyclist, says he hopes they will stop in Wales. This is very unkind—so far as Wales is concerned, anyhow.</p>
        <p>Rumour has it that Lawreoce Fletcher and G. r. Mills both intend to try to rower the safety record bet- ween Land's End and John a' Groats. So much depends upon atmospheric conditions in a ride of this kind that whoever is most lucky in this aspect will have the Lest chance.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>Swimmtlirs.</head>
        <p>Swimmtlirs.</p>
        <p>The sad calamity which happened at the new baths on Monday evening cast a shadow duriv)g the remainder of the week, upon the place. It was an event of a most lamentable character—as an unfortunate mark in the early history ol the institution, and also because of ths extraordinary character of the fatafity. There wers more than 40 bathers in the bath at Me time, and not a single soul saw poor Jeffery either struggle or show any signs of distress. As a matter of fact, when he was dis- covered, he was lying at the bottom of the bath, and right opposite the spot where "Fair "Turner and Mr. Williamson, of Thelwall, stood.</p>
        <p>A meeting next week cf the Swimming' Club is going to be held next week for the purpose of appointing a captain, and also for the purpose of adopting club rules. These rules I am glad to see,st;pulate that the club shall amalgamate itself both with the Northern and Counties Swimming Association, and also with the life Saving Society. To affiliate with the latter institution will be a very advisable thing to do, for the chief object of the society is to disseminate knowledge which will be useful alike in saving one's own life and the lives c,i'others.</p>
        <p>There will be a weekly practice held under the rules of the Satiety, comprising exercises for rescuing drowning men, guarding against the clutches of a drowning man, and also pumping and restoring to animation a body drawn from the water.</p>
        <p>A large number of now members have joined'the club, ram glad to hear during the present week,. although, perhaps not unnaturally, the attendance has, suffered temporarily throurzh the pathetic incident of' Monday. I have heard it whispered that the name to be submitted at the appointment of captain will be "Fair" Turner. And what more suitable nam- could be suilmitted should like to ask, unless it is that of Ms_ °rounds, which may not be submitted I have heard because Mr. Grounds objects to take the office.</p>
        <p>By the way, it is worthy of mention at a new arrangement has been made about the pfirege bath. Mr. liarlow.the manager, is now spending most of his time each evening in "keeping a fatherly- eye," upon bathers, and he has been allowed the assZtEance of a Junior club in his office works.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>MiscELLsNigous.</head>
        <p>MiscELLsNigous.</p>
        <p>Several local athletes were again well tz-the fore on Saturday,and some excellent results are to. be recorded. At the Crewe Alexandra A.C. Sports, &amp;Osborne Cap- tured the Erst place in the 120 yards in gra.nd style. The race was a capital one, S. H. Huaon, London, being only six inches behind Osborne at the finish. H. Webster came in second in the 300 yards hurdle.. behind L. Walt, Renton H., who had sine yards start of the local man. J. J. Rabbitt, Warrington, took the first prise in the 440 yards. At the Radcliffe C.C. Sports, W.Fair, of Warrington, came in the quarter inile. after a good race.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>SALE OF WINWICK HALL. ESTATE.</head>
        <p>SALE OF WINWICK HALL. ESTATE.</p>
        <p>At the Lion Hotel, Warringtoin, on Thursday afternoon, Mr. • Thomas Sntt3o, auctioneer, offsred for sale by public action, the Winwick Hall estate. There vras a large attendance. Lot one, a cottage, was sold for nez to Air. H. J. Longton, solicitor, who acted on behalf of Mks Lucy Handley the present oecnpier. Lot twa, consisted of three houses, rent varying from £5O to £3, and was withdrawn at £1,600. Lot three, consisting of a wheelwright's shop. co• t ige, yard, lec., was sold to Mee, D. Pickton, Alien-t.. for£33o. Lots four and five, two farms, one at a ran- tal of £B2, another £124, were withdrawn at £2,100. Lot six, two cottages, was withdrawn at £2lO. Lots given, eight, ani nine. which comprised Winwick Hall farm, and about 20) acres or land, at a total terttal of over £378, mere, we are informed, purchased hy private tres'y by tha Laura:hire C33.ity Couzicil, for tha pur- pose of erecting an asylum.</p>
