        <p>Thelist race of the season for old birds vsas flown on fitonday from Avranches (France) a 'distance of 332 •mile. The birds were liberated by the convcryeurs of the English Homing Federation at 6.22 sang in veryclear weather with a light. south- west wind:. Owing to the great loss the members have stained in the previous races (the weathsr during the last 2:montbs being entirely against the horn- ing Pivot*. 27 birds were all that could be mustered, and out 4:4 this number only 4 managed to reach home. Mr. Edward Burrows, Lirh-street, Warrington was again to the-front, timing ths first bird. in at 534,tn. the same ditv,, t eing closly followed by Mr. Hugh F. Greenough at 5404).m. Warrington fanciers are not ' alone in tbe great Wielder, us nearly every meter in each of the Lancashire Clubs have met with the same fate. Manchester Plying Club, the strongest society in England havronly about a dozen birds returned out of a number OF I.= sent to avranches. Gentlemen, who have been studying the propensity of the homing pigeon since the long distance homer came into vogue in Eng- Istad, positively staie that they have never known such disaster that has lagallen them this year. Belorx will he found the result of the rAce from Avrancher, together with the list of average. veibeities that each member has aecoinpli,sheil in• the 5 races. As will be teen Mr. Burrows again tops the list and we are sure, thatlse has every reason to :eel proud of the stock, the strain of ,w.hich he has trade a Bretons study. It may he men- tioned that he has won every continental race thin club has held since its ferrnation in 1891.</p>
