      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>UNDERTSKER'6 ASSISTANT. A. MELANCHOLY TOWN CHARACTER</head>
        <p>THE UNDERTSKER'6 ASSISTANT. A. MELANCHOLY TOWN CHARACTER</p>
        <p>BY LtIC BLUNT.</p>
        <p>Among the several curious characters whom I have ferreted out in Warrington, a man by the , name of Baldwin was the strangest. When I heard ' of his death not long ago, my memory stretched back over the space of three years to the first time I met the man. I had been attending the funeral of a friend's relative at the cemetery, and Baldwin, who had been one of the bearers was riding back in our coach. Possibly the reader will remember him ;he was a tall, sallowfaced, sad-eyed individual, and on Ibis occasion he looked sadder and more grieved than even the deceased's relatives. Ills was an impressive personality ; he resembled, oh ! so much —the piol urea of the tender hearted minister who is always found gracing the funeral of some ob- scure but noble youth who forms the hero of Sun- day School books. Ile possessed all the charsotr istics which a ratn need* to btl in piled a66,„d with funerqi ; vay voice liad a sepulchral :;61!Cii&amp;el abut it. Somehow, I don't know ex- actly how it was, we found ourselves in a few moments listening to what he termed the story of his life, lie said:</p>
        <p>" It isu't often I have much to say that's serious, or may be,that's true. The kind of life I lead won't always allow it. But what lam telling you now, airs ia true. "Aye" he resumed with a quaver in his voice, " racy true. It has been so true that it has made Ned Baldwin what he is—a poor sinning. broken down, useless and purposeless -ivuulicant almost . And before it did that—l am speaking truth 1—.1109r.:`. it did that, i.t; made him spend many .11 Wedejr dreary, sleepless night, with a heart so broken, so lacerated, that he only required the courage to drown himself. Yes— and—it—would—have—batter—so," lie concluded with a sob in his throat as be turned his grief- stricken face to the window.</p>
        <p>"Better so" he rut:mitred abstractedly again. Then he took up and commenced :—Somehow I . was always unlucky. I Was born in Liverpool. When I %vas IS, I was still at school, but not learn- ing much, My bother was cashier to a firm of brokers, and he absconded with £ooo—he was the only lucky member of the family. This broke my father's gentle heart ; and a few months afterwards ' my mother—yes, my mother—she followed him to the cemetery. I had only one friend in the world, and she was a widowed aunt. She kept lodgers, and I lived with her for almost a year. One day, my life of quiet contentment was broken. She came to me and said that a travelling theatre pro- prietor, who had apartments wiih us, had remarked that be wanted a young fellow to perform a part in a piece which he was going to play. What a glorious chance for me to earn my livelihood ! she said ; and under her persuasive influence I applied for and obtained the position, and thug I ventured forth into the world. The proprietor's name was McSwinton, and he was a magnificent physiqued man, though be smelt somewhat too strongly of alcholic beverages. His daughter was a girl about my own ago; her eyes so large and sorrowful, and her smile so kindly, yet sad, impressed me at once, and the first time we met I liked her.</p>
        <p>" Sirs, I knew I should like her right away. Something told me there was a communion of the affinity between our sffils. Aye, and God knows I did like her—With a passion that was absorbing, that was powerful.</p>
        <p>I had to impersonate " Dic., the he witer," in the play which was called the " Match Girl." It was la sublime, a beautiful play ; I have seen its pathos make hundreds of eyes welter in tears; I have seen strong men blubber like babies over it. The scene was in London. There were six characters in it. Maud, the girl of my affections took the chief char- acter, "Nell," She was supposed to be a rich man's daughter. In the first scene the villian, an engag- ing young man came t 3 stay at her house, and so fascinated the simple girl that she eloped with him to London, though she was engaged to her cousin Timothy. However, to cut a long tale short, the villian was arrested as a forger. " Nell " was left destitute, and when winter came on she grew sick- lier, and weaker, and then I met her and tried to help her. But harder and harder did the tale tell upon her—till at length I discovered her fathar and brought him down to the city. He stayed at an hotel while I searched for his daughter, 'Twas then the beautiful scene occurred. It was on a Sunday night,—durk and cold, and snowing-_I found her sitting on some church steps, with her pale thin face covered with her hands. The rolling strains of the organ blended with the voices of the singing congregation. The Mow \vas falling quietly,thickly and last, and everything ware an aspect of sacred coldness.</p>
        <p>niy hand on her shoulder and said: Nell !" There was no answer. Again I said: "Nell! Don't stop here! It's too cold!" . . .. .</p>
        <p>She raised her head slowly and looked wild and strange and said, with her he inclined :—" Dick listen Don't you hear them singing—the angels." / pretended ta listen but Just Mien the girl sprang to her feet and with a scared Inok on her face screamed "Father!" Sure enough it was the silvery beaded old man. She rushed intothis arms,but it wit only to imprint skits upon his hem, and then die. " Wo went on playing this for some months giv- ing our performances in a large tent. Lancashire wati almost completely travelled,, by which time a strong. a-biding affection had speatig up between Maud itsd 1. But things began to change, now. The propeeitor took to heavy thinking—almost every night he was intoxicated and money got short. Worst 04'01, she began to lose health, and day by day, her face became thinner and paler, and her , manner %Meter, and when dressed. up as "sell" her appearance was pitifully delicate. This Made me feel miserable, and one day, when we reached Widnes, B induced her to consults dootor,and when she returned she was ashy white,. 'oat simply said she had been ordered to tale rest. It was the next week when we came to Warring- ton with cur tent, which we put put up near the Market l".itre. Feeling very lonesome on the Sun- day night, l strolled out into Bevneey. I had proceeded aver the bridge there when.lithought I detected a noise amongst the trees, I listened. Someone was sobbing bitterly and fast.. I walked , quietly amongst the treesand presently came across Maud, kneeling with her face to heaven arid the teare Coursing down her cheeks. Just than a lance of silver moonlight glinted upon me,. and 1 was discovered.. But tefore I left Maud: that night I learned that the Widnes doctor had warned her that she suffered from consumption and weakness of heart. MI that night mid all the next day, a tearaomd feeling came over me, and in the evening, when I maw Maud, pale and ill. playing her part, a sickness atithe heart seized me—olnit enresrpitiful to see h.r wasted face-e,actingsomookingly a char- acter akindo herself. " At lass-the final scene came._ The lig.hss were , turned lova. everyone became silent, .whfiniitli.e drop 1 scene wog ttp, showing the lighted church and the] falling anew. 'lnd when the "ahorus "commenced isinging the hymn and" Nell 'sank onto the slept. I was conamencing to• go thrnagh the zienat per- formance-when I noticed a strange look of fear and agony ornher ashy white face.. I bent or.serler and whispered " Whs.&amp; ails you, Maud ?" And se whispered in a hcaky gasping voice: Nedllim dying. Fetch-w—quick, ray breath is stopping—fetch-my-fat bor.' Nobody seemed to notims there was anything wrong.,-; nobody knew it wasn't the ordinary piece. I Bites, her up gently and carried her into the side wing. The audience burst into a howl ot applause. But before they ceased, Nnud McSwinton was ly- ing abed on the floor of the booth! Wlien I had seen her body interred at the cemetery—where we Wane just come fkons--a heavy darkness came over my, mind. I begfin to drink. This dark feeling came on worse, I drank heavier and heavier. When 1 hey down to ;oat I Leonid hear nothing but the soumk.of the organ-and hymn, see- ing nothing but the 2/tiling anew, and my starving, Maud ! I wont taad with it, anti for RiX ;asontha was confined to a padded room at Rainhill. ! it's a terrible thing to be =die '</p>
        <p>- Later on, upon Meeting the undertaker I mentioned the stsange story a Buldwin's, and the undertaker said.:--</p>
        <p>" I don't kiksw how math, of its true—maybe none ot it is, for I've knOwn, him since he was a lad. lie used to be a graxe digger, but he was given so much to crying and, aympathising with the, friends of orery decesak.N.t who came there, that )e. had to lenve. If he czaildn't get work with the spade he was advised to.go to the next best 4.43,. and he vime to me. I engaged him just for "style, for he its such an acquision to one who supplies bearws—he's a perfect decoration to a funeral. and then he can always allure the attention of the ' mourners .from their own sorrow by his own f rathetic experiences. lie was always reckoned ja , f the bill as an "extra?' Well, N.'s dead blinself now, poor letitm,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-01_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <p>THE FATALITY TO A WARRINGTON WELL-SINKER. '</p>
        <p>• An inquest was held at the Railway Hotel, Heapey, on Tuesday, by Dr: Gilbertson, coroner, touching the death ot Wllliam Litherland, well- sinker, of Warrington, who was killed by the aides of a well in which he was working on the tarnrof Mr. Phillipson, of Ifeapey, falling upon him azi burying him. - •</p>
        <p>Erizaitherland, wife of the deceased, was the first witness called, and said that she resided at 12, Clare's. buildings, Warrington. Her husband was a welloinker and was 32 years of age. On the previous. Wednesday morning she last saw him alive. He had worked at well- sinking all his life.</p>
        <p>Jam-es Coyne, labourer, of Warrington, said he had been working with the deceased for about seven weeks. They both went on the jib at Pnillipson'ii farm the prev- ious Wednesday. They were sinking a well, and Litherland had charge of the undertaking. There were three other men engaged besides witness and Litherland. When the accident happened they had gone down five or six feet, but the well had been previously sunk 11 yards. At about twenty minutes to three o'clock in the afternoon, deceased said to witness, who was at the top, " Jim, keep that bucket up and send me the chafes ciownond DI come-up on the next bucket to put that ring in." Deceased was alone in the well, which was bricked down 11-i yards deep. Witness was leaning over talking to deceased when the bricks round the sides fell in, and covered him immediately. The* bricks had looked sound enough. They began to give way about five or six yards down. There were iron rods fastened from the top to hold the bricks in place. The iron reds did not give way, but the bricks fell in. There was about ten yards of stuff on deceased. They set to work on the following day to get him nut, and the body was arrived at and got out about half-past six o'clock. on Sunday night. By the Foreman (Mr. Donlan) : When deceased came on the Monday to look at the place he only then decided to do the work. Deceased expressed himself satisfied that it was safe to commence after looking at the place. Some of the others went down on the Thursday, but only to take out timber work, and they were apparently satiefied that it was safe. He could not say whether it was customary to go five feet' before bricking. He had only been at the sinking of four wells previously, and one only of these was sandy. At that one they went five . feet before bricking. Between the wooden rings the nata, 'A varied, but he did not consider five feet to be tix, mach fro,- the next higher ring. Only one side of the brickwork are v first, and it seemed to bulge "g tip wook then followed. out and fall forward. The . h pto of the There were four bars of iron Phi'°n "r*r"siIj be four or well to hold the brickwork up, Then i wcn.b- it,„• five inches of a claw at the bottom to graspi leke —By a Juror : There Wag no protection at the the five or six feet they had excavated. William Critchley, joiner, of Adlitigton, said he was down the well about eleven o'clock on Thursday morn- ing, along with William Litherland. Witness went down to cut some lags off a drum so that they could , get the latter out of the way. There had not been any fresh sinking then done. The brickwork at the bottom did not appear to be good,and deceased wanted to get the drum away fzoin it so that he could get another ring in. As soon as the drum was got out, Litherland began to sink deeper. Witness was only down about ten minutes. He saw the well fall in, and the brickwork seemed to take a shoot right from the bottom.—By the Foreman: The drum was put" in by the previous sinkers for the purposes of sinking. The idea was to sink, and take the drum down along with them as they proceeded. Toe bad retch of briCkw)rk was about a yard and a half deep, and was becoming bulgy. Snaylani of Bretherten, put in the drum. The brickwork at die bottom was not good, to his idea. • There were four rings in, but Littler- land was just going to put another ring in whew the sides fell in.</p>
        <p>Richard Markland, well-sinker, of 11, Oxford-street Chorley, said that he was fetched the previous Friday morning to assist in the extrication of the deceated.. There were about seven yards of solii brick work in the the Foreman : In that kind of ground and the distance they had gone, he should consider four feet a reasonable distance to go between ring and ring. For a width in the well of 41t. 61n., he should consider the brickwork ought to be nine inchee in thickness. Lither- land had still another eight • yards to go. The bricks at the bottom were standing. The man when they found him, was stood up in a stooping posi- tion. The brickwons hi this case was four-and-a-half inches, in thickness.</p>
        <p>Mr. Litlierland was re-called, and in answer to a question said her husbend made no complaint about the well being unsafe after be had visited it on the previous Monday.</p>
        <p>A veidiet of " Aceiderhal death "was returned by the jury.</p>
