        <p>NARROW SSOAPR OF DIRER Foocusse.—On Saturday afternoon three brothers named Iferriers had a most ex- citing experience on the dam, which ahricsit culminated in a most distressing tragedy. The three brothers ars boys. and range in age from. ailbwnvranis. They went on a fishing expedition to the drum.. Sport, however, proving slew the lads began to meddle with what they believed to be a wasps' nest in a hole- under the banks of the darn. In order to push a stick:dew-a the hole, pre- sumably to disturb the waspsono of the boys learted rather too far forward over 01ov-water's edge, and losing his balanco.fell into the dam.. CC title point the water is rather deem. Here the lad began. to- plunge about in a most frantic fashion, and rapidly he was in water con- siderably beyond his depth. One.of the other brothers, observingjhat the lad was Loh dnownime,, with admir- able heroZon jumped int) the lake in order to effect his drowning: brother's rescue. :fortunately, the water was too deep for the would-be suceoerer. and he 6.1,40 was soot, in need of help. Th* sad; plight, of the two drowning brothers provoked the.thitd tad to attempt to save them, and he blindly meshed on to a similar fate. His efforts were utterly unaveriting,,fOrthe water Was too deep, anti immediati ly he wasesteuggliag to save his own life. Vliat, the ultimate fste-oU the I have Isn't hers iniglit have been had not assistancetheer nigh it is not difficult to surmise.. Fortunately, a .y.nung ahem named Lomax, residieg at Dunham Masseywahodiapperied td be pass- ing alon the footpath whiAlevrinda alioag the top of the [steep !maks of the darn heard threvies of the drowning trio foe help. For a few zwinenta he was unable to dis- coventheir whereabouts,pwing to- the dense foliage or-. the &amp;Ate of the lake. Ott, ascortakeling the part of the. darn .in which they werwakruggling he at once proceeded.; . .</p>
        <p>to a*rescue, and soon tweased to land the dripPbxl?l boYat. who, fortunately,have not saatiered any ill effec* fro:* tbeir immersiora. Nob so fortunate was thoias resouer, however, for haJoas since contracted ratheze. sp.:does illness througlii.hla effortis; in fact,- he fainted solo after he had rescued the lads, The Royal Hurreee Saalety have bean tua4;otiogtia'utlierl with-the facts °Ube reacme, and it is liopd4liatt htetrts, which have t,iette been fraught with 30i. seristie results, will be day retwnized.</p>
